window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jss-mock-2"] = {"id":"jss-mock-2","title":"JSS Mock 2","fullTitle":"JSS Mock 2 (90 hard, high-yield real PYQs, JSS syllabus)","sub":"90 questions · JSS's 9 subjects × 10 · hard + high yield · untimed","sectionLabels":{"general":"General Psychology / Theories","assessment":"Psychological Assessment","social":"Social Psychology","personality":"Personality","physio":"Physiological Psychology","dev":"Developmental Psychology","clinical":"Clinical Psychology","disorders":"Psychiatric Abnormalities / Disorders","research":"Research Methodology"},"questions":[{"q":"According to Albert Bandura, which of the following is NOT a necessary component process for observational learning to occur?","options":{"a":"Attention","b":"Motivation","c":"Reinforcement","d":"Reproduction"},"correct":"c","section":"general","explanation":"The answer is reinforcement, because in Albert Bandura's theory of observational learning the four necessary component processes are attention, retention, reproduction, and motivation, and direct reinforcement is not among the requirements for learning to occur. Understand Bandura's contribution and why this distinction matters. Bandura, famous for the Bobo doll experiments of the early 1960s, showed that children could acquire novel aggressive behaviors simply by watching an adult model, without ever being reinforced themselves, which challenged the strict behaviorist claim that learning requires direct reinforcement of the learner. His social learning theory, later social cognitive theory, specified four mediational processes that must operate for observational learning to succeed. Attention: the observer must notice and attend to the model's behavior, influenced by the model's salience, status, and attractiveness. Retention: the observer must encode and store a memory representation of the behavior, often symbolically. Reproduction, also called motor reproduction: the observer must have the physical and skill capacity to reproduce the modeled action. Motivation: the observer must have a reason to enact the behavior, and here is where reinforcement enters, but in a specific way. Bandura introduced vicarious reinforcement, meaning we are more likely to imitate a model we have seen rewarded and less likely to imitate one we have seen punished; reinforcement thus influences performance and motivation, not the acquisition of the learning itself. Because the behavior can be learned without any reinforcement, reinforcement is not one of the four required processes, making it the correct exception.","topic":"Bandura's observational learning processes"},{"q":"Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is based on which technique of psychological testing?","options":{"a":"Expressive technique","b":"Construction technique","c":"Association technique","d":"Completion technique"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is construction technique. Projective tests are commonly grouped by the type of response task they require, a classification associated with Lawrence Frank, who coined the term projective and with Bruno Klopfer and others. The main categories are association techniques (respond with the first associations to a stimulus, as in the Rorschach Inkblot Test of Hermann Rorschach and word-association tests), construction techniques (build or create a product such as a story from stimulus material), completion techniques (finish incomplete stimuli, as in sentence-completion tests), choice or ordering techniques (arrange or select items), and expressive techniques (express the self through free activity such as drawing, painting, or play, as in the Draw-a-Person test (Florence Goodenough, 1926) and play therapy). The Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard, presents ambiguous pictures and asks the examinee to construct a full story about each: what is happening, what led up to it, what the characters are thinking and feeling, and how it will end. Because the subject must build a complete narrative from the ambiguous scene, the TAT is a construction technique, making option b correct. It is interpreted in terms of Murray's needs and presses and the projected themes revealing the examinee's motives and conflicts.","topic":"Lindzey's projective techniques: construction (TAT)"},{"q":"Tim would like to participate in his high school's musical. He has been taking vocal lessons for years and is an excellent singer, but does not have much experience as a dancer. The director of the play decides to give him a part where he has to sing and dance. Based on the theory of social facilitation, how will the presence of an audience affect Tim's performance in the play?","options":{"a":"His dancing will improve, but his singing will be impaired.","b":"Both his singing and dancing will improve.","c":"His singing will improve, but his dancing will be impaired.","d":"Both his singing and dancing will be impaired."},"correct":"c","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is that his singing will improve, but his dancing will be impaired. Social facilitation refers to the effect of the presence of others on task performance, first noted by Norman Triplett and later refined by Robert Zajonc. Zajonc resolved seemingly contradictory findings with a key principle: the presence of others increases physiological arousal, and heightened arousal strengthens the dominant response, the response most likely to occur for a given task. For tasks that are simple, well-learned, or well-practised, the dominant response is the correct one, so an audience enhances performance. For tasks that are complex, novel, or poorly learned, the dominant response is often incorrect, so an audience impairs performance. Applying this to Tim: singing is a skill he has practised for years, so it is well-learned and the dominant response is correct, meaning the audience's arousal will enhance his singing. Dancing, however, is a skill in which he has little experience, so it is poorly learned and the dominant response is likely to be an error, meaning the audience will impair his dancing. This is why performing arts differ for experts (helped by crowds) versus novices (hurt by them). The distractors reverse or overgeneralise the effect. This item tests the dominant-response logic of social facilitation.","topic":"Social facilitation vs inhibition"},{"q":"A patient who describes a particularly gruesome thought or fantasy but denies any feelings of anxiety or disgust associated with it is using which of the following Ego's defense:","options":{"a":"Denial","b":"Rationalization","c":"Isolation","d":"Displacement"},"correct":"c","section":"personality","explanation":"A patient who describes a gruesome thought while denying any associated feeling is using the defence of isolation, so the answer is c. Isolation (more fully, isolation of affect) is an ego defence mechanism in which the emotional charge attached to a thought, memory, or impulse is split off and removed from awareness, so that the idea remains conscious but is stripped of the feeling that should accompany it. The person can talk about something disturbing, a violent fantasy, a traumatic event, in a flat, detached, intellectualised way, as if it carried no emotional significance. This protects the person from the anxiety that the affect would arouse while still allowing the cognitive content into consciousness. Isolation is particularly characteristic of obsessive compulsive personality and obsessional neurosis, where thinking is over invested and feeling is kept at a distance, and it is closely related to intellectualisation. The exam discriminator is the precise pattern described: the content (the gruesome thought) is fully present and reported, but the affect (anxiety, disgust) that belongs with it is absent. That specific separation of idea from feeling is the signature of isolation, as opposed to denial (where the content itself is rejected), rationalisation (where false reasons justify it), or displacement (where the feeling is redirected to another object).","topic":"Defence mechanisms: isolation of affect"},{"q":"A patient presents with an inability to perform coordinated, purposeful movements, despite having intact motor function, sensation, and comprehension of the task. When asked to brush their hair, they cannot sequence the movements. This deficit is best described as:","options":{"a":"Agnosia","b":"Ataxia","c":"Apraxia","d":"Alexia"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is apraxia, because the inability to carry out coordinated, purposeful, learned movements despite intact motor power, sensation, coordination, and comprehension is the defining feature of apraxia. Understand the concept precisely, because it is defined by exclusion of simpler explanations. The word comes from Greek roots meaning without action. Apraxia is a disorder of the higher-order planning and programming of skilled movement, so the patient knows what they want to do and has the physical capacity to move, yet cannot organize the movements into the correct sequence to accomplish the intended act. In the vignette, the person cannot sequence the movements to brush their hair even though their arm is strong, their sensation is normal, and they understand the request; this dissociation between preserved elementary functions and lost skilled action is the hallmark. Neurologically, apraxia typically results from damage to the association areas involved in motor planning, especially the left parietal lobe and its connections to premotor and frontal regions, which store the learned motor programs (sometimes called praxicons). Clinicians distinguish subtypes such as ideomotor apraxia (impaired ability to perform a gesture on command despite knowing the concept) and ideational apraxia (impaired ability to sequence a multistep action). Contrast apraxia with its neighbors that examiners bundle together: agnosia is impaired recognition, ataxia is impaired coordination from cerebellar damage, and alexia is impaired reading. Because the deficit here is specifically the inability to execute purposeful movement sequences with everything else intact, the answer is apraxia.","topic":"Apraxia vs agnosia vs ataxia"},{"q":"A 5-year-old child is asked to sort a deck of cards. The cards can be sorted by color (red/black) or by suit (hearts, spades, etc.). The child successfully sorts by color but cannot then re-sort the same deck by suit. This inflexibility is an example of:","options":{"a":"Egocentrism","b":"Animism","c":"Centration","d":"Transductive reasoning"},"correct":"c","section":"dev","explanation":"The answer is centration, because the child's inability to abandon color and re-sort the same cards by suit is a textbook demonstration of centration, the tendency to focus on a single salient dimension of a situation while ignoring all others. Understand this within Jean Piaget's account of preoperational thinking, roughly ages two to seven. Centration is one of the central limitations of this stage, and it explains many of the child's characteristic errors. Because the preoperational child can attend to only one feature at a time, they cannot coordinate multiple dimensions simultaneously, which is why they fail conservation tasks (fixating only on the height of liquid, ignoring width) and why, in this card task, having centered on the color dimension, the child cannot mentally step back and reconceive the same cards along a different dimension, suit. This rigidity, the inability to flexibly shift the basis of classification, is precisely what centration produces. Cognitive scientists would today describe the underlying skill the child lacks as set-shifting or cognitive flexibility, an executive function, and tasks like the Dimensional Change Card Sort, developed by Philip Zelazo, formalize exactly this scenario to study its development. Overcoming centration, learning to decenter and consider several dimensions at once, is a key achievement of the concrete operational stage. The distractors all name other genuine preoperational features, egocentrism, animism, and transductive reasoning, but the specific error of fixating on one dimension and being unable to switch is centration, making it the correct answer.","topic":"Piaget preoperational: centration"},{"q":"A patient sees a friend in the street who fails to acknowledge him; he thinks his friend does not like him anymore. He has the following type of cognitive error:","options":{"a":"Selective abstraction","b":"Arbitrary inference","c":"Personalization","d":"Magnification"},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"Concluding that a friend dislikes him because the friend failed to greet him is arbitrary inference, so the answer is b. Arbitrary inference is one of the cognitive distortions identified by Aaron Beck in his cognitive model of depression and emotional disorders. It means drawing a specific negative conclusion in the absence of supporting evidence, or even in the face of contrary evidence; the person jumps to a conclusion that is arbitrary because nothing actually justifies it. In the example, the friend's failure to acknowledge him could have countless innocent explanations, the friend was distracted, did not see him, was preoccupied, yet the patient leaps to the unfounded negative conclusion that the friend no longer likes him. There is no evidence for that interpretation; it is simply assumed. This is the essence of arbitrary inference. It is worth distinguishing it from the neighbouring distortions: selective abstraction focuses on one detail out of context while ignoring the whole, magnification exaggerates the importance of an event, and personalisation attributes external events to oneself without basis. While the example does involve relating the event to the self, the most precise label for jumping to a negative conclusion with no supporting evidence is arbitrary inference. The exam discriminator is the absence of evidence for the conclusion, which is the defining feature of arbitrary inference in Beck's taxonomy.","topic":"Beck's cognitive errors"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy begins to have new episodes of temper outbursts that are out of proportion to the situation. Which of the following is NOT a diagnostic possibility in this patient?","options":{"a":"Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder","b":"Bipolar Disorder","c":"Conduct Disorder","d":"Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder"},"correct":"a","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD). DMDD was introduced in the DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association to capture children with chronic, severe, persistent irritability and frequent temper outbursts, partly to reduce over-diagnosis of pediatric bipolar disorder. A critical diagnostic rule is the age criterion: DMDD may be diagnosed only when the onset of symptoms is before age 10, and it should not be diagnosed for the first time before age 6 or after age 18. In this vignette the outbursts are described as new in a 12-year-old, meaning onset is after the age-10 cutoff, so DMDD is specifically excluded. That is why it is the one diagnosis that is NOT a possibility, whereas Bipolar Disorder, Conduct Disorder, and ADHD all remain on the differential for a 12-year-old presenting with disproportionate temper outbursts. The item tests careful attention to DMDD's onset-age requirement, a detail that reliably distinguishes it from the other externalizing and mood conditions with which it shares surface features.","topic":"DMDD and childhood irritability differentials"},{"q":"A researcher is interested in the impact of anxiety on performance. To manipulate anxiety, some subjects were given plain chips to eat while others were given barbecue flavoured chips to eat. The most obvious problem with this experiment is a lack of :","options":{"a":"Statistical Validity","b":"Empirical Validity","c":"Internal Validity","d":"Construct Validity"},"correct":"d","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is construct validity. Construct validity is the degree to which an operational manipulation or measure actually captures the theoretical construct it is meant to represent, a concept formalized by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl. In this study the construct of interest is anxiety, but the independent variable, giving subjects plain versus barbecue-flavoured chips, has no plausible connection to inducing anxiety at all. Flavour of a snack is not a valid operationalization of an anxiety manipulation; whatever effect appears cannot be interpreted as an effect of anxiety because anxiety was never meaningfully varied. This is a failure of construct validity: the manipulation does not embody the construct named in the hypothesis. It is worth distinguishing this from internal validity, the confidence that the manipulation rather than a confound caused the outcome, and from external validity, the generalizability of results. Here the flaw is more fundamental than a confound: the manipulation is simply not a measure of anxiety. Because the operationalization of the independent variable does not correspond to the intended psychological construct, the study most obviously lacks construct validity.","topic":"Construct validity of a manipulation"},{"q":"Which of the following secondary tasks is thought to affect the central executive in terms of working memory?","options":{"a":"Continually repeating a word","b":"Remembering a spatial configuration","c":"Listening to irrelevant speech","d":"Random number generation"},"correct":"d","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is random number generation. The working memory model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposes a central executive that controls attention and coordinates two subsidiary slave systems: the phonological loop, which holds and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, and the visuospatial sketchpad, which handles visual and spatial material (a later addition was the episodic buffer). To identify which task loads which component, researchers use interfering secondary tasks. Articulatory suppression, continually repeating a word, occupies the phonological loop; the irrelevant speech effect, listening to unattended speech, also disrupts the phonological loop; remembering a spatial pattern taxes the visuospatial sketchpad. Random number (or random letter) generation, by contrast, requires continuous attentional control: one must suppress habitual counting sequences, monitor recent outputs, and flexibly switch to avoid patterns, which are exactly the supervisory attention functions of the central executive. Baddeley used random generation precisely as a way to load and study the central executive, showing that it becomes less random when a person must simultaneously perform another demanding executive task. So among these options, random number generation is the task that specifically engages and impairs the central executive rather than a slave subsystem.","topic":"Working memory: central executive (Baddeley)"},{"q":"Which of the following is an association technique of psychological testing?","options":{"a":"Sentence completion test","b":"Rorschach inkblot test","c":"Thematic apperception test","d":"Draw-a-person test"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is the Rorschach inkblot test. Projective techniques are commonly classified by the response task they demand, a scheme associated with Lawrence Frank. Association techniques require the respondent to react to a stimulus with the first thing that comes to mind, such as an association, image, or perception. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by Hermann Rorschach, is the prototypical association technique: the examinee is shown ambiguous inkblots and asked what each might be, giving their spontaneous associations to the blot. Word-association tests are another example. The other options each represent a different category. The sentence completion test is a completion technique, in which the person finishes incomplete sentence stems (for example, My greatest fear is...). The Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan is a construction technique, requiring the examinee to construct a full narrative story about ambiguous pictures. The Draw-A-Person test of Karen Machover is an expressive technique, in which the individual expresses themselves through the free activity of drawing. Because only the Rorschach asks for immediate associations to a stimulus, it is the association technique among these, making option b correct.","topic":"Lindzey's projective techniques: association (Rorschach)"},{"q":"In which of the following scenarios would it be most difficult to predict behaviour from attitudes?","options":{"a":"When the attitude has a great deal of personal relevance for the individual","b":"When the attitude is formed by indirect experience","c":"When the individual has a lot of knowledge about the attitude object","d":"When the attitude is formed by the individual's direct experience"},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is when the attitude is formed by indirect experience. Research on attitude-behaviour consistency, notably by Russell Fazio and by Icek Ajzen and Martin Fishbein, shows that attitudes predict behaviour best when they are strong, accessible, and personally significant. Several moderators strengthen the attitude-behaviour link: high personal relevance or vested interest, extensive knowledge about the attitude object, and especially attitudes formed through direct personal experience rather than hearsay. Regina Fazio Regan and Russell Fazio demonstrated that direct experience makes attitudes more accessible in memory and thus more predictive of action. Conversely, attitudes formed indirectly, through secondhand information, media, or others' reports rather than firsthand contact, tend to be weaker, less certain, and less accessible, so they are the poorest predictors of actual behaviour. Therefore the scenario in which prediction is most difficult is the indirect-experience one. The three distractors all describe conditions that increase attitude strength and accessibility, which is why they make behaviour easier to predict, exactly the opposite of what the question asks. This item tests knowledge of the moderators of attitude-behaviour consistency and the special role of direct experience.","topic":"Attitude-behaviour consistency"},{"q":"Self-report ratings of one's characteristics, attributes, and interests is placed under :","options":{"a":"L-data","b":"Z-data","c":"P-data","d":"Q-data"},"correct":"d","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is Q-data. Raymond Cattell, the personality theorist who used factor analysis to derive his sixteen source traits (the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire), proposed that personality can be studied through three main sources of data, which he labelled to ensure that trait findings converge across methods. L-data (life-record data) consists of information gathered from a person's actual life history and everyday behaviour, including ratings made by observers who know the person and objective records such as school grades or work performance. Q-data (questionnaire data) consists of information obtained from the person's own self-report on questionnaires and rating scales, that is, the individual's own ratings of their characteristics, attributes, interests, attitudes, and feelings. T-data (objective test data) consists of measurements taken from objective, often disguised, laboratory tests and behavioural situations in which the person does not know what dimension is being assessed, reducing the possibility of faking. Because self-report ratings, in which a person rates their own traits and interests, fall squarely into the questionnaire category, they are classified as Q-data, making option d correct. The distractors Z-data and P-data are not part of Cattell's standard L-Q-T classification.","topic":"Cattell's L-, Q- and T-data"},{"q":"A scientist is studying brain function in rats and destroys the animal's ventromedial hypothalamus. Which of the following is most likely to occur?","options":{"a":"The rat will drink excessive amounts of water.","b":"The rat will eat excessive amounts of food.","c":"The rat will refuse to drink until it is forced.","d":"The rat will refuse to eat until it is forced."},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is that the rat will eat excessive amounts of food. The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) functions as a satiety centre: it signals when the animal has eaten enough and should stop. When it is destroyed, that 'stop eating' signal is lost, producing hyperphagia and a syndrome of gross obesity, a finding demonstrated in classic lesion studies associated with researchers such as Albert Fisher and later dual-centre models. This contrasts with the lateral hypothalamus (LH), which acts as a hunger or feeding-initiation centre; lesioning the LH produces aphagia (refusal to eat) and can be fatal without force-feeding. This dual-centre hypothalamic model of hunger, one centre driving eating and one inhibiting it, is a foundational piece of biopsychology and physiological psychology. The item tests whether the student can pair the correct nucleus with the correct behavioural outcome. The key memory hook is: Ventromedial equals Very full (satiety, so lesion causes overeating), while Lateral equals Lack of eating when lesioned. Thirst and drinking are governed by separate mechanisms, so the water-related distractors are designed to mislead students who confuse feeding and drinking regulation.","topic":"Hypothalamus: ventromedial vs lateral (hunger and satiety)"},{"q":"Formal operational reasoning involves all the following EXCEPT:","options":{"a":"hypothetical reasoning","b":"counterfactual reasoning","c":"dialectical reasoning","d":"metacognitive reasoning"},"correct":"c","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer (the EXCEPT) is dialectical reasoning. In Jean Piaget's theory, the formal operational stage begins around age eleven or twelve and represents the capacity for abstract, systematic, logical thought. Its hallmarks include hypothetical-deductive reasoning (generating and systematically testing hypotheses, as in the pendulum problem), counterfactual reasoning (reasoning about possibilities and states contrary to present fact), abstract and propositional thinking, and increased metacognition (the ability to reflect on and reason about one's own thought processes). Dialectical reasoning, however, is generally considered a form of postformal thought, said to develop, if at all, in adulthood beyond Piaget's final stage. Dialectical thinking involves recognizing that knowledge is relative and context-bound, tolerating contradiction, and integrating opposing ideas (thesis and antithesis) into a synthesis, an idea rooted in the philosophy of Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel and elaborated in adult-development theories by researchers such as Klaus Riegel and Michael Basseches. Because dialectical reasoning belongs to postformal rather than formal operational cognition, it is the element that does not fit Piaget's formal operational stage, making it the correct exception among the four options.","topic":"Piaget formal operations"},{"q":"Gregory Bateson describes a classic example of the interaction between a mother and her son who had fairly well recovered from an acute schizophrenic episode. Bateson described this interaction as follows: '[The son] was glad to see her [his mother] and impulsively put his arm around her shoulders, whereupon she stiffened. He withdrew his arm and she asked, Don't you love me anymore? He then blushed, and she said, Dear, you must not be so easily embarrassed and afraid of your feelings. The patient was able to stay with her only a few minutes more, and following her departure, he assaulted an aide and was put in the isolation room.' Bateson used this interaction to illustrate which ONE of the following concepts?","options":{"a":"The cybernetic system","b":"The homeostatic system","c":"The incongruent family dyad","d":"The symptomatic double bind"},"correct":"d","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct answer is the symptomatic double bind. Gregory Bateson, together with colleagues Don Jackson, Jay Haley, and John Weakland, proposed the double bind theory in 1956 as an early communicational and systemic account of schizophrenia. A double bind is a situation in which a person repeatedly receives contradictory messages on different levels of communication, cannot escape or step outside the situation (cannot leave the field), and cannot comment on or resolve the contradiction, leaving them in an impossible, no-win position. In the classic example quoted, the mother sends contradictory messages: she stiffens and withdraws when her son shows affection (a nonverbal 'do not be close to me'), yet then verbally reproaches him with 'Don't you love me anymore?' and criticises him for being embarrassed. Whatever the son does, express affection or withdraw, he is in the wrong, and he cannot comment on the paradox or escape the relationship. Bateson argued that chronic exposure to such double binds could contribute to the disordered communication and thinking seen in schizophrenia. Though the theory is no longer accepted as a cause of schizophrenia, it was historically important in the development of family systems therapy. The distractors reference other systemic concepts (cybernetics, homeostasis) or a non-standard term. This item tests recognition of Bateson's double bind.","topic":"Bateson's double bind"},{"q":"In which of the following situations would a diagnosis of \"global developmental delay\" be inappropriate?","options":{"a":"The patient is a child who is too young to fully manifest specific symptoms or to complete requisite assessments.","b":"The patient, a 7-year-old boy, has a full-scale IQ of 65 and severe impairment in adaptive functioning.","c":"The patient's scores on psychometric tests suggest intellectual disability, but there is insufficient information about the patient's adaptive functional skills.","d":"The patient's impaired adaptive functioning suggests intellectual developmental disorder, but there is insufficient information about the level of cognitive impairment measured by standardized instruments."},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is the 7-year-old boy with a full-scale IQ of 65 and severe adaptive impairment. In the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, Global Developmental Delay is a provisional diagnosis reserved for children under age 5 who fail to meet expected developmental milestones in several areas but who cannot yet be reliably assessed by standardized testing, either because they are too young or too impaired to complete valid measures. It is explicitly a placeholder that requires reassessment later. The diagnosis is inappropriate for the 7-year-old because he is old enough for, and has already undergone, valid standardized testing that yields a clear IQ of 65 together with documented severe adaptive impairment. That combination meets the full criteria for Intellectual Disability (Intellectual Developmental Disorder), so the definitive diagnosis should be made rather than the provisional Global Developmental Delay label. The other three scenarios each involve missing or unobtainable assessment data, precisely the circumstances the provisional category was designed for. The item tests understanding that Global Developmental Delay is an under-5, cannot-yet-assess placeholder, not a substitute when full diagnostic data already exist.","topic":"Global developmental delay vs intellectual disability"},{"q":"A researcher wants to study the prevalence of test anxiety among all school students in India. Due to feasibility, they first randomly select 5 districts from each state, then randomly select 10 schools from each district, and finally randomly select 30 students from each school. This sampling technique is known as:","options":{"a":"Stratified Random Sampling","b":"Quota Sampling","c":"Cluster Sampling","d":"Purposive Sampling"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is cluster sampling, specifically its multistage form, because the researcher is randomly selecting intact groups (clusters) in successive nested stages rather than sampling individuals directly from a national list. Understand the core logic from scratch: when a population is enormous and geographically scattered, like all school students in India, there is no practical sampling frame listing every student, and visiting randomly scattered individuals across the country would be ruinously expensive. Cluster sampling solves this by exploiting the fact that people are naturally grouped: students sit inside schools, schools sit inside districts, districts sit inside states. The researcher randomly selects clusters at each level, first districts, then schools within the chosen districts, then students within the chosen schools, which is why this is called multistage cluster sampling. Every stage uses random selection, so it remains a probability method and sampling error can still be estimated, though it is typically larger than for simple random sampling because students in the same school resemble each other. The crucial contrast with stratified sampling, which examiners exploit relentlessly, is this: in stratified sampling you sample from every subgroup to guarantee representation, whereas in cluster sampling you randomly choose only some clusters and ignore the rest, purely for feasibility and cost. Nested random stages of naturally occurring units always signal cluster sampling.","topic":"Cluster vs stratified sampling"},{"q":"Sheena is studying for her final exam in her psychology class. For every 45 minutes that she studies, she gives herself a mini chocolate, then goes back to studying. What Schedule of reinforcement is Sheena using?","options":{"a":"Variable ratio schedule","b":"Variable interval schedule","c":"Fixed interval schedule","d":"Fixed ratio schedule"},"correct":"c","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is a fixed interval schedule. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) described four partial reinforcement schedules built from two dimensions: ratio versus interval (whether reinforcement depends on number of responses or passage of time) and fixed versus variable (whether the requirement is constant or changes). Sheena rewards herself after a constant, predictable amount of time (every 45 minutes) regardless of how much work she does in that window, which is the defining feature of a fixed interval schedule. The classic signature of fixed interval reinforcement is the scalloped response curve: behaviour slows just after reinforcement and accelerates as the next reward time approaches, because the learner can anticipate when the reward is due. Ratio schedules (fixed or variable) instead reward a count of responses and produce higher, steadier response rates. Variable schedules resist extinction best because the unpredictability keeps responding high. Recognising these four schedules is core operant-conditioning content, and the trick in this item is to notice the cue word 'minutes,' which signals an interval schedule, combined with the constant 45-minute gap, which signals fixed rather than variable.","topic":"Schedules of reinforcement"},{"q":"Odd-even method of splitting the test to estimate split-half reliability is MOST appropriate for :","options":{"a":"Projective tests","b":"Neuropsychological tests","c":"Power tests","d":"Speed tests"},"correct":"c","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is power tests. Split-half reliability estimates internal consistency by dividing a test into two halves, scoring each, and correlating the two half-scores (then usually correcting with the Spearman-Brown formula, named after Charles Spearman and William Brown). The odd-even method, assigning odd-numbered items to one half and even-numbered items to the other, is a common way to create equivalent halves. This procedure is appropriate for power tests, in which items vary in difficulty, there is generous or no time limit, and the score reflects how many problems a person can solve rather than how fast. In a power test, examinees attempt all or most items, so both halves contain comparable numbers of attempted items and the correlation is meaningful. Split-half reliability is specifically inappropriate for speed tests, in which items are easy and the score depends on how many items a person completes within a strict time limit. In a pure speed test, a person tends to get all attempted items right and simply stops where time runs out, so odd and even halves would each contain the same number of correct items for everyone, artificially inflating the correlation to near unity. Because the odd-even split-half method validly estimates reliability only when speed is not the limiting factor, it is most appropriate for power tests, making option c correct.","topic":"Split-half reliability: power vs speed tests"},{"q":"According to Cialdini, techniques for gaining compliance rest to a large extent on six basic principles. These do NOT include:","options":{"a":"Scarcity","b":"Social validation","c":"Friendship/liking","d":"Reward"},"correct":"d","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is reward. The social psychologist Robert Cialdini, in his influential work on persuasion and compliance, identified six basic principles that underlie most compliance-gaining techniques: reciprocity (feeling obligated to return favors), commitment and consistency (aligning behavior with prior commitments, exploited by the foot-in-the-door technique (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966)), social validation or social proof (looking to others' behavior to guide our own), liking or friendship (complying more readily with people we like or find attractive), authority (deferring to legitimate or expert authority figures), and scarcity (valuing things more when they are rare or dwindling in availability). Reward, while central to reinforcement theory and operant conditioning in the tradition of B. F. Skinner, is not one of Cialdini's six compliance principles as such. Although reciprocity involves an exchange of benefits, Cialdini's framework is about the psychological triggers of social influence rather than the simple delivery of rewards. Therefore, among the options, the one that is not one of Cialdini's six principles is reward, making option d correct. Recognizing these six principles is useful both for understanding persuasion and for resisting manipulative sales and marketing tactics.","topic":"Cialdini's six principles of influence"},{"q":"A client tells you that they are \"no one\" and feels \"empty inside.\" They report chronic fears of abandonment and have a history of intense, unstable relationships. According to psychoanalytic object relations theory, this pathology of the self is most likely a result of:","options":{"a":"Fixation at the oral stage of psychosexual development.","b":"A failure in parental mirroring and idealizing, leading to a fragmented self.","c":"Unresolved Oedipal complex and castration anxiety.","d":"Harsh superego demands leading to constant guilt."},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The answer is a failure in parental mirroring and idealizing leading to a fragmented self, because the clinical picture of chronic emptiness, a sense of being no one, intense fears of abandonment, and unstable relationships is best explained, within psychoanalytic object relations and self-psychology, as arising from early relational failures that leave the self fragmented rather than cohesive. Understand the theoretical lens. This vignette describes features characteristic of borderline-level personality organization, and the question asks you to explain it through the object relations and self-psychology tradition rather than classical Freudian drive theory (Sigmund Freud). Heinz Kohut's self-psychology holds that a stable, cohesive sense of self develops only when the child's selfobject needs are adequately met by caregivers, chiefly the mirroring need (having one's worth reflected back through an attuned parent's admiring responsiveness) and the idealizing need (being able to look up to and draw calm and strength from an admired, soothing parent). When these needs are chronically unmet, the self fails to consolidate and remains fragmented, prone to feelings of emptiness, inner deadness, and an unstable, poorly defined identity, exactly the person who says they are no one and feel empty inside. Object relations theorists like Otto Kernberg similarly trace borderline pathology to early disturbances in the relationship with caregivers that prevent the integration of good and bad representations of self and others (splitting), producing identity diffusion, terror of abandonment, and stormy, unstable relationships. Both frameworks locate the origin in failed early relational provision, not in Freudian drive fixations. So the emptiness, abandonment fears, and unstable relationships reflect failed mirroring and idealizing and a fragmented self.","topic":"Kohut's self psychology and borderline pathology"},{"q":"In a typical major sleep period, how many cycles of NREM-REM is there:","options":{"a":"2-4","b":"4-6","c":"6-10","d":"10-12"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is 4 to 6. During a typical night of sleep, a person passes through several complete cycles of NREM (non-rapid eye movement) and REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, with each cycle lasting roughly 90 minutes, so across a full night of about seven to eight hours there are approximately four to six such cycles. Sleep architecture works as follows: a cycle begins with NREM sleep, which itself progresses through stages, from light sleep (stages N1 and N2) into deep slow-wave sleep (stage N3, dominated by delta waves and important for physical restoration), and then ascends back toward lighter sleep before entering REM sleep, the stage of vivid dreaming characterised by rapid eye movements, muscle atonia (paralysis of voluntary muscles), and brain activity resembling wakefulness. Importantly, the composition of cycles changes across the night: early cycles contain more deep slow-wave NREM sleep, whereas later cycles contain progressively longer REM periods, which is why most vivid dreaming and the longest REM episode occur toward morning. Knowing that a healthy adult cycles through NREM and REM about four to six times per night, with each cycle around 90 minutes, is a standard fact in the biopsychology of sleep.","topic":"Sleep architecture: NREM-REM cycles"},{"q":"According to Vygotsky :","options":{"a":"Intra-subjectivity precedes inter-subjectivity","b":"Children do not engage in inter-subjectivity","c":"Inter-subjectivity and intra-subjectivity are related","d":"Inter-subjectivity precedes intra-subjectivity"},"correct":"d","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is that inter-subjectivity precedes intra-subjectivity. This reflects the central principle of Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory, often called the general genetic law of cultural development: every higher mental function appears twice, first on the social (interpsychological) plane between people, and only later on the individual (intrapsychological) plane within the child. In other words, cognition originates in shared social interaction and is subsequently internalized. Intersubjectivity refers to the shared understanding, common focus, and mutual coordination that arise between a child and a more knowledgeable partner (a caregiver or teacher) during joint activity, for example when adult and child establish a shared reference point in the zone of proximal development. Through participation in such intersubjective exchanges, mediated by language and other cultural tools, the child gradually internalizes the meanings and thought processes, converting shared social understanding into individual (intrasubjective) mental functioning. Thus the interpersonal comes first and the intrapersonal is derived from it, the opposite of a view that would place the individual mind before the social. This social-to-individual direction of development is the hallmark of Vygotsky's account, making option d correct.","topic":"Vygotsky: intersubjectivity and internalisation"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a key concept in Carl Rogers' Client-Centered Therapy?","options":{"a":"Conditions of Worth","b":"Unconditional Positive Regard","c":"Actualizing Tendency","d":"The Therapeutic Alliance (as defined by Bordin)"},"correct":"d","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is the therapeutic alliance as defined by Edward Bordin, because that construct comes from a different theoretical lineage and is not one of Carl Rogers' client-centered concepts. Understand Rogers' framework so you can see which three options are native to it. Carl Rogers, a founder of humanistic psychology, built client-centered (later person-centered) therapy on an optimistic view of human nature: every person has an actualizing tendency, an innate drive toward growth, health, and the fulfillment of potential. Problems arise, in his view, from conditions of worth, the messages from parents and society that one is only acceptable if one meets certain standards, which cause people to distort or deny their genuine experience and create incongruence between the real self and the self-concept. Therapy heals this through the therapist providing three core conditions, the necessary and sufficient conditions for change: unconditional positive regard (nonjudgmental acceptance of the client as a person of worth), empathy (accurately sensing the client's inner world), and congruence or genuineness (being real and transparent). So conditions of worth, unconditional positive regard, and the actualizing tendency are all Rogerian. The odd one out is the therapeutic alliance as formulated by Edward Bordin, whose influential 1979 model defined the working alliance in terms of three components, agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and the emotional bond, as a transtheoretical or integrative concept applicable across therapies, growing out of psychodynamic ideas about the working relationship rather than Rogers' theory. Hence Bordin's therapeutic alliance is the correct exception.","topic":"Rogers' client-centred therapy concepts"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a Schneider’s first-rank symptom?","options":{"a":"Auditory hallucination","b":"Delusional perception","c":"Passivity phenomenon","d":"Delusion of self-reference"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is delusion of self-reference. Kurt Schneider proposed a list of first-rank symptoms (FRS) which he considered, in the absence of organic disease, strongly suggestive of schizophrenia and useful for diagnosis. The classic Schneiderian first-rank symptoms are: auditory hallucinations of specific types (third-person voices discussing the patient, voices commenting on the patient's actions, or thought echo/audible thoughts), thought alienation phenomena (thought insertion, thought withdrawal, thought broadcasting), passivity phenomena or made experiences (made feelings, made impulses, made acts, and somatic passivity, where the patient feels their will, emotions, or body are controlled by an external force), and delusional perception (attaching an abnormal delusional significance to a normally perceived object). A delusion of self-reference (or simple ideas/delusions of reference), where the patient believes ordinary events, remarks, or media broadcasts are specifically about them, is a common symptom in schizophrenia but is NOT on Schneider's first-rank list, which is why it is the exception here. The first-rank symptoms are not pathognomonic (they occur in other psychoses too), but historically they shaped diagnostic criteria and remain a frequent exam topic for distinguishing core Schneiderian phenomena from other schizophrenic features.","topic":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms"},{"q":"Which of the following is a pooled variance estimate that constitutes the denominator of an F-ratio?","options":{"a":"The between-cells mean square (MSB).","b":"The mean of the sampling distribution.","c":"The sum of the deviations from the grand mean.","d":"The within-cells mean square (MSw)."},"correct":"d","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is the within-cells mean square (MSw). Analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher, tests whether the means of several groups differ by partitioning the total variability in the data into components and forming a ratio of variances. The F-ratio is calculated as the between-groups mean square divided by the within-groups mean square. The numerator, the between-cells or between-groups mean square (MSB), captures variance due to the treatment effect plus error, reflecting how much the group means differ from the grand mean. The denominator, the within-cells or within-groups mean square (MSw, also called the error mean square), is a pooled estimate of the variance within each group, that is, the average variability of scores around their own group means, and it represents random error or noise unaffected by the treatment. Because MSw pools the variance estimates from all the separate cells or groups, it is the pooled variance estimate that serves as the F-ratio denominator. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance and F is near 1.00; if the treatment has an effect, the numerator grows and F increases. The distractors are either the numerator, a non-variance quantity, or a sum-of-squares term. This item tests understanding of the structure of the F-ratio in ANOVA.","topic":"ANOVA: F-ratio and mean square within"},{"q":"Which theory of emotion explains the phenomenon of misattribution of arousal (e.g. feeling romantic attraction when actually scared)?","options":{"a":"James-Lange theory","b":"Two-factor theory","c":"Cannon-Bard theory","d":"Facial feedback hypothesis"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The two-factor theory of emotion, developed by Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer in 1962, proposes that emotion arises from two ingredients: physiological arousal plus a cognitive label that the person applies to that arousal based on the situational context. The arousal itself is relatively general and undifferentiated; it is the cognitive interpretation of why one is aroused that determines which specific emotion is felt. This is precisely what allows misattribution of arousal: if the true source of arousal is ambiguous, a person may attribute it to the wrong cause. The classic demonstration is the study by Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron on a high, wobbly suspension bridge, in which men experiencing fear-induced arousal misattributed it to attraction toward an interviewer, reporting more romantic interest than men on a stable bridge. The distractors cannot account for this: James-Lange (William James and Carl Lange) ties each emotion to a specific bodily pattern, Cannon-Bard (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard) treats arousal and emotion as simultaneous and independent, and the facial feedback hypothesis concerns expressions. Because the two-factor theory explains misattribution through relabeling of arousal, option b is correct.","topic":"Schachter-Singer two-factor theory of emotion"},{"q":"At what age can the Malin's Intelligence Scale be administered to Indian children?","options":{"a":"4 years","b":"2 years","c":"5 years","d":"6 years"},"correct":"d","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is 6 years. The Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is an Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (David Wechsler), adapted by Arthur J. Malin. Like the Wechsler children's scale on which it is based, it is designed for school-age children and is standardized for administration from about 6 years up to roughly 15 or 16 years of age. It is one of the most widely used individually administered intelligence tests in Indian clinical and educational settings, yielding a verbal IQ, a performance IQ, and a full-scale IQ derived from a set of verbal subtests (such as information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, vocabulary, and digit span) and performance subtests (such as picture completion, block design, object assembly, coding, and mazes). Because it is adapted and standardized on an Indian population, it provides more culturally appropriate norms than directly importing a Western test. This lower age limit of 6 years distinguishes it from preschool-level instruments used for younger children, such as the Indian adaptations of the Binet scales (Alfred Binet) or the Gesell Developmental Schedules (Arnold Gesell). Because the Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children can be administered from age 6, option d is correct.","topic":"Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC)"},{"q":"Raven and his colleagues formulated a model identifying the six bases of power for influencing others. These do not include:","options":{"a":"Reciprocity","b":"Coercion","c":"Expertise","d":"Information"},"correct":"a","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is reciprocity. The classic taxonomy of social power was developed by John French and Bertram Raven, who originally identified five bases of power a person can use to influence others: reward power (the ability to give positive outcomes), coercive power (the ability to punish or impose negative outcomes), legitimate power (authority derived from one's role or position), referent power (influence arising from others' identification with or admiration of the person), and expert power (influence based on possessing superior knowledge or skill). Raven later added a sixth base, informational power, which rests on the persuasive content of a message or the information one provides, rather than on the influencer's position or relationship, and unlike the others it can produce lasting, independent change because the target internalizes the reasoning. Reciprocity, by contrast, is not one of French and Raven's bases of power; it is a norm of social exchange and one of the principles of compliance identified by Robert Cialdini, the felt obligation to return a favour. So while reciprocity is a genuine influence mechanism, it belongs to a different framework and is not among the six bases of power. Because reciprocity is not one of Raven's six bases, option a is correct.","topic":"Raven's bases of social power"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man reports a persistent, pervasive pattern of grandiosity, a need for constant admiration, and a distinct lack of empathy. According to Kohut's self-psychology, this pathology arises from a failure in parental:","options":{"a":"Mirroring","b":"Idealizing","c":"Twinship","d":"Confrontation"},"correct":"a","section":"personality","explanation":"The answer is mirroring, because in Heinz Kohut's self-psychology, the grandiose, admiration-seeking, empathy-deficient presentation of narcissistic pathology arises specifically from a failure in parental mirroring. Learn Kohut's framework from scratch. Breaking from Sigmund Freud's drive theory, Kohut argued that the self develops through relationships with what he called selfobjects, meaning other people who are experienced not as fully separate individuals but as parts of the self that perform vital psychological functions for the child. He described three selfobject needs that must be adequately met for a cohesive, stable self to form. The first is the mirroring need: the child's healthy grandiosity and exhibitionism ('look at me, admire me') must be reflected back by an attuned parent's approving gaze, the gleam in the mother's eye, so the child gradually builds realistic self-esteem. The second is the idealizing need: the child needs to look up to and merge with a calm, powerful, admired parent, which internalizes into self-soothing capacities and guiding ideals. The third is the twinship or alter-ego need: the sense of being essentially like others, which grounds belonging. When mirroring fails, the child's grandiosity is never metabolized into mature self-esteem and instead persists in raw, archaic form, producing exactly the adult picture of grandiosity, insatiable need for admiration, and shallow empathy. So the correct link is grandiosity plus admiration-hunger equals failed mirroring in Kohut's self-psychology.","topic":"Kohut's self psychology: mirroring and narcissism"},{"q":"Cutting through a person’s corpus callosum causes the person to :","options":{"a":"Die","b":"Become blind","c":"Be unable to speak or understand language","d":"Have difficulty coordinating certain kind of activities"},"correct":"d","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is that the person will have difficulty coordinating certain kinds of activities. The corpus callosum is the large bundle of nerve fibres (the major commissure) that connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres, allowing them to communicate and share information. When it is surgically severed, a procedure called a corpus callosotomy historically used to treat severe, intractable epilepsy by preventing seizures from spreading between hemispheres, the result is a split-brain patient. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga's famous studies of these patients revealed that the two hemispheres can no longer exchange information, producing striking but subtle effects. For example, an object shown only to the left visual field (processed by the right hemisphere) cannot be named aloud, because language resides in the left hemisphere, yet the patient can pick out the object with the left hand. In everyday life, split-brain patients function surprisingly normally, but they show difficulties in tasks requiring the two hemispheres to coordinate, such as certain bimanual movements where each hand must cooperate, and they can show conflicts between the hands. Crucially, cutting the corpus callosum does not kill the person, blind them, or destroy language, so the only accurate consequence among the options is difficulty coordinating certain kinds of activities.","topic":"Split-brain and the corpus callosum"},{"q":"Children, who are distressed by their parent's absence, seek interaction but also reject it when offered. How can the reaction be categorised?","options":{"a":"Insecure-avoidant","b":"Insecure-resistant","c":"Insecure-disorganized","d":"Secure-resistant"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is insecure-resistant (also called anxious-ambivalent or insecure-ambivalent) attachment. Mary Ainsworth developed the Strange Situation procedure to classify infant attachment based on the child's reactions to separation from and reunion with the caregiver. She identified secure attachment and two insecure patterns, later joined by a fourth. Insecure-resistant infants are highly distressed by the caregiver's absence and are difficult to soothe on reunion: they simultaneously seek contact and comfort yet resist or reject it when it is offered, showing an angry, ambivalent, push-pull pattern of clinging and rejecting. This stems from inconsistent, unpredictable caregiving, so the infant cannot rely on the caregiver as a secure base. By contrast, insecure-avoidant infants (from rejecting or unresponsive caregiving) minimise distress and avoid the caregiver, and securely attached infants (from sensitive, responsive caregiving) are readily comforted. The disorganised category, added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon, describes contradictory, fearful behaviour often linked to frightening caregiving. The described approach-then-resist ambivalence maps precisely onto the resistant type. This item tests knowledge of Ainsworth's attachment classifications and their distinguishing reunion behaviours.","topic":"Attachment styles (Ainsworth's Strange Situation)"},{"q":"Which of the following disabilities is explicitly covered under the scope of The National Trust Act (1999) in India for the welfare of persons with the disability?","options":{"a":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","b":"Specific Learning Disability","c":"Intellectual Disability (Mental Retardation)","d":"Both (a) and (c)"},"correct":"d","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is both autism and intellectual disability, because the National Trust Act of 1999 explicitly names four conditions, and the two offered in this item both belong to that list. Learn the statute precisely, because Indian disability law is a favorite exam target and the trap is confusing this Act with the broader disability laws. The full title is the National Trust for the Welfare of Persons with Autism, Cerebral Palsy, Mental Retardation and Multiple Disabilities Act, 1999. That title itself is the answer key: the four covered conditions are autism, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability (the Act uses the older term mental retardation), and multiple disabilities. The Act created a statutory body, the National Trust, to promote independent living, arrange legal guardianship, and support families of persons with these severe developmental conditions. Contrast this with two other laws examiners deliberately blur together. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 recognizes a much wider list of twenty-one disabilities, including specific learning disability, mental illness, and blindness, which is why specific learning disability is a distractor here. The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 governs treatment and rights of persons with mental illness, a different domain again. When a question cites the National Trust Act, restrict yourself to its four named conditions, and remember that two of them, autism and intellectual disability, appear together in this item, so the combined option is correct.","topic":"National Trust Act 1999"},{"q":"Following are negative features of schizophrenia except:","options":{"a":"Avolition","b":"Alogia","c":"Ambivalence","d":"Affective blunting"},"correct":"c","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is ambivalence, which is the exception. The symptoms of schizophrenia are traditionally divided into positive and negative symptoms. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal functions, things added to experience, such as hallucinations, delusions, and disorganised thought and speech. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal functions, things taken away, and they are summarised by several A-words: avolition (lack of motivation and inability to initiate or sustain goal-directed activity), alogia (poverty of speech, reduced verbal output and content), affective blunting or flattening (diminished emotional expression in face, voice, and gesture), anhedonia (inability to experience pleasure), and asociality (social withdrawal). Avolition, alogia, and affective blunting are all classic negative symptoms, so they are not the exception. Ambivalence, the simultaneous holding of contradictory feelings or wishes, is one of Eugen Bleuler's fundamental four As of schizophrenia (along with loosening of associations, affective disturbance, and autism), but in the modern positive-versus-negative symptom classification it is not categorised as a negative symptom in the same way the deficit symptoms are. Because the question asks which is NOT a negative feature, ambivalence is the correct answer to select.","topic":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia"},{"q":"A researcher uses a Scree plot to determine the number of factors to retain in an EFA. The plot shows a sharp drop from eigenvalue 3.5 to 1.8, followed by a gradual plateau where eigenvalues are 0.9, 0.7, and 0.5. According to the Kaiser-Guttman rule and the Scree plot, how many factors should ideally be retained?","options":{"a":"5","b":"3","c":"2","d":"1"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is 2 factors, and you get there by applying the two classic retention rules in exploratory factor analysis and seeing that they agree. First, the Kaiser-Guttman rule, proposed by Henry Kaiser building on Louis Guttman's work, says retain only factors whose eigenvalue exceeds 1.0. The logic is that in a standardized correlation matrix each original variable contributes exactly one unit of variance, so a factor with an eigenvalue below 1 explains less variance than a single raw variable and is not worth keeping. Here the eigenvalues are 3.5, 1.8, 0.9, 0.7 and 0.5; only 3.5 and 1.8 clear the threshold, giving two factors. Second, the scree test, introduced by Raymond Cattell, plots eigenvalues in descending order and looks for the elbow, the point where the steep cliff turns into flat rubble (scree is the loose rock at a cliff's base). You keep the factors on the cliff before the plateau begins. The question describes a sharp drop from 3.5 to 1.8 followed by a gradual plateau starting at 0.9, so the elbow falls after the second factor, again giving two. When both criteria converge, as here, the retention decision is unambiguous. Remember the pairing of names: Kaiser-Guttman for the eigenvalue-greater-than-one rule, Cattell for the scree plot.","topic":"Factor analysis: scree plot, eigenvalues"},{"q":"Which type of intelligence according to Sternberg's Triarchic theory of intelligence includes the ability to easily adopt to the present environment or select a more favourable environment than the existing one or modify the environment to fit one's need?","options":{"a":"Componential","b":"Contextual","c":"Incremental","d":"Experimental"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is: Contextual. Robert Sternberg's CONTEXTUAL (practical) intelligence is the ability to adapt to, shape, or select environments, exactly the adapt/select/modify language in the stem. Componential is analytical intelligence; experiential is the third (creative) component.","topic":"Sternberg's triarchic theory: contextual intelligence"},{"q":"Under which grade of insight does \"intellectual insight\" come under?","options":{"a":"Grade 3","b":"Grade 4","c":"Grade 5","d":"Grade 6"},"correct":"c","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is Grade 5. In psychiatric assessment, insight, the patient's awareness and understanding of their own illness, is often graded on a six-point scale. The commonly taught grading runs as follows: Grade 1 is complete denial of illness; Grade 2 is slight awareness of being sick and needing help but denying it at the same time; Grade 3 is awareness of being sick but blaming it on others, on external factors, or on physical or organic causes; Grade 4 is awareness that the illness is due to something unknown within the patient; Grade 5 is intellectual insight, in which the patient admits they are ill and acknowledges that their symptoms or social maladjustment are due to their own particular irrational feelings or disturbance, yet does not apply this understanding to actually change future behavior; and Grade 6 is true emotional insight, a deeper emotional awareness of one's motives and feelings that does lead to meaningful change in behavior and personality. The crucial distinction is that intellectual insight (Grade 5) is a head-level acknowledgment without behavioral change, whereas emotional insight (Grade 6) is accompanied by genuine change. Because intellectual insight corresponds specifically to Grade 5, option c is correct.","topic":"Grades of insight"},{"q":"A group of scientists is tasked with making a decision about the use of live animals in their university research. There is great deal of community pressure to create a definitive policy and the leading scientist is concerned about the group's ability to make the correct decision since the majority of the group has used live animals in their research. How might the leader reduce groupthink among the scientists as they make their decision?","options":{"a":"The leader could make a cohesive team building exercise a mandatory part of the meeting","b":"The leader could relate his opinion at the beginning the discussion to guide conversation","c":"The leader could ask a subset of individuals in the group to critique animal research","d":"The leader could ask the group members not to argue with one another's opinions"},"correct":"c","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is that the leader could ask a subset of individuals to critique animal research. Groupthink is a phenomenon identified by Irving Janis, in which the desire for harmony, unanimity, and conformity within a cohesive group overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives, leading to poor, irrational decisions. Symptoms include an illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, suppression of dissent, self-censorship, an illusion of unanimity, and the emergence of self-appointed mindguards. Janis analysed fiascos such as the Bay of Pigs invasion. Crucially, Janis also proposed remedies to prevent groupthink, and the best ones involve deliberately encouraging dissent and critical evaluation. Assigning one or more members the role of devil's advocate or critical evaluator, exactly what option C describes by asking a subset to critique the group's leaning toward animal research, forces the group to examine opposing views and counters premature consensus. Other recommended safeguards include the leader remaining impartial and withholding their own opinion at first, inviting outside experts, and breaking into subgroups. Options A, B, and D each do the opposite of Janis's advice: building cohesion, having the leader state a view first, and banning argument all suppress dissent and intensify groupthink. This item tests knowledge of Janis's groupthink concept and its prevention strategies.","topic":"Groupthink and how to prevent it"},{"q":"In paranoia, the ego’s defense mechanism as given by Melaine Klein is seen as:","options":{"a":"Reaction formation","b":"Splitting","c":"Suppression","d":"Projection"},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is splitting. Within Melanie Klein's object-relations theory, splitting is the most primitive defence and the one she linked to the earliest, paranoid-schizoid position of infancy. The infant cannot yet hold the idea that the same caregiver who gratifies (the good breast) is also the one who frustrates (the bad breast). To manage the resulting anxiety, the infant splits the object and the self into wholly good and wholly bad parts, keeping them separate so that the loving feelings are not contaminated or destroyed by the hostile ones. In Kleinian theory the persecutory anxiety of the paranoid-schizoid position, where the bad object is felt to threaten and persecute the self, underlies paranoid functioning, and splitting (together with projective identification) is the characteristic defence. This is why, in paranoia, the mechanism Klein emphasised is splitting rather than the more classically Freudian projection (Sigmund Freud). Splitting also reappears in adult borderline personality organisation, where people oscillate between idealising and devaluing the same person. Understanding splitting is central to object-relations and to grasping how very early mental life is organised around managing aggression and preserving the good object.","topic":"Melanie Klein: splitting and the paranoid-schizoid position"},{"q":"Brain regions in procedural learning is related to","options":{"a":"hindbrain, spinal cord","b":"cerebellum, basal ganglia, motor cortex","c":"frontal lobe, association cortex","d":"hippocampus, cerebral cortex, amygdala"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is: cerebellum, basal ganglia, motor cortex. Procedural (implicit, skill) learning depends on the cerebellum, basal ganglia and motor cortex. The hippocampus, in contrast, supports declarative/explicit memory, which is why option (d) is the trap.","topic":"Procedural memory: cerebellum and basal ganglia"},{"q":"Which among the following is not included within the classification of structure of temperament given by Rothbart and Bates","options":{"a":"Extraversion","b":"Inhibition to the unfamiliar","c":"Negative affectivity","d":"Effortful control"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is inhibition to the unfamiliar. Mary Rothbart and John Bates built their structural model of temperament on exactly three broad dimensions: (1) Surgency / extraversion, meaning positive affect, activity level, approach and sociability; (2) Negative affectivity, meaning fear, frustration, sadness and discomfort; (3) Effortful control, the capacity to voluntarily focus and shift attention and to inhibit a dominant response, which is the self-regulatory core tied to executive attention. Inhibition to the unfamiliar, better known as behavioral inhibition, is a real temperament construct but it belongs to Jerome Kagan, who studied inhibited versus uninhibited children and their wariness in novel situations. It overlaps with fear inside negative affectivity, yet it is not one of the three named Rothbart and Bates dimensions. Exam hook: surgency, negative affectivity and effortful control map to Rothbart and Bates; behavioral inhibition maps to Kagan.","topic":"Rothbart's temperament model"},{"q":"Which kind of empathy is considered inappropriate for an initial counselling interview or session?","options":{"a":"Advanced empathy","b":"Primary empathy","c":"Both a and b","d":"Culturally sensitive empathy"},"correct":"a","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct answer is advanced empathy. In the influential skilled-helper model of counselling developed by Gerard Egan, a distinction is drawn between primary (basic) empathy and advanced (accurate) empathy. Primary empathy is the counsellor's accurate understanding and reflection of the feelings and meanings the client has directly expressed; it communicates that the client has been heard and understood and is essential for building rapport and the therapeutic alliance from the very first session. Advanced empathy goes further: it involves the counsellor drawing out and putting into words the feelings, meanings, themes, and implications that the client has only hinted at, implied, or left half-stated, in effect helping the client see themselves more fully, including material they have not yet consciously articulated. Because advanced empathy probes beneath the surface and confronts the client with deeper or unspoken content, it can feel intrusive, premature, or threatening if used before a solid, trusting relationship has been established. It is therefore considered inappropriate in an initial interview and is reserved for later stages once trust and rapport are firm. Primary empathy and culturally sensitive empathy, by contrast, are appropriate and necessary from the outset. Because advanced empathy is the type to avoid early on, option a is correct.","topic":"Egan: primary vs advanced empathy"},{"q":"Which of the following is a culture-bound syndrome where the patient responds to minimal stimuli with exaggerated startles, often exclaiming normally inhibited sexually denotative words?","options":{"a":"Latah","b":"Koro","c":"Dhat","d":"Susto"},"correct":"a","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is Latah, a culture-bound syndrome documented primarily in Malaysia and Indonesia. It is characterized by an exaggerated startle response to a sudden stimulus, followed by involuntary behaviors such as echolalia (repeating others' words), echopraxia (imitating others' movements), automatic obedience, and coprolalia (uttering normally inhibited, sexually explicit words). Culture-bound syndromes were catalogued in the DSM-IV glossary and later reframed as cultural concepts of distress in DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association. Latah usually affects middle-aged women and is often treated within the culture as a source of amusement rather than illness. The condition illustrates how psychiatric presentations are shaped by cultural context: the hyperstartle exists cross-culturally, but the specific patterned aftermath is locally scripted. Distinguishing Latah from Koro (genital retraction panic, reported in Southeast Asia and southern China), Dhat (semen-loss anxiety in South Asia), and Susto (fright-induced soul loss in Latin America) is a common entrance-exam task, so anchoring each to its defining feature and region is the reliable strategy.","topic":"Culture-bound syndromes (Latah, Koro, Dhat, Susto)"},{"q":"A researcher wants to calculate the degrees of freedom ($df$) for a $3 \\times 4$ contingency table in a Chi-square test. The df is:","options":{"a":"12","b":"7","c":"6","d":"11"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is 6 because the degrees of freedom for a chi-square test of independence on a contingency table are calculated as (number of rows minus 1) multiplied by (number of columns minus 1). For a 3 by 4 table, that is (3 minus 1) times (4 minus 1), which equals 2 times 3, which equals 6. Degrees of freedom represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints (here, the fixed row and column totals, called marginal totals) are imposed. Intuitively, once you know the marginal totals of a contingency table, only a limited number of cell values can be filled in freely before the rest are forced by the requirement that the rows and columns must add up to those margins; in a 3 by 4 table exactly 6 cells can vary independently, and the remaining ones are then determined. This is why the formula subtracts 1 from each dimension before multiplying. It is important to distinguish this from the chi-square goodness-of-fit test, which compares observed frequencies in a single row of categories against expected frequencies and uses df equal to the number of categories minus 1, which is where the tempting value of 11 comes from. For a two-way table always use (r minus 1)(c minus 1). Here that gives 2 times 3 equals 6.","topic":"Chi-square degrees of freedom"},{"q":"The first-grade teacher gives students stickers when they perform well. If they earn five stickers in one day they are exempt from homework. The stickers in this example could also be called:","options":{"a":"Primary reinforcer","b":"Token","c":"Generalized reinforcer","d":"Prepotent responses"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is token. In operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner), a token is a conditioned reinforcer (a secondary reinforcer) that has no intrinsic value but can be accumulated and exchanged for something the learner actually wants, called a backup reinforcer. Here the stickers themselves do nothing biologically rewarding; their power comes entirely from the rule that five of them earn an exemption from homework. This is the defining structure of a token economy, a behaviour-modification system developed by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin and widely used in classrooms, psychiatric wards, and rehabilitation settings. The procedure works because tokens bridge the delay between desirable behaviour and the eventual reward, can be delivered immediately and consistently, and let the learner choose when to cash in. Tokens become reinforcing through association with the backup reward, exactly as money becomes reinforcing because it buys goods. The question specifically names the stickers, the exchangeable items, which is why 'token' rather than the more general 'generalized reinforcer' is the most precise label, since the stickers here buy one specific privilege rather than a wide range of rewards.","topic":"Token economy and secondary reinforcers"},{"q":"To determine whether a newly developed intelligence test truly accurately measures intelligence or not, it is necessary to administer the test with one that has been in the field for some time. Which of the following needs to be looked for?","options":{"a":"Construct validity","b":"Concurrent validity","c":"Content validity","d":"Predictive validity"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is: Concurrent validity. Correlating a new test with an already established test given at the same time is CONCURRENT validity (a form of criterion validity where the criterion is measured concurrently). The presence of an existing well-validated test administered alongside is the giveaway.","topic":"Concurrent validity"},{"q":"Miss Pooja is a school teacher who gives her students group projects as part of their Maths curriculum. What might she do to minimise the effects of social loafing?","options":{"a":"Grade the group as a whole on the project instead of giving each group member a grade","b":"Grade both the groups as a whole and each individual group member on the project","c":"Assign the children to groups randomly instead of letting them choose their own","d":"Give students the option to present their project in front of the class for extra credit"},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is to grade both the group as a whole and each individual group member. Social loafing is the tendency for individuals to exert less effort when working collectively on a task than when working alone, because their individual contribution is pooled and cannot be easily identified or evaluated. It was demonstrated in early work by Max Ringelmann (the Ringelmann effect, where individual pulling effort on a rope decreased as group size increased) and studied further by Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams, and Stephen Harkins. A central cause is the loss of individual identifiability and accountability: when only the group outcome is evaluated, individuals feel their slacking will go unnoticed and their extra effort unrewarded (dispensability of effort). The most effective remedy, therefore, is to restore individual accountability by making each person's contribution identifiable and evaluable. Grading both the group product and each member's individual contribution ensures that students cannot hide in the collective and are held responsible for their own share, which directly reduces loafing. Option A does the opposite by removing individual evaluation entirely. Random assignment and optional presentations do not address identifiability. This item tests understanding of social loafing and the accountability-based strategy for minimising it.","topic":"Social loafing and how to reduce it"},{"q":"Gordon Allport preferred which of the following research approaches in his personality research?","options":{"a":"Factorial Design","b":"Nomothetic Approach","c":"Idiographic Approach","d":"Chi square Method"},"correct":"c","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is the idiographic approach. Gordon Allport, a founding figure of personality psychology, distinguished two broad approaches to studying personality: the nomothetic approach, which seeks general laws and compares people on the same set of common traits (favoured by trait and factor theorists such as Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck), and the idiographic approach, which focuses on the depth, uniqueness, and individuality of a single person, using methods like case studies, diaries, and letters. Although Allport acknowledged common traits shared across people, he strongly emphasised the idiographic study of the individual, arguing that each person possesses unique personal dispositions that cannot be fully captured by comparing them on universal dimensions. He famously classified traits into cardinal traits (dominant, pervasive), central traits (general characteristics forming the core personality), and secondary traits (situation-specific). His idiographic leaning is illustrated by his intensive analysis of personal documents, such as the Letters from Jenny study. The distractors point to statistical or group-comparison methods (factorial design, chi-square, nomothetic) that emphasise commonalities rather than individuality. This item tests whether the student links Allport specifically with the idiographic, person-centred tradition.","topic":"Idiographic vs nomothetic approach (Allport)"},{"q":"Despite his severe amnesia, patient H.M. was able to learn and improve performance on a mirror-tracing task over several days, even though he had no conscious recollection of having practiced it before. This demonstrates the preservation of:","options":{"a":"Episodic memory","b":"Declarative memory","c":"Procedural memory","d":"Semantic memory"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is procedural memory, because H.M.'s steady improvement on the mirror-tracing task across days, despite having no conscious memory of ever practicing it, demonstrates that his procedural memory, the memory for motor skills and habits, remained intact even though his declarative memory was devastated. Understand the fundamental dissociation this reveals. Memory is not a single system; a major division separates declarative (explicit) memory, the conscious recollection of facts (semantic) and events (episodic), from nondeclarative (implicit) memory, which includes procedural memory for skills, priming, and conditioning, and operates without conscious awareness. Procedural memory is the know-how of riding a bicycle, typing, or tracing a figure while watching your hand in a mirror, a difficult perceptual-motor skill. When Brenda Milner tested H.M. on the mirror-drawing task over successive days, his performance got measurably better and smoother, the normal learning curve, yet each day he insisted he had never done the task before, because his damaged hippocampus could not form the declarative memory of the practice sessions. This double dissociation, impaired declarative learning alongside preserved procedural learning, was crucial evidence that these memory systems depend on different brain structures: the medial temporal lobe and hippocampus for declarative memory, and other structures such as the basal ganglia and cerebellum for procedural memory. So the ability H.M. retained, learning a skill without any conscious memory of learning it, is procedural (implicit) memory.","topic":"H.M. and procedural vs declarative memory"},{"q":"According to Jean Piaget, a child in the concrete operational stage is capable of logical thought, but struggles with:","options":{"a":"Object permanence","b":"Egocentrism","c":"Hypothetico-deductive reasoning","d":"Conservation of mass"},"correct":"c","section":"dev","explanation":"The answer is hypothetico-deductive reasoning, because the concrete operational child can reason logically about tangible, present realities but cannot yet think abstractly and systematically about hypothetical possibilities, which is the hallmark of the later formal operational stage. Understand Jean Piaget's four stages so the boundary is clear. Piaget proposed that children construct knowledge through stages of qualitatively different thinking. The sensorimotor stage (birth to about two) is about acting on the world and achieving object permanence. The preoperational stage (about two to seven) brings symbolic thought and language but is limited by egocentrism, centration, and the inability to conserve. The concrete operational stage (about seven to eleven) is when genuine logical operations appear: the child can conserve quantity, classify, seriate, and reason reversibly, but crucially only about concrete, actual, observable things in front of them. What still eludes the concrete operational child is abstraction, and specifically hypothetico-deductive reasoning, the ability to generate hypotheses about what could be true and test them systematically, as in reasoning through a scientific problem or contemplating purely verbal, contrary-to-fact propositions. That capacity emerges in the formal operational stage (about eleven onward), which also brings abstract, idealistic, and propositional thought. So the concrete operational child is defined by mastery of concrete logic paired with an inability to handle the abstract and hypothetical, making hypothetico-deductive reasoning the correct answer, and note that conservation is an achievement of this stage, not a struggle.","topic":"Piaget concrete operations"},{"q":"What is Socratic questioning?","options":{"a":"a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously overlooked information and to guide the discovery of the therapist rather than interpretation from the individual","b":"a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously attended information and to guide the discovery of the individual rather than interpretation from the therapist","c":"a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously overlooked information and to guide the discovery of the individual rather than interpretation from the therapist","d":"a form of dialogue that helps a person to discover and attend to previously attended information and to guide the discovery of the therapist rather than interpretation from the individual"},"correct":"c","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct answer is option c. Socratic questioning, a cornerstone of the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (named after the Greek philosopher Socrates and his method of eliciting knowledge through disciplined questioning), is a form of guided dialogue in which the therapist asks a systematic series of questions to help clients examine their own thoughts and beliefs. Two features define it precisely, and option c captures both. First, its purpose is to help the person discover and attend to previously overlooked information, that is, evidence, alternative interpretations, and perspectives the client had not previously considered or had ignored because of their cognitive distortions. Second, the direction of discovery belongs to the individual (the client), not the therapist: the therapist does not lecture, interpret, or impose conclusions but instead uses questions to guide the client to reach new understandings themselves. This client-led discovery is exactly what makes it part of guided discovery and collaborative empiricism in cognitive therapy, and it contrasts sharply with the interpretive stance of psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud). The distractor options each corrupt one or both of these elements, either by substituting previously attended for previously overlooked information, or by locating the discovery in the therapist rather than the individual. Only option c states both correctly.","topic":"Socratic questioning in CBT"},{"q":"The genetic mechanisms that ultimately contribute to the symptoms and difficulties experienced by people with psychological disorders are known as:","options":{"a":"Endophenotypes","b":"Phenotypes","c":"Genotypes","d":"Molecular genetics"},"correct":"a","section":"disorders","explanation":"The answer is endophenotypes because the question asks for the genetically influenced intermediate mechanisms that lie between the underlying genes and the observable symptoms of a psychological disorder. An endophenotype is a measurable, heritable trait, often a neurophysiological, biochemical, cognitive, or neuroanatomical characteristic, that is not visible to the naked eye but sits along the causal pathway from genotype to the full clinical phenotype. The concept was introduced to psychiatric genetics because complex disorders like schizophrenia or bipolar disorder are not caused by single genes producing obvious effects; instead, many genes contribute to subtle intermediate processes (for example, deficits in working memory, abnormal eye-tracking, or altered prepulse inhibition) that are more directly tied to specific genes than the broad diagnostic syndrome is. By studying these simpler, more genetically tractable endophenotypes, researchers hope to bridge the gap between DNA and the messy, multiply-determined symptom picture. Contrast this with the broader terms: the genotype is the organism's complete genetic constitution, the phenotype is any observable characteristic resulting from genes plus environment, and molecular genetics is the field that studies genes at the level of DNA. The defining feature of an endophenotype is that it is the heritable intermediate that ultimately contributes to symptoms, which is exactly what the question describes, making endophenotypes the precise answer.","topic":"Endophenotypes"},{"q":"In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is :","options":{"a":"Left of the median","b":"Right of the median","c":"Same as median","d":"Same as a mode"},"correct":"a","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is left of the median. Skewness describes the asymmetry of a distribution. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution, the long tail points toward the low (left) end of the scale. Extreme low scores in that tail pull the arithmetic mean downward more than they move the median, because the mean is sensitive to every value while the median depends only on rank position. The result is the ordering mean less than median less than mode. So the mean sits to the left of the median. The mirror image holds for a positively (right) skewed distribution, where the ordering is mode less than median less than mean, placing the mean to the right. This relationship among the three measures of central tendency was popularized through the empirical work of Karl Pearson, who also defined coefficients of skewness using the gap between mean and mode or mean and median. Understanding it lets you infer the shape of a distribution from summary statistics alone, which is why it is a staple of psychology statistics papers.","topic":"Skewness: mean, median, mode positions"},{"q":"The correct sequence of the four stages of creative thinking, as proposed by Graham Wallas, is:","options":{"a":"Preparation, Illumination, Incubation, Verification","b":"Incubation, Preparation, Verification, Illumination","c":"Preparation, Incubation, Illumination, Verification","d":"Illumination, Preparation, Verification, Incubation"},"correct":"c","section":"general","explanation":"The answer is preparation, incubation, illumination, verification, because that is the exact four-stage sequence of the creative process as described by Graham Wallas in his 1926 book The Art of Thought. Understand each stage so the order becomes logical rather than memorized. Preparation is the first stage, the deliberate, conscious work of defining the problem, gathering relevant information, and attempting solutions through effortful analysis; the mind loads itself with the raw materials. Incubation follows, a period in which the person stops actively working on the problem and turns attention elsewhere, allowing unconscious processing to continue in the background; this is why solutions often surface during a walk, a shower, or sleep rather than at the desk. Illumination, sometimes called the insight or aha moment, is the third stage, the sudden emergence of the solution or key idea into consciousness, often experienced as instantaneous and effortless precisely because the unconscious work of incubation prepared it. Verification is the final stage, in which the insight is consciously tested, evaluated, refined, and elaborated to confirm it actually works and to render it usable. The logic of the ordering is a rhythm of conscious effort, then unconscious rest, then sudden insight, then conscious checking, so preparation must come first and verification last, with incubation necessarily preceding illumination. Some later writers insert a fifth stage, but Wallas's canonical four in the order preparation, incubation, illumination, verification is the exam answer, and the name to attach is Graham Wallas.","topic":"Wallas' four stages of creativity"},{"q":"In the Rorschach Inkblot Test, a response that is determined primarily by the chromatic color of the blot (e.g., \"this is a blood stain because it's red\") is scored as:","options":{"a":"FC (Form-Color)","b":"CF (Color-Form)","c":"C (Pure Color)","d":"Cn (Color Naming)"},"correct":"c","section":"assessment","explanation":"The answer is C, pure Color, because a Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) response determined primarily by the chromatic color of the blot with essentially no contribution from form, such as saying it is a blood stain because it is red, is scored as a pure Color response, coded C. Understand the color determinants in Rorschach scoring, which form a graded series reflecting how much form versus color drives the response, and which are interpreted as indexing emotional control. When a person responds to the colored (chromatic) areas of the inkblots, the scorer distinguishes among several codes based on the relative weight of form and color. FC (Form-Color) means form is the dominant determinant and color is secondary; the response has a definite shape that fits the blot, with color playing a supporting role, for example a green caterpillar where the shape is caterpillar-like and green adds to it. CF (Color-Form) means color is dominant and form is secondary and vaguer, for example some ice cream that is mostly about the color with only loose form. C (pure Color) means the response is based on color alone with no form at all, for example blood because it is red, where nothing about the shape matters, only the redness. Cn (Color Naming) is the extreme case where the person merely names colors without using them to build any percept. Interpretively, this series is read as reflecting the balance between emotional reactivity and rational control: FC suggests well-modulated, controlled affect, while CF and especially pure C suggest more impulsive, poorly regulated emotional responsiveness, since form (associated with cognitive control) drops out. The stem's blood-because-red response, driven purely by chromatic color with no form, is scored pure Color, C.","topic":"Rorschach scoring: colour determinants (FC, CF, C)"},{"q":"Cyber bullies are individuals who use electronic devices to harass and act aggressively toward other individuals online. Because the internet is an anonymous forum these individuals may say and do things that they would never do in person. They are cruel, hurtful and their behaviour is often reprehensible. What group dynamic does the behaviour of a cyber bully represent?","options":{"a":"Social facilitation","b":"Group polarisation","c":"Bystander effect","d":"Deindividuation"},"correct":"d","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is deindividuation. Deindividuation is a social psychological state in which individuals lose their sense of individual identity and self-awareness within a group or under conditions of anonymity, leading to a reduction in personal responsibility and self-restraint, which in turn can produce impulsive, antisocial, or aggressive behaviour that the person would normally inhibit. The concept was elaborated by Leon Festinger (who introduced the term with colleagues), Philip Zimbardo, and Edward Diener. Anonymity, diffused responsibility, and high arousal are key contributors: when people feel unidentifiable and less accountable, internal moral controls weaken. Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment and studies showing that anonymity (for example, wearing hoods) increased aggression illustrate the effect. The internet is a powerful engine of deindividuation because it grants anonymity and physical distance, so cyberbullies feel unidentifiable and unaccountable and behave in cruel ways they would never enact face to face, exactly the scenario described. This is sometimes called the online disinhibition effect. The distractors name other group phenomena that do not hinge on loss of identity through anonymity: social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898), group polarisation, and the bystander effect (John Darley and Bibb Latané, 1968). This item tests recognition of deindividuation as the mechanism behind anonymous online aggression.","topic":"Deindividuation"},{"q":"The concept of \"object relations\" in psychoanalytic theory refers to:","options":{"a":"The relationship between the id, ego, and superego.","b":"The internalized mental representations of self and others and the relationship between them.","c":"The use of defense mechanisms to protect the ego from anxiety.","d":"The stage of psychosexual development focused on the genitals."},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The answer is the internalized mental representations of self and others and the relationships between them, because that is precisely what object relations refers to in psychoanalytic theory. Understand object relations theory and how it departs from classical Freudian thought (Sigmund Freud). In psychoanalytic terminology, the word object has a specific technical meaning: it refers to the target of one's emotional energy or drives, almost always another person (or a mental image of a person), beginning with the primary caregiver. Object relations theory, developed by a group of largely British theorists including Melanie Klein, W. R. D. Fairbairn, Donald Winnicott, and later Otto Kernberg, shifted the emphasis of psychoanalysis away from Freud's focus on instinctual drives seeking gratification and toward the fundamental human need for relationships. The central claim is that from infancy we internalize our experiences with important others, forming enduring unconscious mental representations, or internal objects, of both other people and of ourselves in relation to them, along with the emotional tone connecting them. These internalized self-and-object representations, laid down in early relationships, form templates that shape how we perceive, feel about, and relate to people throughout life, largely outside awareness. Disturbances in early relationships produce distorted or split internal object relations, which underlie many personality difficulties; for example, Kernberg explained borderline pathology in terms of splitting, the failure to integrate good and bad representations of self and others. So object relations means the internalized mental representations of self and others and the dynamic relationships between them, which is the correct answer.","topic":"Object relations theory"},{"q":"Ria has difficulty enunciating words correctly and speaks in a slow and laboured way; her speech often makes sense, but it includes only keywords. Which part of her brain is damaged?","options":{"a":"Wernicke's area","b":"Broca's area","c":"Temporal lobe","d":"Frontal lobe"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is Broca's area. Broca's area, located in the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus of the (usually left) frontal lobe, was identified by the French physician Paul Broca in the 1860s through his patient known as Tan, who could understand language but could barely produce speech. Damage to this region causes Broca's aphasia, also called expressive or non-fluent aphasia. Its hallmarks match Ria's presentation exactly: speech is slow, effortful, and poorly articulated, output is reduced to essential content or keywords (agrammatic, telegraphic speech that drops function words like articles and prepositions), yet comprehension is relatively preserved and what the patient does say tends to be meaningful. Patients are often painfully aware of their deficit, which distinguishes it from Wernicke's aphasia. Wernicke's area, in the superior temporal gyrus of the temporal lobe and described by Carl Wernicke, produces the opposite pattern when damaged: fluent, effortless, but empty or nonsensical speech with impaired comprehension. Because Ria's difficulty is in production, effortful and telegraphic but meaningful, the damage localizes to Broca's area, making it the precise answer over the broader frontal lobe option.","topic":"Broca's aphasia vs Wernicke's aphasia"},{"q":"Thomas and Chess identified nine dimensions of temperament by:","options":{"a":"Interviewing parents using questionnaires","b":"Drawing on their clinical experience and previous studies","c":"Observing babies","d":"Both a) and c)"},"correct":"d","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is both interviewing parents using questionnaires and observing babies. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess conducted the landmark New York Longitudinal Study (NYLS), beginning in 1956, which followed infants over many years to study temperament, the biologically based, early-appearing individual differences in behavioural style. From detailed parent interviews and questionnaires about their infants' typical reactions, combined with direct observation of the babies, they identified nine dimensions of temperament: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity), approach or withdrawal to new stimuli, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span or persistence. By clustering these dimensions, Thomas and Chess described three broad temperamental types: the easy child (regular, adaptable, positive mood), the difficult child (irregular, intense, slow to adapt, negative mood), and the slow-to-warm-up child (mild, initially withdrawing but gradually adapting). They also introduced the influential concept of goodness of fit, the match between a child's temperament and environmental demands. Because their methodology combined both parental report and observation of the infants, the inclusive option is correct. This item tests knowledge of the NYLS methodology and Thomas and Chess's temperament framework.","topic":"Thomas and Chess temperament"},{"q":"A client with OCD washes their hands excessively to neutralize the anxiety caused by intrusive thoughts of contamination. This hand-washing behavior is BEST conceptualized as a(n):","options":{"a":"Conditioned stimulus","b":"Unconditioned response","c":"Avoidance response / Safety behavior","d":"Operant behavior maintained by positive reinforcement"},"correct":"c","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is an avoidance response or safety behavior, because the compulsive hand-washing functions to reduce and prevent the anxiety triggered by contamination obsessions, and behavior that removes or forestalls an aversive state is an avoidance response maintained by negative reinforcement. Understand the behavioral model of obsessive-compulsive disorder, most influentially the two-process theory applied to OCD by Stanley Rachman and others, building on O. Hobart Mowrer's account of how fears are acquired and maintained. In OCD, an intrusive thought, such as an image of contamination, becomes associated with intense anxiety (this classical-conditioning-like process establishes the obsession as a trigger for distress). The person then discovers that performing a compulsion, here hand-washing, temporarily reduces that anxiety. This is the operant half of the model: because the compulsion is followed by relief from an aversive state (the drop in anxiety), it is strengthened through negative reinforcement, the removal of an unpleasant stimulus increasing the behavior. The compulsion thus becomes an avoidance or safety behavior, an action performed to escape or prevent feared consequences and the accompanying distress. The tragic paradox is that this relief is short-lived and the behavior is self-perpetuating: by neutralizing the anxiety, the compulsion prevents the person from ever learning that the feared catastrophe would not have happened anyway, so the obsession is never disconfirmed and the cycle strengthens. This is exactly why the treatment of choice, exposure and response prevention, deliberately blocks the safety behavior so the anxiety can extinguish naturally. The hand-washing is best conceptualized as an avoidance or safety behavior sustained by negative reinforcement.","topic":"OCD: compulsions as safety behaviours"},{"q":"Choose the correct arrangement of these stages of elevated mood based upon severity: i) Elation, ii) Exaltation, iii) Euphoria, iv) Ecstasy.","options":{"a":"i, ii, iii & iv","b":"iii, i, ii & iv","c":"iv, iii, ii & i","d":"None of the above"},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct ascending order of elevated mood by severity is euphoria, elation, exaltation, and ecstasy, which corresponds to sequence iii, i, ii, iv, making b the right answer. This question tests the descriptive psychopathology of mood elevation, a graded continuum used especially in describing mania and hypomania. At the mildest end is euphoria, an exaggerated sense of well-being and contentment that may still seem within normal bounds. Next is elation, a more pronounced and infectious feeling of joy and heightened confidence accompanied by increased activity and optimism. More severe is exaltation, an intense elevation of mood often coloured by grandiosity, in which the person may feel exceptionally powerful, important, or expansive. The most extreme degree is ecstasy, a state of overwhelming, transcendent rapture or bliss, frequently associated with a sense of mystical union or being in contact with the divine, and seen in severe mania and certain psychotic or seizure-related states. Recognising this hierarchy matters clinically because the degree of mood elevation helps gauge the severity of a manic episode and the likelihood of accompanying psychotic features and impaired insight. The trick in the question is that the four terms are listed out of order, so the candidate must reorder them from least to most intense rather than accept the given sequence.","topic":"Elevated mood: euphoria, elation, exaltation, ecstasy"},{"q":"‘Reinforcement will have positive effect on performance,’ which type of hypothesis is this?","options":{"a":"Null and directional","b":"Alternate and directional","c":"Null and non-directional","d":"Alternate and non-directional"},"correct":"b","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is alternate and directional. In hypothesis testing, researchers distinguish between the null hypothesis and the alternate (alternative) hypothesis, and between directional and non-directional forms. The null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or no relationship, for example that reinforcement has no effect on performance. The alternate hypothesis (H1) states that there is an effect or relationship. The statement 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' asserts that an effect exists, so it is an alternate hypothesis, not a null one. Furthermore, it specifies the direction of that effect: not merely that reinforcement affects performance, but that it improves it (a positive effect). When a hypothesis specifies the direction of the expected effect (such as 'greater than,' 'less than,' 'improves,' or 'reduces'), it is a directional hypothesis, tested with a one-tailed test. When it merely predicts a difference or relationship without specifying which way it will go (such as 'reinforcement will affect performance'), it is non-directional, tested with a two-tailed test. Because this statement both claims an effect (alternate) and specifies that the effect is positive (directional), it is an alternate and directional hypothesis. This is a standard research-methodology classification.","topic":"Types of hypotheses: null, alternate, directional"},{"q":"\"Escape learning\" differs from \"avoidance learning\" in that, in escape learning, the behavior:","options":{"a":"Prevents the onset of an aversive stimulus.","b":"Terminates an aversive stimulus that is already present.","c":"Is maintained by positive reinforcement.","d":"Is under the control of a discriminative stimulus."},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The answer is that in escape learning the behavior terminates an aversive stimulus that is already present, because this is precisely what distinguishes escape learning from avoidance learning. Understand both within the framework of negative reinforcement and O. Hobart Mowrer's two-process theory. Both escape and avoidance are forms of negatively reinforced behavior, meaning the behavior is strengthened by the removal or non-occurrence of something aversive, and the key difference lies in timing relative to the aversive event. In escape learning, the aversive stimulus is already occurring, and the behavior ends it. For example, a rat in a chamber where the floor is being electrified learns to jump to the other side to stop the shock it is currently feeling; the shock is present and the response escapes it. In avoidance learning, the behavior occurs before the aversive stimulus begins and prevents its onset altogether, usually cued by a warning signal. For instance, if a light comes on a few seconds before the shock, the rat learns to jump when the light appears, thereby avoiding the shock entirely so it never occurs. Mowrer's two-process theory explains avoidance as involving both classical conditioning (the warning signal becomes a conditioned stimulus that elicits fear) and operant conditioning (the avoidance response is negatively reinforced by the reduction of that conditioned fear). Developmentally, avoidance often grows out of escape: the organism first learns to escape the aversive event, then learns to respond to the warning signal to avoid it. Because the stem asks what escape learning specifically involves, the correct feature is terminating an aversive stimulus that is already present.","topic":"Escape vs avoidance learning"},{"q":"Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM) measure which kind of intelligence?","options":{"a":"Crystallised intelligence","b":"Fluid intelligence","c":"Both a and b","d":"General Intelligence"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is fluid intelligence. Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM), developed by John C. Raven in 1938, is a nonverbal test in which the examinee identifies the missing element that completes a visual pattern or matrix, with items increasing in difficulty. It is designed to measure fluid intelligence, the ability to reason, perceive relationships, and solve novel problems independently of previously acquired knowledge or education. This maps onto the Gf-Gc distinction introduced by Raymond Cattell and elaborated by John Horn: fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity for abstract reasoning and problem-solving with novel material, whereas crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the accumulated knowledge, vocabulary, and skills gained through experience and education. Because the matrices rely on abstract visual reasoning rather than language or learned facts, they are considered relatively culture-fair (or culture-reduced) and are used cross-culturally. The test was also historically important as a strong marker of Spearman's g, the general intelligence factor identified by Charles Spearman, and the SPM does correlate highly with general intelligence. However, its specific and intended construct is fluid reasoning, not crystallised knowledge. Because Raven's Progressive Matrices most precisely measure fluid intelligence, option b is correct.","topic":"Raven's Progressive Matrices and fluid intelligence"},{"q":"The bystander effect, where the presence of others inhibits helping, is primarily explained by two factors:","options":{"a":"Social loafing and deindividuation.","b":"Diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance.","c":"Social comparison and normative influence.","d":"Group polarization and groupthink."},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"The answer is diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance, because these are the two classic mechanisms that Bibb Latane and John Darley identified as producing the bystander effect. Understand the phenomenon and the research behind it. The bystander effect is the counterintuitive finding that the more people who are present at an emergency, the less likely any single individual is to help. Latane and Darley investigated this after the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese, and through a series of ingenious experiments they isolated the psychological processes responsible. The first is diffusion of responsibility. When many bystanders are present, the felt personal responsibility to act is divided among all of them, so each individual feels less obligated to intervene, reasoning that someone else will or should do it; when a person is the sole witness, all the responsibility falls on them and they are far more likely to help. The second is pluralistic ignorance, a process operating during the interpretation of the situation. In an ambiguous emergency, each bystander looks to the reactions of others to decide whether the situation is truly an emergency; because everyone is trying to appear calm and is simultaneously looking to others, each person concludes from the others' apparent calm that nothing is wrong, so the group collectively misreads the emergency as a non-emergency. Latane and Darley formalized these ideas in a decision model of helping with sequential steps, noticing the event, interpreting it as an emergency, assuming responsibility, knowing how to help, and deciding to act. The two processes that inhibit helping when others are present are diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance.","topic":"Bystander effect: diffusion of responsibility, pluralistic ignorance"},{"q":"The basic assumption of Adler's individual psychology is","options":{"a":"human behaviour is purposeful and goal directed.","b":"an optimistic view of human life.","c":"human beings are social beings who could be understood in terms of their relationship with others.","d":"development is a lifelong process and ego identity is granted a central place in the process."},"correct":"a","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is: human behaviour is purposeful and goal directed.. Behaviour is purposeful and goal directed. This teleology is the core of Alfred Adler: people are pulled by future goals (striving for superiority, fictional finalism) rather than only pushed by past drives. Optimism (b) and social embeddedness/social interest (c) are Adlerian flavours but not the defining basic assumption, and (d) is Erik Erikson's lifespan/ego-identity view.","topic":"Adler's individual psychology"},{"q":"The brain region most critically involved in processing the prosody or emotional tone of speech (speech intonation) is typically:","options":{"a":"Left inferior frontal gyrus (Broca's area)","b":"Right hemisphere regions homotopic to language areas","c":"Primary motor cortex","d":"Occipital lobe"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is right-hemisphere regions homotopic to the language areas, because the emotional prosody of speech, its melodic intonation and emotional tone, is processed chiefly by regions of the right hemisphere that mirror, or are homotopic to, the classical language areas of the left hemisphere. Understand the concept of hemispheric specialization and homotopic regions. In most people the left hemisphere is dominant for the propositional or literal aspects of language, the phonology, grammar, and lexical meaning handled by Broca's area (production; Paul Broca) and Wernicke's area (comprehension; Carl Wernicke). But language carries more than literal content; the same sentence can convey very different meanings and emotions through its prosody, the pitch, rhythm, stress, and melody of speech that signal whether a statement is a question, sarcastic, angry, joyful, or sad. Research, notably by Elliott Ross who described the aprosodias, showed that this emotional and affective prosody is largely a right-hemisphere function, and specifically it is processed by areas in the right hemisphere that are the mirror-image counterparts of the left-hemisphere language areas; these mirror-image regions are called homotopic areas (homotopic meaning occupying corresponding positions in the two hemispheres). Thus damage to the right-hemisphere region homotopic to Broca's area produces motor aprosodia, an inability to produce emotional intonation, so speech sounds flat and monotone, while damage to the right-hemisphere region homotopic to Wernicke's area produces sensory aprosodia, difficulty comprehending the emotional tone in others' speech. This elegant left-right division means the left hemisphere handles what is said while the right handles how it is said emotionally. The correct answer is therefore the right-hemisphere regions homotopic to the language areas.","topic":"Right hemisphere and prosody"},{"q":"At which stage, according to Kohlberg's theory of moral development, does the individual view laws as instruments for expressing the will of the majority and furthering welfare?","options":{"a":"Punishment-and-obedience orientation","b":"Social-order-maintaining morality","c":"Morality of individual principles of conscience","d":"Social-contract orientation"},"correct":"d","section":"dev","explanation":"The keyed answer is the social contract orientation. Lawrence Kohlberg proposed that moral reasoning develops through three broad levels, each with two stages, defined by the kind of reasoning behind a judgment rather than the content of the judgment. The preconventional level (stages 1 and 2) judges right and wrong by consequences to the self, avoiding punishment and seeking reward. The conventional level (stages 3 and 4) judges by social approval and then by maintaining law and order for its own sake. The postconventional or principled level (stages 5 and 6) reasons from abstract ethical principles. Stage 5, the social contract orientation, is exactly the view the question describes: laws are understood not as fixed and sacred but as social contracts, instruments agreed upon to express the will of the majority and to maximise welfare, and therefore as changeable through democratic process when they fail to serve the common good. Stage 6, the morality of individual conscience, goes further to universal ethical principles that may override unjust laws. So the phrase laws as instruments for expressing the will of the majority and furthering welfare is the textbook signature of stage 5. The broader exam point is that Kohlberg's progression moves from self interest, through conformity and rule following, to a flexible, principle based understanding of justice.","topic":"Kohlberg's stages of moral development"},{"q":"The behavioral technique that involves removing a desirable stimulus following an undesirable behavior in order to reduce the frequency of that behavior is called:","options":{"a":"Negative reinforcement","b":"Response cost","c":"Positive punishment","d":"Extinction"},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is response cost because it is precisely defined as the removal of a desirable or reinforcing stimulus following an unwanted behaviour, in order to decrease that behaviour. Response cost is a form of negative punishment within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning framework, and understanding the two-by-two logic of operant consequences makes it clear. Operant consequences are classified by two questions: does the consequence increase or decrease the behaviour, and does it involve adding (positive) or removing (negative) a stimulus. Reinforcement always increases behaviour, punishment always decreases it; positive means a stimulus is presented, negative means a stimulus is taken away. So negative punishment means decreasing a behaviour by taking away something the person values. Response cost is the everyday name for this: fining someone money for speeding, deducting points or privileges, or taking tokens away in a token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) after misbehaviour. A closely related negative-punishment procedure is time-out, which removes access to a reinforcing environment. The distractors each occupy a different cell of the framework: negative reinforcement removes an aversive to increase behaviour, positive punishment adds an aversive to decrease behaviour, and extinction is a separate process of withholding the reinforcement that maintained a behaviour so it gradually fades. Because the question describes taking away a pleasant stimulus to reduce a behaviour, that is response cost.","topic":"Response cost vs punishment vs reinforcement"},{"q":"We know that a distinction is made between positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia. What is the difference between these symptoms?","options":{"a":"Negative symptoms indicate the presence of something normal and negative symptoms indicate the absence of something unusual","b":"Positive symptoms indicate the presence of something normal, and Negative symptoms indicate the absence of something unusual","c":"Positive symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual, and Negative symptoms indicate the absence of something normal","d":"Positive symptoms indicate the absence of something normal, negative symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual"},"correct":"c","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is that positive symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual, and negative symptoms indicate the absence of something normal. In schizophrenia, symptoms are grouped into two broad categories based on whether they add something abnormal or take away something normal, a distinction refined by researchers such as Timothy Crow and Nancy Andreasen. Positive symptoms are pathological excesses or additions: they reflect the presence of experiences that should not be there, such as hallucinations (perceiving things that are not present), delusions (fixed false beliefs), and disorganised speech and thought. The word positive here means added, not good. Negative symptoms are pathological deficits: they reflect the absence or reduction of normal functions, such as flat or blunted affect (reduced emotional expression), avolition (loss of motivation), alogia (poverty of speech), and social withdrawal. The word negative means taken away, not bad. Option c captures this precisely, while the other options either reverse the definitions or muddle presence and absence. This distinction matters clinically because positive and negative symptoms respond differently to treatment: antipsychotic medications, especially older ones, are more effective against positive symptoms, whereas negative symptoms are often more persistent and harder to treat, and they strongly predict long-term disability and functional outcome.","topic":"Positive vs negative symptoms of schizophrenia"},{"q":"In experimental psychology, what does a p-value represent when using biostatistical tests?","options":{"a":"The likelihood that the null hypothesis is true","b":"The probability of observing the data if the null hypothesis is true","c":"The magnitude of correlation","d":"The sample size of the experiment"},"correct":"b","section":"research","explanation":"The p-value, central to null hypothesis significance testing developed by Ronald Fisher and formalized alongside the framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, is defined as the probability of obtaining results at least as extreme as those observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true. It is a conditional probability: it is computed under the assumption that the null (usually of no effect or no difference) holds, and it quantifies how surprising the data would be under that assumption. A small p-value (conventionally below 0.05) means the observed data would be unlikely if the null were true, providing evidence to reject the null. The most common error, captured by distractor a, is to read the p-value as the probability that the null hypothesis is true; it is not, because it presupposes the null rather than evaluating it directly. Distractor c confuses it with effect size, and d with sample size. Because the p-value is the probability of the observed (or more extreme) data given a true null hypothesis, option b is correct.","topic":"p-value interpretation"},{"q":"The representativeness heuristic can lead to errors when :","options":{"a":"Individuals ignore base rates and focus solely on similarity","b":"Individuals rely on readily available information","c":"Individuals fail to consider alternative explanations","d":"Individuals adjust their judgements based on initial estimates"},"correct":"a","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is individuals ignore base rates and focus solely on similarity. The representativeness heuristic, identified by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, is the shortcut of judging the probability that an object or event belongs to a category by how closely it resembles the typical or prototypical member of that category. Its most famous failure mode is base-rate neglect: people focus on how representative or similar a case seems and ignore the prior probability, or base rate, of the category in the population. For instance, in the classic Linda problem and the lawyer-engineer studies, participants judged category membership by stereotype fit while disregarding how common each category actually was, leading to systematic errors including the conjunction fallacy. Because the defining error of representativeness is overweighting similarity and underweighting base rates, option A is correct. Relying on readily available information (option B) describes the availability heuristic, a different shortcut based on ease of recall. Failing to consider alternative explanations (option C) is a broad reasoning shortcoming, not the specific mechanism by which representativeness misleads. Adjusting judgements based on initial estimates (option D) describes the anchoring and adjustment heuristic, in which an initial anchor unduly influences the final judgement. The signature pitfall of representativeness is precisely the neglect of base rates in favour of resemblance. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","topic":"Representativeness heuristic and base rates"},{"q":"Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?","options":{"a":"That two tests are done at the same time","b":"Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome","c":"The items on the test consistently relate to each other.","d":"The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute"},"correct":"d","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute (assessed at the same time). Concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion-related validity, the family of validity concerned with how well a test's scores correspond to an external criterion. Criterion validity has two forms distinguished by timing. Predictive validity assesses how well a test predicts a criterion measured in the future (for example, whether an aptitude test predicts later job performance). Concurrent validity assesses how well a test correlates with an established criterion or an existing validated measure of the same construct assessed at roughly the same point in time. For instance, a new, shorter depression scale shows concurrent validity if its scores correlate highly with scores on an already-established depression measure administered concurrently. The essence is agreement with a criterion in the present. Option A is a trap because it takes 'concurrent' too literally as merely testing simultaneously, without the crucial requirement of correlation with a valid criterion measuring the same attribute. Option B describes inter-rater reliability, and option C describes internal consistency reliability, both of which are reliability rather than validity. This item tests the correct definition of concurrent validity as present-time correlation with a criterion measure of the same construct.","topic":"Concurrent validity"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of \"self-esteem\" in contrast to \"self-concept\"?","options":{"a":"Self-esteem is the cognitive appraisal of one's traits, while self-concept is the evaluative and emotional component.","b":"Self-esteem is the evaluative and affective component of the self (how one feels about oneself), while self-concept is the cognitive description of who one is.","c":"Self-esteem and self-concept are identical and can be used interchangeably.","d":"Self-esteem is a collective term for the self in relation to others, while self-concept is the individual self."},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"The answer is that self-esteem is the evaluative and affective component of the self, how one feels about oneself, while self-concept is the cognitive description of who one is, because this correctly distinguishes the two closely related constructs. Understand the structure of the self in psychology, where theorists separate the descriptive from the evaluative. The self can be analyzed into components that answer different questions. Self-concept answers the question who am I; it is the cognitive component, the organized set of beliefs, descriptions, and knowledge a person holds about their own attributes, roles, values, physical characteristics, and abilities. It is essentially a mental self-portrait or self-schema (Hazel Markus, 1977), for example I am a student, I am tall, I am a sister, I play the guitar, statements of fact and description without necessarily any judgment of good or bad attached. Self-esteem answers a different question, how do I feel about who I am; it is the evaluative and affective component, the overall judgment of one's own worth and the emotions, pride or shame, satisfaction or self-dislike, that accompany that judgment. Self-esteem is whether a person likes and values themselves, high or low, positive or negative. The two are linked but distinct: the self-concept provides the content that self-esteem evaluates, so a person can have a detailed self-concept (I am short and shy) and then feel positively or negatively about those descriptions, and that feeling is self-esteem. William James early on connected self-esteem to the ratio of successes to pretensions or aspirations. The correct distinction, then, is that self-concept is the cognitive description of who one is and self-esteem is the affective evaluation of one's worth.","topic":"Self-esteem vs self-concept"},{"q":"Kelly is to the Rep test as Cattell is to the","options":{"a":"The California Psychological Inventory (CPI)","b":"The Q-sort method","c":"Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF)","d":"Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI)"},"correct":"c","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF). This analogy item asks you to match each theorist with the assessment instrument they created. George Kelly, the founder of personal construct theory, developed the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep test or Rep grid), a technique for eliciting the bipolar personal constructs an individual uses to interpret and anticipate their world; by comparing and contrasting significant people in their life, the person reveals the dimensions of meaning through which they construe experience. Analogously, Raymond Cattell developed the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF), a self-report personality inventory built through factor analysis to measure sixteen primary source traits (such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, and vigilance) that Cattell derived as the fundamental dimensions of personality. So just as the Rep test is Kelly's characteristic instrument, the 16 PF is Cattell's. The distractors are all real inventories but attributable to other authors: the California Psychological Inventory to Harrison Gough, the Q-sort method to William Stephenson (and used clinically by Carl Rogers to study the self-concept and the real-ideal self discrepancy), and the MMPI to Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley. Because Cattell's instrument is the 16 PF, option c correctly completes the analogy.","topic":"Personality tests: Rep test, 16PF"},{"q":"According to Karl Lashley's research on memory storage, where did he conclude that memories are primarily stored in the brain?","options":{"a":"In specific localized brain regions dedicated to particular memories","b":"Distributed throughout the entire brain cortex","c":"Exclusively in the hippocampus","d":"Only in the frontal and temporal lobes"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is distributed throughout the entire cortex because Karl Lashley, after decades of experiments searching for the physical location of the memory trace (which he called the engram), concluded that memories are not stored in one specific spot but are spread across the cortex. Lashley trained rats to run mazes and then surgically removed varying amounts of cortical tissue from different locations, expecting to find the one region that, when destroyed, would erase the maze memory. He never found it. Instead he discovered two principles. The first is mass action: the impairment of learning and memory was proportional to the amount of cortex removed, not to which particular area was removed, meaning the cortex acts as a whole and more damage means more deficit. The second is equipotentiality: within a functional area, any part can, up to a point, take over the job of a damaged part, so no single region holds a given memory exclusively. Together these implied the engram is distributed rather than localised. This work was foundational even though later research refined it, showing that specific structures like the hippocampus (from patient H.M.) and specific circuits do have specialised memory roles. For the exam, tie Lashley's name to mass action, equipotentiality, and distributed cortical storage of memory.","topic":"Lashley: equipotentiality and mass action"},{"q":"Jean Piaget's theory of moral development suggests that children in the stage of \"heteronomous morality\" (typically ages 5-10) base their moral judgments on:","options":{"a":"Intentions and equality","b":"Rules as being unchangeable and the consequences of actions","c":"Social contracts and individual rights","d":"Abstract principles of justice"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The answer is that heteronomous children judge morality by treating rules as unchangeable and by focusing on the consequences of actions, because that is exactly how Jean Piaget characterized the first stage of moral development. Understand Piaget's two-stage theory of moral reasoning, which he derived from studying children playing marbles and responding to moral stories. The word heteronomous means being governed by others, and heteronomous morality, also called moral realism, dominates roughly ages five to ten. In this stage the child sees rules as fixed, sacred, absolute, and handed down by authority figures such as parents or God, and therefore unchangeable; rules simply exist and must be obeyed. Two features define the child's judgments here. First, morality is judged by objective consequences rather than intentions, so a child who accidentally breaks fifteen cups is judged naughtier than one who breaks a single cup while deliberately stealing jam, because the physical damage is greater. Second, the child believes in immanent justice, the idea that wrongdoing is automatically and inevitably punished by the world itself. Piaget contrasted this with autonomous morality, or moral relativism, emerging around age ten, when the child understands that rules are social agreements that can be changed by consent, and begins judging acts by the actor's intentions rather than raw outcomes. The stem's two clues, rules as unchangeable and focus on consequences, are the twin signatures of heteronomous morality, and note that the distractors about social contracts and abstract justice belong to Lawrence Kohlberg, not Piaget.","topic":"Piaget's moral development: heteronomous morality"},{"q":"Which behavioral technique involves imagining a scene where an undesirable behavior is paired with an unpleasant consequence to reduce the behavior?","options":{"a":"Flooding","b":"Implosion","c":"Covert sensitization","d":"Systematic desensitization"},"correct":"c","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is covert sensitization because it is precisely the technique in which an undesirable behaviour is imagined and then paired, in imagination, with an unpleasant or aversive consequence in order to reduce that behaviour. Developed by Joseph Cautela in the 1960s, covert sensitization is a form of aversion therapy, but the word 'covert' is the key clue: everything happens internally, in the client's imagination, rather than using real aversive stimuli like actual nausea-inducing drugs or electric shocks. A classic application is with problem drinking or unwanted sexual or eating behaviours: the therapist has the client vividly imagine engaging in the target behaviour and then imagine a strongly aversive outcome such as becoming violently nauseous and vomiting. Through repeated pairing, the behaviour itself begins to evoke the aversive feeling, so its appeal diminishes, an application of classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) to internal imagery. The distractors are all exposure-based anxiety treatments with a different aim, and distinguishing them is the exam skill: flooding is intense real exposure, implosion is intense imagined exposure, and systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe) is gradual exposure paired with relaxation. All three reduce fear through exposure, whereas covert sensitization reduces an unwanted behaviour by pairing it with an imagined aversive consequence. The cue words 'imagine' plus 'unpleasant consequence to reduce the behaviour' point to covert sensitization.","topic":"Covert sensitization"},{"q":"The chance of developing schizophrenia if you have an identical twin with the disorder is approximately:","options":{"a":"1%","b":"10%","c":"80%","d":"50%"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is approximately 50 percent. Schizophrenia has a strong genetic component, and twin studies provide the clearest evidence for it. The monozygotic (identical) twin concordance rate for schizophrenia is around 40 to 50 percent, meaning that if one identical twin has schizophrenia, the other has roughly a 50 percent chance of also developing it. This figure, drawn from classic twin research including work reviewed by Irving Gottesman, is far higher than the roughly 1 percent lifetime risk in the general population and higher than the roughly 17 percent concordance in dizygotic (fraternal) twins or about 9 to 13 percent risk in a sibling or child of an affected person. The crucial interpretive point is that because identical twins share 100 percent of their genes yet the concordance is only about 50 percent rather than 100 percent, genetics alone cannot fully explain the disorder. This supports the diathesis-stress model, in which a genetic vulnerability (diathesis) interacts with environmental stressors (such as prenatal factors, cannabis use, or psychosocial stress) to trigger onset. The distractors correspond to the general-population risk, first-degree relative risk, and an implausibly high near-deterministic figure. This item tests knowledge of twin concordance and the gene-environment interaction in schizophrenia.","topic":"Genetics of schizophrenia: twin concordance"},{"q":"Which of the following statements about the F-distribution is false?","options":{"a":"The distribution is asymmetrical.","b":"The distribution is one-tailed.","c":"Negative values of F are associated with a higher probability value.","d":"If the amount of between-cells variance is equal to the amount of within-subjects variance, the value of F will be 1.00."},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is that 'negative values of F are associated with a higher probability value' is FALSE, because F can never be negative. The F-distribution, named after Ronald Aylmer Fisher, is the sampling distribution of the ratio of two variance estimates (mean squares) in analysis of variance. Because it is a ratio of two variances, and variances are computed from squared deviations, both the numerator and denominator are always non-negative, so the F statistic itself can only range from 0 to positive infinity. Negative F values are mathematically impossible, which makes the statement about negative F values with higher probability simply false. The other three statements are all true properties of the F-distribution. It is asymmetrical, specifically positively skewed (skewed to the right), with the exact shape determined by two separate degrees-of-freedom parameters for the numerator and denominator. It is one-tailed in application: because larger between-groups variance relative to within-groups variance drives F upward, the region of rejection lies only in the upper tail. And when the between-groups (between-cells) variance equals the within-groups (within-subjects) variance, the ratio equals exactly 1.00, the value expected under the null hypothesis of no group differences. This item tests knowledge of the mathematical properties of the F-distribution.","topic":"F-distribution properties"},{"q":"Which of the following is most effective in conditioning?","options":{"a":"Backward Conditioning","b":"Delayed Conditioning","c":"Simultaneous Conditioning","d":"Trace Conditioning"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is delayed conditioning, which is generally the most effective arrangement for forming a strong conditioned response in classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov). The effectiveness of conditioning depends heavily on the temporal relationship between the conditioned stimulus (CS) and the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). In delayed conditioning, the CS is presented first and remains on (or its onset precedes the UCS) and overlaps with the UCS, so that the CS is still present when the UCS arrives. This arrangement is considered the most effective because the CS reliably signals and predicts the coming UCS while the two are paired in time, allowing the strongest associative link to form. Compare the alternatives along this timing dimension. In trace conditioning the CS begins and ends before the UCS appears, leaving a gap or trace in between, which weakens learning because the organism must hold the CS in memory. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS occur at exactly the same instant, so the CS has no predictive value (it does not warn of anything) and conditioning is weak. In backward conditioning the UCS comes before the CS, which is the least effective of all because the CS cannot predict an event that has already happened. The unifying principle is predictiveness: the CS must reliably precede and signal the UCS, and delayed conditioning maximises this signalling while preserving close temporal pairing, which is why it produces the most robust learning.","topic":"Classical conditioning: delayed vs trace timing"},{"q":"Construct validity is a notion that refers to:","options":{"a":"The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute.","b":"The level of which the theoretical construct is related to other similar measures","c":"The degree to which the items in the test consistently relate to each other","d":"The test will produce consistent results"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is the level to which the theoretical construct is related to other similar measures (that is, how well the test represents and behaves like the underlying theoretical construct). Construct validity is the most fundamental and overarching form of validity, formalised in the influential work of Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl. It concerns the degree to which a test truly measures the abstract theoretical construct (such as intelligence, anxiety, or self-esteem) that it is intended to measure, given that such constructs are hypothetical and cannot be observed directly. Construct validity is established by accumulating evidence that the test relates to other variables in ways predicted by theory. It has two key components: convergent validity, where the test correlates strongly with other measures of the same or related constructs (option A actually describes convergent validity specifically), and discriminant (divergent) validity, where the test does not correlate with measures of unrelated constructs. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske developed the multitrait-multimethod matrix to assess both together. Because option B captures the broad idea of how the construct relates to other similar measures, it is the best fit for the overall notion of construct validity, whereas option A is a subtype, and options C and D describe reliability (internal consistency and consistency of results). This item tests the definition of construct validity.","topic":"Construct validity"},{"q":"Some students experience an intense period of adaptation when they leave their old school environment and start college, especially during those initial days before they find companions, start to fit in and feel comfortable. This process is best described as:","options":{"a":"Looking-glass self","b":"Self-awareness","c":"Institutionalisation","d":"Re-socialisation"},"correct":"d","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is re-socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong sociological process by which individuals learn the norms, values, and behaviours of their society. Re-socialisation refers to the process of shedding former behaviour patterns and adopting new ones as part of a transition into a new social environment, role, or stage of life. When students leave a familiar school setting and enter college, they must unlearn old routines and learn the new expectations, culture, and social norms of the college, which is precisely re-socialisation. The concept is associated with sociologist Erving Goffman, who studied intense re-socialisation in total institutions, though it also applies to milder everyday transitions like starting college, a new job, or moving countries. The distractors reference other self and identity concepts: the looking-glass self from Charles Horton Cooley describes how we build self-image from others' perceived judgments, self-awareness is a state of self-focused attention, and institutionalisation is the loss of independence within a controlling institution. The item tests whether the student can distinguish the socialisation processes and correctly label adaptation to a new environment as re-socialisation.","topic":"Resocialisation"},{"q":"The cognitive strategy where an individual sets low expectations for their performance and experiences anxiety about a task, which paradoxically motivates them to prepare thoroughly to avoid failure, is BEST described as:","options":{"a":"Strategic Optimism","b":"Depressive Realism","c":"Defensive Pessimism","d":"Self-handicapping"},"correct":"c","section":"personality","explanation":"The answer is defensive pessimism, because setting low expectations and using the resulting anxiety as a spur to prepare thoroughly and avoid failure is exactly the cognitive strategy known as defensive pessimism. Understand the concept and how it contrasts with related strategies. Defensive pessimism was identified and studied by the psychologist Nancy Cantor and her colleague Julie Norem in the 1980s. It describes a strategy that some anxious but high-performing people use to manage their anxiety in achievement situations. Rather than trying to suppress their worry, defensive pessimists deliberately set unrealistically low expectations about how they will perform, expecting the worst, and then they engage in extensive mental rehearsal of everything that could go wrong. Crucially, this negative thinking is not paralyzing for them; it is motivating. By vividly imagining possible failures, they channel their anxiety into concrete preparation, planning for each contingency and working hard so that the feared bad outcome does not occur. Paradoxically, this pessimistic strategy leads to good performance, and importantly, if you force defensive pessimists to think positively or reassure them, their performance actually suffers, because you have removed the anxiety-management mechanism they depend on. Contrast this with strategic optimism, the opposite style where people set high expectations and stay calm, and with self-handicapping, where people sabotage their own preparation to have a ready excuse for failure. Defensive pessimism is adaptive and preparation-focused, whereas self-handicapping (Steven Berglas and Edward Jones, 1978) is self-defeating. The stem's combination of low expectations plus anxiety channeled into thorough preparation is the signature of defensive pessimism, a term from Norem and Cantor.","topic":"Defensive pessimism vs self-handicapping"},{"q":"The part of the brain responsible for producing enhanced memory for emotional or arousing events is:","options":{"a":"Hippocampus","b":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","c":"Amygdala","d":"Hypothalamus"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is the amygdala, the almond-shaped limbic structure that is critical for attaching emotional significance to events and for enhancing the consolidation of memories that occur during emotional arousal. The mechanism is well established: during emotionally charged or stressful experiences, the body releases stress hormones (adrenaline and cortisol) and the amygdala becomes highly active, and this amygdala activation modulates the hippocampus, which is the structure that actually forms new declarative memories. In effect the amygdala tells the hippocampus that an event matters and should be remembered strongly, producing the vivid, durable, often emotionally laden memories we associate with significant life events (sometimes called flashbulb memories). Joseph LeDoux's research mapped the amygdala's central role in fear processing and the fast subcortical pathway from thalamus to amygdala that allows rapid emotional appraisal. James McGaugh's work specifically demonstrated that the amygdala enhances memory consolidation for arousing material and that blocking adrenergic activity in the amygdala reduces this emotional memory advantage. The key distinction to learn is the division of labor in the limbic system: the hippocampus encodes and consolidates the factual content of memories, while the amygdala assigns emotional value and boosts the strength of memories formed under arousal. So when a question specifically links emotion or arousal to enhanced memory, the answer points to the amygdala rather than the hippocampus alone.","topic":"Amygdala and emotional memory"},{"q":"According to Erik Erikson's psychosocial development theory, the virtue of \"purpose\" emerges from the successful resolution of which developmental crisis?","options":{"a":"Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt","b":"Initiative vs. Guilt","c":"Industry vs. Inferiority","d":"Identity vs. Role Confusion"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The answer is Initiative vs. Guilt because in Erik Erikson's eight-stage theory each psychosocial crisis, when resolved successfully, produces a specific 'virtue' or ego strength, and the virtue of purpose is the one that emerges from the Initiative vs. Guilt stage. This stage occurs during the preschool or play age, roughly ages three to six years. In it, the child becomes increasingly capable of initiating activities, making plans, asserting power over their environment through play, questioning, and taking on tasks and goals of their own devising. If caregivers encourage this budding initiative and allow the child to make choices and lead activities, the child develops a sense of initiative and gains the virtue of purpose, the confidence to pursue goals without excessive fear. If, instead, the child's efforts are constantly criticised, controlled, or made to feel like an intrusion, the child develops guilt about their needs and desires and becomes inhibited. The wider exam skill is memorising Erikson's stage-to-virtue pairings, since these are frequently tested: Trust vs. Mistrust yields hope, Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt yields will, Initiative vs. Guilt yields purpose, Industry vs. Inferiority yields competence, Identity vs. Role Confusion yields fidelity, Intimacy vs. Isolation yields love, Generativity vs. Stagnation yields care, and Integrity vs. Despair yields wisdom. Purpose maps to Initiative vs. Guilt.","topic":"Erikson's stages and virtues"},{"q":"In the context of psychopathology, a \"formulation\" differs from a \"diagnosis\" in that a formulation primarily aims to:","options":{"a":"Classify the client's symptoms according to a standardized nosological system like DSM-5.","b":"Provide a hypothesis about the cause, precipitants, and maintaining factors of an individual's difficulties.","c":"Determine the prognosis and likely course of the disorder over time.","d":"Rule out organic causes for the presenting psychological symptoms."},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is that a formulation provides a hypothesis about the cause, precipitants, and maintaining factors of an individual's difficulties, because that individualized explanatory function is precisely what separates formulation from diagnosis. Understand the distinction from first principles, since it sits at the heart of clinical psychology practice. A diagnosis is a category. It takes a person's presenting problems and matches them to a labeled disorder in a nosological system such as the DSM-5 or ICD-11; its strengths are shared language, communication, and access to treatment guidelines, but it is descriptive and generic, telling you what to call the problem, not why this person has it. A formulation is a personalized explanatory story. Drawing on a theoretical model, often the cognitive-behavioral framework associated with Aaron Beck or, in the UK tradition, the biopsychosocial and the five-Ps approach, the clinician hypothesizes how the difficulties arose and persist. Clinicians frequently organize it around predisposing factors (vulnerabilities such as temperament or early adversity), precipitating factors (the triggers or recent stressors), perpetuating or maintaining factors (the cycles like avoidance or safety behaviors that keep the problem alive), and protective factors (strengths and resources). Because a formulation is testable and revisable, it directly guides which interventions to choose and can be shared collaboratively with the client. Two people with the identical diagnosis will have quite different formulations. The exam signal is any wording about causes, triggers, or maintaining factors, which always points to formulation, not diagnosis.","topic":"Case formulation vs diagnosis"},{"q":"According to the DSM-5-TR, which of the following is NOT a specifier for a manic episode?","options":{"a":"With Mixed Features","b":"With Psychotic Features","c":"With Anxious Distress","d":"With Limited Prosocial Emotions"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"The answer is 'with limited prosocial emotions,' because that specifier belongs to Conduct Disorder, not to a manic episode. This is a cross-category recognition question, and the way to master it is to know where each specifier actually lives in the DSM-5. A specifier is an extension attached to a diagnosis to add clinically important detail. For mood episodes, including manic episodes in bipolar disorder, the DSM-5 offers a specific menu of specifiers: with anxious distress, with mixed features, with rapid cycling, with melancholic features, with atypical features, with mood-congruent or mood-incongruent psychotic features, with catatonia, with peripartum onset, and with seasonal pattern. Three of the four options here, mixed features, psychotic features, and anxious distress, sit squarely on that mood-episode menu. The odd one out, with limited prosocial emotions, comes from an entirely different part of the manual. It is the specifier for Conduct Disorder, used to identify the callous-unemotional presentation, marked by lack of remorse or guilt, shallow affect, callous lack of empathy, and unconcern about performance. This subgroup is theoretically important because it flags youths at higher risk for later psychopathy and is linked to research by Paul Frick and others on callous-unemotional traits. The exam trap is that all four options sound clinical and plausible, but only one is misfiled: limited prosocial emotions is a conduct-disorder specifier, so it is the correct answer to a question asking which is NOT a manic-episode specifier.","topic":"DSM-5-TR manic episode specifiers"},{"q":"In the analysis of variance the significance level indicates","options":{"a":"The probability of making Type I error","b":"That F is correct","c":"The probability of making Type II error","d":"(A) and (B)"},"correct":"a","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is the probability of making a Type I error. In hypothesis testing, including the analysis of variance, the significance level, denoted alpha, is the threshold probability that the researcher sets in advance (commonly 0.05) for rejecting the null hypothesis. It represents the probability of making a Type I error, which is rejecting a true null hypothesis, in other words concluding that there is a real effect or group difference when in fact none exists (a false positive). This framework of controlling error rates was developed by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, who formalised the distinction between two kinds of error. A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: wrongly rejecting a true null hypothesis. A Type II error (beta) is a false negative: failing to reject a false null hypothesis, that is, missing a real effect. The power of a test equals 1 minus beta. Setting alpha at 0.05 means the researcher accepts a 5 percent risk of a Type I error. So in ANOVA, when we test the F ratio against a critical value at a chosen significance level, that level tells us the probability of a Type I error we are willing to tolerate. Option C confuses alpha with beta, and option B is not a coherent statistical statement. This item tests understanding of the significance level as the Type I error rate.","topic":"Type I error and significance level"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jss-mock-2": [{"q":"According to Albert Bandura, which of the following is NOT a necessary component process for observational learning to occur?","concept":"The answer is reinforcement, because in Albert Bandura's theory of observational learning the four necessary component processes are attention, retention, reproduction, and motivation, and direct reinforcement is not among the requirements for learning to occur. Understand Bandura's contribution and why this distinction matters. Bandura, famous for the Bobo doll experiments of the early 1960s, showed that children could acquire novel aggressive behaviors simply by watching an adult model, without ever being reinforced themselves, which challenged the strict behaviorist claim that learning requires direct reinforcement of the learner. His social learning theory, later social cognitive theory, specified four mediational processes that must operate for observational learning to succeed. Attention: the observer must notice and attend to the model's behavior, influenced by the model's salience, status, and attractiveness. Retention: the observer must encode and store a memory representation of the behavior, often symbolically. Reproduction, also called motor reproduction: the observer must have the physical and skill capacity to reproduce the modeled action. Motivation: the observer must have a reason to enact the behavior, and here is where reinforcement enters, but in a specific way. Bandura introduced vicarious reinforcement, meaning we are more likely to imitate a model we have seen rewarded and less likely to imitate one we have seen punished; reinforcement thus influences performance and motivation, not the acquisition of the learning itself. Because the behavior can be learned without any reinforcement, reinforcement is not one of the four required processes, making it the correct exception.","traps":{"a":"Attention is genuinely one of Bandura's four required processes; the observer must attend to the model's behavior to learn it, so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Motivation is one of the four necessary processes; without motivation the observer may have learned the behavior but will not perform it, so it belongs to the model.","d":"Reproduction (motor reproduction) is one of the four processes, the physical capacity to reproduce the observed action, so it is required rather than excluded."}},{"q":"Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is based on which technique of psychological testing?","concept":"The correct answer is construction technique. Projective tests are commonly grouped by the type of response task they require, a classification associated with Lawrence Frank, who coined the term projective and with Bruno Klopfer and others. The main categories are association techniques (respond with the first associations to a stimulus, as in the Rorschach Inkblot Test of Hermann Rorschach and word-association tests), construction techniques (build or create a product such as a story from stimulus material), completion techniques (finish incomplete stimuli, as in sentence-completion tests), choice or ordering techniques (arrange or select items), and expressive techniques (express the self through free activity such as drawing, painting, or play, as in the Draw-a-Person test (Florence Goodenough, 1926) and play therapy). The Thematic Apperception Test, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard, presents ambiguous pictures and asks the examinee to construct a full story about each: what is happening, what led up to it, what the characters are thinking and feeling, and how it will end. Because the subject must build a complete narrative from the ambiguous scene, the TAT is a construction technique, making option b correct. It is interpreted in terms of Murray's needs and presses and the projected themes revealing the examinee's motives and conflicts.","traps":{"a":"Expressive techniques involve free activity like drawing or play (for example the Draw-a-Person test), not story construction.","c":"Association techniques ask for the first response to a stimulus, as in the Rorschach or word association, not narrative building.","d":"Completion techniques ask the subject to finish incomplete material like sentences, not to construct a full story."}},{"q":"Tim would like to participate in his high school's musical. He has been taking vocal lessons for years and is an excellent singer, but does not have much experience as a dancer. The director of the play decides to give him a part where he has to sing and dance. Based on the theory of social facilitation, how will the presence of an audience affect Tim's performance in the play?","concept":"The correct answer is that his singing will improve, but his dancing will be impaired. Social facilitation refers to the effect of the presence of others on task performance, first noted by Norman Triplett and later refined by Robert Zajonc. Zajonc resolved seemingly contradictory findings with a key principle: the presence of others increases physiological arousal, and heightened arousal strengthens the dominant response, the response most likely to occur for a given task. For tasks that are simple, well-learned, or well-practised, the dominant response is the correct one, so an audience enhances performance. For tasks that are complex, novel, or poorly learned, the dominant response is often incorrect, so an audience impairs performance. Applying this to Tim: singing is a skill he has practised for years, so it is well-learned and the dominant response is correct, meaning the audience's arousal will enhance his singing. Dancing, however, is a skill in which he has little experience, so it is poorly learned and the dominant response is likely to be an error, meaning the audience will impair his dancing. This is why performing arts differ for experts (helped by crowds) versus novices (hurt by them). The distractors reverse or overgeneralise the effect. This item tests the dominant-response logic of social facilitation.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the effect: an audience impairs the poorly learned task (dancing) and enhances the well-learned one (singing), not the other way around.","b":"An audience only enhances well-practised, dominant responses; Tim's weak dancing is not well learned, so it would be impaired, not improved.","d":"Singing is Tim's well-learned skill, so the audience should enhance it rather than impair it, making a blanket impairment incorrect."}},{"q":"A patient who describes a particularly gruesome thought or fantasy but denies any feelings of anxiety or disgust associated with it is using which of the following Ego's defense:","concept":"A patient who describes a gruesome thought while denying any associated feeling is using the defence of isolation, so the answer is c. Isolation (more fully, isolation of affect) is an ego defence mechanism in which the emotional charge attached to a thought, memory, or impulse is split off and removed from awareness, so that the idea remains conscious but is stripped of the feeling that should accompany it. The person can talk about something disturbing, a violent fantasy, a traumatic event, in a flat, detached, intellectualised way, as if it carried no emotional significance. This protects the person from the anxiety that the affect would arouse while still allowing the cognitive content into consciousness. Isolation is particularly characteristic of obsessive compulsive personality and obsessional neurosis, where thinking is over invested and feeling is kept at a distance, and it is closely related to intellectualisation. The exam discriminator is the precise pattern described: the content (the gruesome thought) is fully present and reported, but the affect (anxiety, disgust) that belongs with it is absent. That specific separation of idea from feeling is the signature of isolation, as opposed to denial (where the content itself is rejected), rationalisation (where false reasons justify it), or displacement (where the feeling is redirected to another object).","traps":{"a":"Denial rejects the reality of the disturbing content altogether, but here the patient fully acknowledges and describes the thought; only the affect is missing.","b":"Rationalisation invents plausible but false justifications for an act or feeling; the patient is not justifying anything, merely reporting a thought without emotion.","d":"Displacement redirects an emotion from its real target onto a safer substitute; here the affect is removed entirely, not transferred elsewhere."}},{"q":"A patient presents with an inability to perform coordinated, purposeful movements, despite having intact motor function, sensation, and comprehension of the task. When asked to brush their hair, they cannot sequence the movements. This deficit is best described as:","concept":"The answer is apraxia, because the inability to carry out coordinated, purposeful, learned movements despite intact motor power, sensation, coordination, and comprehension is the defining feature of apraxia. Understand the concept precisely, because it is defined by exclusion of simpler explanations. The word comes from Greek roots meaning without action. Apraxia is a disorder of the higher-order planning and programming of skilled movement, so the patient knows what they want to do and has the physical capacity to move, yet cannot organize the movements into the correct sequence to accomplish the intended act. In the vignette, the person cannot sequence the movements to brush their hair even though their arm is strong, their sensation is normal, and they understand the request; this dissociation between preserved elementary functions and lost skilled action is the hallmark. Neurologically, apraxia typically results from damage to the association areas involved in motor planning, especially the left parietal lobe and its connections to premotor and frontal regions, which store the learned motor programs (sometimes called praxicons). Clinicians distinguish subtypes such as ideomotor apraxia (impaired ability to perform a gesture on command despite knowing the concept) and ideational apraxia (impaired ability to sequence a multistep action). Contrast apraxia with its neighbors that examiners bundle together: agnosia is impaired recognition, ataxia is impaired coordination from cerebellar damage, and alexia is impaired reading. Because the deficit here is specifically the inability to execute purposeful movement sequences with everything else intact, the answer is apraxia.","traps":{"a":"Agnosia is a failure of recognition, the inability to identify objects, faces, or sounds despite intact sensory input, not a failure to execute learned movements.","b":"Ataxia is a loss of coordination and balance due to cerebellar dysfunction, producing clumsy, unsteady, imprecise movements, but the underlying motor and sensory pathways for sequencing purposeful acts are disrupted differently than in the deficit described.","d":"Alexia is an acquired inability to read despite intact vision and prior literacy, a specific language deficit, not a disorder of executing skilled motor sequences."}},{"q":"A 5-year-old child is asked to sort a deck of cards. The cards can be sorted by color (red/black) or by suit (hearts, spades, etc.). The child successfully sorts by color but cannot then re-sort the same deck by suit. This inflexibility is an example of:","concept":"The answer is centration, because the child's inability to abandon color and re-sort the same cards by suit is a textbook demonstration of centration, the tendency to focus on a single salient dimension of a situation while ignoring all others. Understand this within Jean Piaget's account of preoperational thinking, roughly ages two to seven. Centration is one of the central limitations of this stage, and it explains many of the child's characteristic errors. Because the preoperational child can attend to only one feature at a time, they cannot coordinate multiple dimensions simultaneously, which is why they fail conservation tasks (fixating only on the height of liquid, ignoring width) and why, in this card task, having centered on the color dimension, the child cannot mentally step back and reconceive the same cards along a different dimension, suit. This rigidity, the inability to flexibly shift the basis of classification, is precisely what centration produces. Cognitive scientists would today describe the underlying skill the child lacks as set-shifting or cognitive flexibility, an executive function, and tasks like the Dimensional Change Card Sort, developed by Philip Zelazo, formalize exactly this scenario to study its development. Overcoming centration, learning to decenter and consider several dimensions at once, is a key achievement of the concrete operational stage. The distractors all name other genuine preoperational features, egocentrism, animism, and transductive reasoning, but the specific error of fixating on one dimension and being unable to switch is centration, making it the correct answer.","traps":{"a":"Egocentrism is the preoperational child's inability to take another person's perspective, a social-cognitive limitation, whereas failing to re-sort cards is about fixating on one perceptual dimension, not perspective-taking.","b":"Animism is the preoperational tendency to attribute life and intentions to inanimate objects, such as thinking the sun is alive, which has nothing to do with the card-sorting inflexibility described.","d":"Transductive reasoning is the preoperational habit of reasoning from one specific instance to another and inferring false cause-effect links, not the inability to shift sorting dimensions shown here."}},{"q":"A patient sees a friend in the street who fails to acknowledge him; he thinks his friend does not like him anymore. He has the following type of cognitive error:","concept":"Concluding that a friend dislikes him because the friend failed to greet him is arbitrary inference, so the answer is b. Arbitrary inference is one of the cognitive distortions identified by Aaron Beck in his cognitive model of depression and emotional disorders. It means drawing a specific negative conclusion in the absence of supporting evidence, or even in the face of contrary evidence; the person jumps to a conclusion that is arbitrary because nothing actually justifies it. In the example, the friend's failure to acknowledge him could have countless innocent explanations, the friend was distracted, did not see him, was preoccupied, yet the patient leaps to the unfounded negative conclusion that the friend no longer likes him. There is no evidence for that interpretation; it is simply assumed. This is the essence of arbitrary inference. It is worth distinguishing it from the neighbouring distortions: selective abstraction focuses on one detail out of context while ignoring the whole, magnification exaggerates the importance of an event, and personalisation attributes external events to oneself without basis. While the example does involve relating the event to the self, the most precise label for jumping to a negative conclusion with no supporting evidence is arbitrary inference. The exam discriminator is the absence of evidence for the conclusion, which is the defining feature of arbitrary inference in Beck's taxonomy.","traps":{"a":"Selective abstraction means focusing on a single negative detail taken out of context while ignoring the broader picture, not jumping to a conclusion without evidence.","c":"Personalisation is attributing external events to oneself without justification; the keyed distortion here is the evidence free negative conclusion, which is arbitrary inference.","d":"Magnification exaggerates the significance or severity of an event; it is not the act of drawing an unsupported conclusion from a neutral cue."}},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy begins to have new episodes of temper outbursts that are out of proportion to the situation. Which of the following is NOT a diagnostic possibility in this patient?","concept":"The correct answer is Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD). DMDD was introduced in the DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association to capture children with chronic, severe, persistent irritability and frequent temper outbursts, partly to reduce over-diagnosis of pediatric bipolar disorder. A critical diagnostic rule is the age criterion: DMDD may be diagnosed only when the onset of symptoms is before age 10, and it should not be diagnosed for the first time before age 6 or after age 18. In this vignette the outbursts are described as new in a 12-year-old, meaning onset is after the age-10 cutoff, so DMDD is specifically excluded. That is why it is the one diagnosis that is NOT a possibility, whereas Bipolar Disorder, Conduct Disorder, and ADHD all remain on the differential for a 12-year-old presenting with disproportionate temper outbursts. The item tests careful attention to DMDD's onset-age requirement, a detail that reliably distinguishes it from the other externalizing and mood conditions with which it shares surface features.","traps":{"b":"Bipolar disorder can present with irritability and outbursts and remains a diagnostic possibility, so it is not the excluded answer.","c":"Conduct disorder features aggression and rule violation, so temper outbursts fit within its possibilities.","d":"ADHD commonly co-occurs with emotional dysregulation and outbursts, so it stays on the differential."}},{"q":"A researcher is interested in the impact of anxiety on performance. To manipulate anxiety, some subjects were given plain chips to eat while others were given barbecue flavoured chips to eat. The most obvious problem with this experiment is a lack of :","concept":"The correct answer is construct validity. Construct validity is the degree to which an operational manipulation or measure actually captures the theoretical construct it is meant to represent, a concept formalized by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl. In this study the construct of interest is anxiety, but the independent variable, giving subjects plain versus barbecue-flavoured chips, has no plausible connection to inducing anxiety at all. Flavour of a snack is not a valid operationalization of an anxiety manipulation; whatever effect appears cannot be interpreted as an effect of anxiety because anxiety was never meaningfully varied. This is a failure of construct validity: the manipulation does not embody the construct named in the hypothesis. It is worth distinguishing this from internal validity, the confidence that the manipulation rather than a confound caused the outcome, and from external validity, the generalizability of results. Here the flaw is more fundamental than a confound: the manipulation is simply not a measure of anxiety. Because the operationalization of the independent variable does not correspond to the intended psychological construct, the study most obviously lacks construct validity.","traps":{"a":"Statistical validity concerns the appropriateness of statistical tests and inferences, not whether the manipulation captures the intended concept.","b":"Empirical validity is not the standard label for this flaw, and it does not name the operationalization problem.","c":"Internal validity concerns whether the manipulation, not confounds, caused the outcome; here the deeper issue is that the manipulation does not represent anxiety at all."}},{"q":"Which of the following secondary tasks is thought to affect the central executive in terms of working memory?","concept":"The correct answer is random number generation. The working memory model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposes a central executive that controls attention and coordinates two subsidiary slave systems: the phonological loop, which holds and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, and the visuospatial sketchpad, which handles visual and spatial material (a later addition was the episodic buffer). To identify which task loads which component, researchers use interfering secondary tasks. Articulatory suppression, continually repeating a word, occupies the phonological loop; the irrelevant speech effect, listening to unattended speech, also disrupts the phonological loop; remembering a spatial pattern taxes the visuospatial sketchpad. Random number (or random letter) generation, by contrast, requires continuous attentional control: one must suppress habitual counting sequences, monitor recent outputs, and flexibly switch to avoid patterns, which are exactly the supervisory attention functions of the central executive. Baddeley used random generation precisely as a way to load and study the central executive, showing that it becomes less random when a person must simultaneously perform another demanding executive task. So among these options, random number generation is the task that specifically engages and impairs the central executive rather than a slave subsystem.","traps":{"a":"Continually repeating a word (articulatory suppression) loads the phonological loop, not the central executive.","b":"Remembering a spatial configuration taxes the visuospatial sketchpad, a slave system, not the central executive.","c":"Listening to irrelevant speech intrudes on the phonological loop via the irrelevant speech effect, not the central executive."}},{"q":"Which of the following is an association technique of psychological testing?","concept":"The correct answer is the Rorschach inkblot test. Projective techniques are commonly classified by the response task they demand, a scheme associated with Lawrence Frank. Association techniques require the respondent to react to a stimulus with the first thing that comes to mind, such as an association, image, or perception. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by Hermann Rorschach, is the prototypical association technique: the examinee is shown ambiguous inkblots and asked what each might be, giving their spontaneous associations to the blot. Word-association tests are another example. The other options each represent a different category. The sentence completion test is a completion technique, in which the person finishes incomplete sentence stems (for example, My greatest fear is...). The Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan is a construction technique, requiring the examinee to construct a full narrative story about ambiguous pictures. The Draw-A-Person test of Karen Machover is an expressive technique, in which the individual expresses themselves through the free activity of drawing. Because only the Rorschach asks for immediate associations to a stimulus, it is the association technique among these, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"The sentence completion test is a completion technique, in which the subject finishes incomplete stems.","c":"The Thematic Apperception Test is a construction technique, requiring the subject to build a story.","d":"The Draw-A-Person test is an expressive technique, in which the self is expressed through free drawing."}},{"q":"In which of the following scenarios would it be most difficult to predict behaviour from attitudes?","concept":"The correct answer is when the attitude is formed by indirect experience. Research on attitude-behaviour consistency, notably by Russell Fazio and by Icek Ajzen and Martin Fishbein, shows that attitudes predict behaviour best when they are strong, accessible, and personally significant. Several moderators strengthen the attitude-behaviour link: high personal relevance or vested interest, extensive knowledge about the attitude object, and especially attitudes formed through direct personal experience rather than hearsay. Regina Fazio Regan and Russell Fazio demonstrated that direct experience makes attitudes more accessible in memory and thus more predictive of action. Conversely, attitudes formed indirectly, through secondhand information, media, or others' reports rather than firsthand contact, tend to be weaker, less certain, and less accessible, so they are the poorest predictors of actual behaviour. Therefore the scenario in which prediction is most difficult is the indirect-experience one. The three distractors all describe conditions that increase attitude strength and accessibility, which is why they make behaviour easier to predict, exactly the opposite of what the question asks. This item tests knowledge of the moderators of attitude-behaviour consistency and the special role of direct experience.","traps":{"a":"High personal relevance strengthens the attitude-behaviour link, making behaviour easier, not harder, to predict from attitude.","c":"Greater knowledge about the attitude object produces a stronger, more accessible attitude that better predicts behaviour.","d":"Attitudes formed through direct experience are more strongly held and accessible, so they predict behaviour more reliably, not less."}},{"q":"Self-report ratings of one's characteristics, attributes, and interests is placed under :","concept":"The correct answer is Q-data. Raymond Cattell, the personality theorist who used factor analysis to derive his sixteen source traits (the 16 Personality Factor Questionnaire), proposed that personality can be studied through three main sources of data, which he labelled to ensure that trait findings converge across methods. L-data (life-record data) consists of information gathered from a person's actual life history and everyday behaviour, including ratings made by observers who know the person and objective records such as school grades or work performance. Q-data (questionnaire data) consists of information obtained from the person's own self-report on questionnaires and rating scales, that is, the individual's own ratings of their characteristics, attributes, interests, attitudes, and feelings. T-data (objective test data) consists of measurements taken from objective, often disguised, laboratory tests and behavioural situations in which the person does not know what dimension is being assessed, reducing the possibility of faking. Because self-report ratings, in which a person rates their own traits and interests, fall squarely into the questionnaire category, they are classified as Q-data, making option d correct. The distractors Z-data and P-data are not part of Cattell's standard L-Q-T classification.","traps":{"a":"L-data (life-record data) comes from real-life records and observer ratings, not self-report.","b":"Z-data is not part of Cattell's classification and serves as a distractor.","c":"P-data is not one of the three standard Cattellian data sources in this scheme."}},{"q":"A scientist is studying brain function in rats and destroys the animal's ventromedial hypothalamus. Which of the following is most likely to occur?","concept":"The correct answer is that the rat will eat excessive amounts of food. The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) functions as a satiety centre: it signals when the animal has eaten enough and should stop. When it is destroyed, that 'stop eating' signal is lost, producing hyperphagia and a syndrome of gross obesity, a finding demonstrated in classic lesion studies associated with researchers such as Albert Fisher and later dual-centre models. This contrasts with the lateral hypothalamus (LH), which acts as a hunger or feeding-initiation centre; lesioning the LH produces aphagia (refusal to eat) and can be fatal without force-feeding. This dual-centre hypothalamic model of hunger, one centre driving eating and one inhibiting it, is a foundational piece of biopsychology and physiological psychology. The item tests whether the student can pair the correct nucleus with the correct behavioural outcome. The key memory hook is: Ventromedial equals Very full (satiety, so lesion causes overeating), while Lateral equals Lack of eating when lesioned. Thirst and drinking are governed by separate mechanisms, so the water-related distractors are designed to mislead students who confuse feeding and drinking regulation.","traps":{"a":"Drinking behaviour is regulated by the lateral hypothalamus and osmoreceptors, not the ventromedial nucleus; lesioning the VMH does not cause polydipsia.","c":"Refusal to drink is not the classic VMH result; this describes an aphagia/adipsia pattern tied to lateral hypothalamic damage.","d":"Refusal to eat (aphagia) follows destruction of the lateral hypothalamus, which is the hunger 'on' centre, the opposite structure to the one lesioned here."}},{"q":"Formal operational reasoning involves all the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The correct answer (the EXCEPT) is dialectical reasoning. In Jean Piaget's theory, the formal operational stage begins around age eleven or twelve and represents the capacity for abstract, systematic, logical thought. Its hallmarks include hypothetical-deductive reasoning (generating and systematically testing hypotheses, as in the pendulum problem), counterfactual reasoning (reasoning about possibilities and states contrary to present fact), abstract and propositional thinking, and increased metacognition (the ability to reflect on and reason about one's own thought processes). Dialectical reasoning, however, is generally considered a form of postformal thought, said to develop, if at all, in adulthood beyond Piaget's final stage. Dialectical thinking involves recognizing that knowledge is relative and context-bound, tolerating contradiction, and integrating opposing ideas (thesis and antithesis) into a synthesis, an idea rooted in the philosophy of Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel and elaborated in adult-development theories by researchers such as Klaus Riegel and Michael Basseches. Because dialectical reasoning belongs to postformal rather than formal operational cognition, it is the element that does not fit Piaget's formal operational stage, making it the correct exception among the four options.","traps":{"a":"Hypothetical-deductive reasoning is a defining feature of the formal operational stage, so it is included.","b":"Counterfactual reasoning about what could be, contrary to fact, is characteristic of formal operations.","d":"Metacognitive reasoning, thinking about one's own thinking, emerges in the formal operational stage."}},{"q":"Gregory Bateson describes a classic example of the interaction between a mother and her son who had fairly well recovered from an acute schizophrenic episode. Bateson described this interaction as follows: '[The son] was glad to see her [his mother] and impulsively put his arm around her shoulders, whereupon she stiffened. He withdrew his arm and she asked, Don't you love me anymore? He then blushed, and she said, Dear, you must not be so easily embarrassed and afraid of your feelings. The patient was able to stay with her only a few minutes more, and following her departure, he assaulted an aide and was put in the isolation room.' Bateson used this interaction to illustrate which ONE of the following concepts?","concept":"The correct answer is the symptomatic double bind. Gregory Bateson, together with colleagues Don Jackson, Jay Haley, and John Weakland, proposed the double bind theory in 1956 as an early communicational and systemic account of schizophrenia. A double bind is a situation in which a person repeatedly receives contradictory messages on different levels of communication, cannot escape or step outside the situation (cannot leave the field), and cannot comment on or resolve the contradiction, leaving them in an impossible, no-win position. In the classic example quoted, the mother sends contradictory messages: she stiffens and withdraws when her son shows affection (a nonverbal 'do not be close to me'), yet then verbally reproaches him with 'Don't you love me anymore?' and criticises him for being embarrassed. Whatever the son does, express affection or withdraw, he is in the wrong, and he cannot comment on the paradox or escape the relationship. Bateson argued that chronic exposure to such double binds could contribute to the disordered communication and thinking seen in schizophrenia. Though the theory is no longer accepted as a cause of schizophrenia, it was historically important in the development of family systems therapy. The distractors reference other systemic concepts (cybernetics, homeostasis) or a non-standard term. This item tests recognition of Bateson's double bind.","traps":{"a":"The cybernetic system refers to feedback and self-regulation in systems theory, not to the contradictory-message trap described in the vignette.","b":"The homeostatic system describes a family's tendency to maintain stability and resist change, not the specific contradictory-communication pattern shown here.","c":"'The incongruent family dyad' is not the specific term Bateson coined; the phenomenon of contradictory messages that trap the recipient is the double bind."}},{"q":"In which of the following situations would a diagnosis of \"global developmental delay\" be inappropriate?","concept":"The correct answer is the 7-year-old boy with a full-scale IQ of 65 and severe adaptive impairment. In the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, Global Developmental Delay is a provisional diagnosis reserved for children under age 5 who fail to meet expected developmental milestones in several areas but who cannot yet be reliably assessed by standardized testing, either because they are too young or too impaired to complete valid measures. It is explicitly a placeholder that requires reassessment later. The diagnosis is inappropriate for the 7-year-old because he is old enough for, and has already undergone, valid standardized testing that yields a clear IQ of 65 together with documented severe adaptive impairment. That combination meets the full criteria for Intellectual Disability (Intellectual Developmental Disorder), so the definitive diagnosis should be made rather than the provisional Global Developmental Delay label. The other three scenarios each involve missing or unobtainable assessment data, precisely the circumstances the provisional category was designed for. The item tests understanding that Global Developmental Delay is an under-5, cannot-yet-assess placeholder, not a substitute when full diagnostic data already exist.","traps":{"a":"Being too young to complete valid assessments is a core reason the provisional global developmental delay category exists, so it is appropriate here.","c":"Insufficient adaptive-functioning information is exactly a case where the provisional label is used pending fuller evaluation.","d":"Missing standardized cognitive data is another appropriate reason to apply the provisional diagnosis rather than a full intellectual disability diagnosis."}},{"q":"A researcher wants to study the prevalence of test anxiety among all school students in India. Due to feasibility, they first randomly select 5 districts from each state, then randomly select 10 schools from each district, and finally randomly select 30 students from each school. This sampling technique is known as:","concept":"The answer is cluster sampling, specifically its multistage form, because the researcher is randomly selecting intact groups (clusters) in successive nested stages rather than sampling individuals directly from a national list. Understand the core logic from scratch: when a population is enormous and geographically scattered, like all school students in India, there is no practical sampling frame listing every student, and visiting randomly scattered individuals across the country would be ruinously expensive. Cluster sampling solves this by exploiting the fact that people are naturally grouped: students sit inside schools, schools sit inside districts, districts sit inside states. The researcher randomly selects clusters at each level, first districts, then schools within the chosen districts, then students within the chosen schools, which is why this is called multistage cluster sampling. Every stage uses random selection, so it remains a probability method and sampling error can still be estimated, though it is typically larger than for simple random sampling because students in the same school resemble each other. The crucial contrast with stratified sampling, which examiners exploit relentlessly, is this: in stratified sampling you sample from every subgroup to guarantee representation, whereas in cluster sampling you randomly choose only some clusters and ignore the rest, purely for feasibility and cost. Nested random stages of naturally occurring units always signal cluster sampling.","traps":{"a":"Stratified random sampling divides the population into meaningful subgroups (strata) and samples individuals from every single stratum, whereas here whole districts and schools are randomly selected as intact units and many are left out entirely.","b":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method in which the researcher fills predetermined quotas using convenience or judgment, with no random selection at any stage, which contradicts the repeated random selection described.","d":"Purposive sampling means deliberately hand-picking participants the researcher judges to be informative, a non-probability approach with no randomness and no nested stages."}},{"q":"Sheena is studying for her final exam in her psychology class. For every 45 minutes that she studies, she gives herself a mini chocolate, then goes back to studying. What Schedule of reinforcement is Sheena using?","concept":"The correct answer is a fixed interval schedule. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (B.F. Skinner) described four partial reinforcement schedules built from two dimensions: ratio versus interval (whether reinforcement depends on number of responses or passage of time) and fixed versus variable (whether the requirement is constant or changes). Sheena rewards herself after a constant, predictable amount of time (every 45 minutes) regardless of how much work she does in that window, which is the defining feature of a fixed interval schedule. The classic signature of fixed interval reinforcement is the scalloped response curve: behaviour slows just after reinforcement and accelerates as the next reward time approaches, because the learner can anticipate when the reward is due. Ratio schedules (fixed or variable) instead reward a count of responses and produce higher, steadier response rates. Variable schedules resist extinction best because the unpredictability keeps responding high. Recognising these four schedules is core operant-conditioning content, and the trick in this item is to notice the cue word 'minutes,' which signals an interval schedule, combined with the constant 45-minute gap, which signals fixed rather than variable.","traps":{"a":"Variable ratio reinforces after an unpredictable number of responses (like a slot machine). Sheena is timing herself, not counting responses, so it is not ratio and not variable.","b":"Variable interval reinforces after unpredictable time gaps. Her interval is fixed at exactly 45 minutes, so it is not variable.","d":"Fixed ratio reinforces after a set number of responses, not after a set amount of time; Sheena rewards elapsed time, not a response count."}},{"q":"Odd-even method of splitting the test to estimate split-half reliability is MOST appropriate for :","concept":"The correct answer is power tests. Split-half reliability estimates internal consistency by dividing a test into two halves, scoring each, and correlating the two half-scores (then usually correcting with the Spearman-Brown formula, named after Charles Spearman and William Brown). The odd-even method, assigning odd-numbered items to one half and even-numbered items to the other, is a common way to create equivalent halves. This procedure is appropriate for power tests, in which items vary in difficulty, there is generous or no time limit, and the score reflects how many problems a person can solve rather than how fast. In a power test, examinees attempt all or most items, so both halves contain comparable numbers of attempted items and the correlation is meaningful. Split-half reliability is specifically inappropriate for speed tests, in which items are easy and the score depends on how many items a person completes within a strict time limit. In a pure speed test, a person tends to get all attempted items right and simply stops where time runs out, so odd and even halves would each contain the same number of correct items for everyone, artificially inflating the correlation to near unity. Because the odd-even split-half method validly estimates reliability only when speed is not the limiting factor, it is most appropriate for power tests, making option c correct.","traps":{"a":"Projective tests yield qualitative, non-item-scored responses, making split-half correlation of item halves inappropriate.","b":"Neuropsychological tests are heterogeneous batteries, not the single homogeneous scale split-half assumes.","d":"Split-half reliability is specifically inappropriate for speed tests because performance depends on how many items are reached, not accuracy."}},{"q":"According to Cialdini, techniques for gaining compliance rest to a large extent on six basic principles. These do NOT include:","concept":"The correct answer is reward. The social psychologist Robert Cialdini, in his influential work on persuasion and compliance, identified six basic principles that underlie most compliance-gaining techniques: reciprocity (feeling obligated to return favors), commitment and consistency (aligning behavior with prior commitments, exploited by the foot-in-the-door technique (Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser, 1966)), social validation or social proof (looking to others' behavior to guide our own), liking or friendship (complying more readily with people we like or find attractive), authority (deferring to legitimate or expert authority figures), and scarcity (valuing things more when they are rare or dwindling in availability). Reward, while central to reinforcement theory and operant conditioning in the tradition of B. F. Skinner, is not one of Cialdini's six compliance principles as such. Although reciprocity involves an exchange of benefits, Cialdini's framework is about the psychological triggers of social influence rather than the simple delivery of rewards. Therefore, among the options, the one that is not one of Cialdini's six principles is reward, making option d correct. Recognizing these six principles is useful both for understanding persuasion and for resisting manipulative sales and marketing tactics.","traps":{"a":"Scarcity, the sense that opportunities are more valuable when limited, is one of Cialdini's six principles.","b":"Social validation (social proof), following what others do, is one of the six principles.","c":"Friendship/liking, complying more with those we like, is one of the six principles."}},{"q":"A client tells you that they are \"no one\" and feels \"empty inside.\" They report chronic fears of abandonment and have a history of intense, unstable relationships. According to psychoanalytic object relations theory, this pathology of the self is most likely a result of:","concept":"The answer is a failure in parental mirroring and idealizing leading to a fragmented self, because the clinical picture of chronic emptiness, a sense of being no one, intense fears of abandonment, and unstable relationships is best explained, within psychoanalytic object relations and self-psychology, as arising from early relational failures that leave the self fragmented rather than cohesive. Understand the theoretical lens. This vignette describes features characteristic of borderline-level personality organization, and the question asks you to explain it through the object relations and self-psychology tradition rather than classical Freudian drive theory (Sigmund Freud). Heinz Kohut's self-psychology holds that a stable, cohesive sense of self develops only when the child's selfobject needs are adequately met by caregivers, chiefly the mirroring need (having one's worth reflected back through an attuned parent's admiring responsiveness) and the idealizing need (being able to look up to and draw calm and strength from an admired, soothing parent). When these needs are chronically unmet, the self fails to consolidate and remains fragmented, prone to feelings of emptiness, inner deadness, and an unstable, poorly defined identity, exactly the person who says they are no one and feel empty inside. Object relations theorists like Otto Kernberg similarly trace borderline pathology to early disturbances in the relationship with caregivers that prevent the integration of good and bad representations of self and others (splitting), producing identity diffusion, terror of abandonment, and stormy, unstable relationships. Both frameworks locate the origin in failed early relational provision, not in Freudian drive fixations. So the emptiness, abandonment fears, and unstable relationships reflect failed mirroring and idealizing and a fragmented self.","traps":{"a":"Fixation at the oral stage is a classical Freudian drive-theory explanation tied to early feeding conflicts and traits like dependency, not the object-relations account of a fragmented self that the question frames.","c":"An unresolved Oedipal complex and castration anxiety belong to Freud's phallic-stage drive theory concerning triangular desire and rivalry, not to the object-relations pathology of emptiness and abandonment described here.","d":"Harsh superego demands leading to constant guilt describe a punitive conscience and guilt-based neurosis, not the identity diffusion, emptiness, and unstable relationships that reflect failed early relational mirroring."}},{"q":"In a typical major sleep period, how many cycles of NREM-REM is there:","concept":"The correct answer is 4 to 6. During a typical night of sleep, a person passes through several complete cycles of NREM (non-rapid eye movement) and REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, with each cycle lasting roughly 90 minutes, so across a full night of about seven to eight hours there are approximately four to six such cycles. Sleep architecture works as follows: a cycle begins with NREM sleep, which itself progresses through stages, from light sleep (stages N1 and N2) into deep slow-wave sleep (stage N3, dominated by delta waves and important for physical restoration), and then ascends back toward lighter sleep before entering REM sleep, the stage of vivid dreaming characterised by rapid eye movements, muscle atonia (paralysis of voluntary muscles), and brain activity resembling wakefulness. Importantly, the composition of cycles changes across the night: early cycles contain more deep slow-wave NREM sleep, whereas later cycles contain progressively longer REM periods, which is why most vivid dreaming and the longest REM episode occur toward morning. Knowing that a healthy adult cycles through NREM and REM about four to six times per night, with each cycle around 90 minutes, is a standard fact in the biopsychology of sleep.","traps":{"a":"2 to 4 cycles is too few for a normal full night's sleep of seven to eight hours.","c":"6 to 10 cycles is too many; with roughly 90-minute cycles a night yields only about four to six.","d":"10 to 12 cycles greatly exceeds what fits into a typical sleep period."}},{"q":"According to Vygotsky :","concept":"The correct answer is that inter-subjectivity precedes intra-subjectivity. This reflects the central principle of Lev Vygotsky's sociocultural theory, often called the general genetic law of cultural development: every higher mental function appears twice, first on the social (interpsychological) plane between people, and only later on the individual (intrapsychological) plane within the child. In other words, cognition originates in shared social interaction and is subsequently internalized. Intersubjectivity refers to the shared understanding, common focus, and mutual coordination that arise between a child and a more knowledgeable partner (a caregiver or teacher) during joint activity, for example when adult and child establish a shared reference point in the zone of proximal development. Through participation in such intersubjective exchanges, mediated by language and other cultural tools, the child gradually internalizes the meanings and thought processes, converting shared social understanding into individual (intrasubjective) mental functioning. Thus the interpersonal comes first and the intrapersonal is derived from it, the opposite of a view that would place the individual mind before the social. This social-to-individual direction of development is the hallmark of Vygotsky's account, making option d correct.","traps":{"a":"This reverses Vygotsky's core claim; for him the shared, social process comes before the internal one.","b":"Vygotsky held that children are deeply engaged in intersubjectivity through social interaction, so this is false.","c":"While the two are related, the question tests Vygotsky's specific sequencing claim, which is that the social precedes the individual."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a key concept in Carl Rogers' Client-Centered Therapy?","concept":"The answer is the therapeutic alliance as defined by Edward Bordin, because that construct comes from a different theoretical lineage and is not one of Carl Rogers' client-centered concepts. Understand Rogers' framework so you can see which three options are native to it. Carl Rogers, a founder of humanistic psychology, built client-centered (later person-centered) therapy on an optimistic view of human nature: every person has an actualizing tendency, an innate drive toward growth, health, and the fulfillment of potential. Problems arise, in his view, from conditions of worth, the messages from parents and society that one is only acceptable if one meets certain standards, which cause people to distort or deny their genuine experience and create incongruence between the real self and the self-concept. Therapy heals this through the therapist providing three core conditions, the necessary and sufficient conditions for change: unconditional positive regard (nonjudgmental acceptance of the client as a person of worth), empathy (accurately sensing the client's inner world), and congruence or genuineness (being real and transparent). So conditions of worth, unconditional positive regard, and the actualizing tendency are all Rogerian. The odd one out is the therapeutic alliance as formulated by Edward Bordin, whose influential 1979 model defined the working alliance in terms of three components, agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and the emotional bond, as a transtheoretical or integrative concept applicable across therapies, growing out of psychodynamic ideas about the working relationship rather than Rogers' theory. Hence Bordin's therapeutic alliance is the correct exception.","traps":{"a":"Conditions of worth is a genuine Rogerian concept, referring to the internalized standards a person believes they must meet to be valued, so it is central to client-centered theory rather than foreign to it.","b":"Unconditional positive regard is a core Rogerian therapist attitude, the warm nonjudgmental acceptance of the client, so it belongs firmly within client-centered therapy.","c":"The actualizing tendency, the innate drive toward growth and fulfillment of potential, is a foundational Rogerian construct, so it is not the outsider concept the question seeks."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a Schneider’s first-rank symptom?","concept":"The correct answer is delusion of self-reference. Kurt Schneider proposed a list of first-rank symptoms (FRS) which he considered, in the absence of organic disease, strongly suggestive of schizophrenia and useful for diagnosis. The classic Schneiderian first-rank symptoms are: auditory hallucinations of specific types (third-person voices discussing the patient, voices commenting on the patient's actions, or thought echo/audible thoughts), thought alienation phenomena (thought insertion, thought withdrawal, thought broadcasting), passivity phenomena or made experiences (made feelings, made impulses, made acts, and somatic passivity, where the patient feels their will, emotions, or body are controlled by an external force), and delusional perception (attaching an abnormal delusional significance to a normally perceived object). A delusion of self-reference (or simple ideas/delusions of reference), where the patient believes ordinary events, remarks, or media broadcasts are specifically about them, is a common symptom in schizophrenia but is NOT on Schneider's first-rank list, which is why it is the exception here. The first-rank symptoms are not pathognomonic (they occur in other psychoses too), but historically they shaped diagnostic criteria and remain a frequent exam topic for distinguishing core Schneiderian phenomena from other schizophrenic features.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations of the specific Schneiderian types (third-person, running commentary, thought echo) ARE first-rank symptoms, so this is not the exception.","b":"Delusional perception, attaching delusional meaning to a real percept, is a recognised first-rank symptom.","c":"Passivity phenomena (made acts, feelings, impulses, somatic passivity) are core first-rank symptoms of external control."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a pooled variance estimate that constitutes the denominator of an F-ratio?","concept":"The correct answer is the within-cells mean square (MSw). Analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald Aylmer Fisher, tests whether the means of several groups differ by partitioning the total variability in the data into components and forming a ratio of variances. The F-ratio is calculated as the between-groups mean square divided by the within-groups mean square. The numerator, the between-cells or between-groups mean square (MSB), captures variance due to the treatment effect plus error, reflecting how much the group means differ from the grand mean. The denominator, the within-cells or within-groups mean square (MSw, also called the error mean square), is a pooled estimate of the variance within each group, that is, the average variability of scores around their own group means, and it represents random error or noise unaffected by the treatment. Because MSw pools the variance estimates from all the separate cells or groups, it is the pooled variance estimate that serves as the F-ratio denominator. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance and F is near 1.00; if the treatment has an effect, the numerator grows and F increases. The distractors are either the numerator, a non-variance quantity, or a sum-of-squares term. This item tests understanding of the structure of the F-ratio in ANOVA.","traps":{"a":"The between-cells mean square (MSB) is the numerator of the F-ratio, reflecting variance due to the treatment or group differences, not the denominator.","b":"The mean of the sampling distribution is a descriptive quantity, not a variance estimate, and plays no role as the F-ratio denominator.","c":"The sum of deviations from the grand mean is a raw sum of squares component, not a mean square (variance estimate), and the deviations sum to zero anyway."}},{"q":"Which theory of emotion explains the phenomenon of misattribution of arousal (e.g. feeling romantic attraction when actually scared)?","concept":"The two-factor theory of emotion, developed by Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer in 1962, proposes that emotion arises from two ingredients: physiological arousal plus a cognitive label that the person applies to that arousal based on the situational context. The arousal itself is relatively general and undifferentiated; it is the cognitive interpretation of why one is aroused that determines which specific emotion is felt. This is precisely what allows misattribution of arousal: if the true source of arousal is ambiguous, a person may attribute it to the wrong cause. The classic demonstration is the study by Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron on a high, wobbly suspension bridge, in which men experiencing fear-induced arousal misattributed it to attraction toward an interviewer, reporting more romantic interest than men on a stable bridge. The distractors cannot account for this: James-Lange (William James and Carl Lange) ties each emotion to a specific bodily pattern, Cannon-Bard (Walter Cannon and Philip Bard) treats arousal and emotion as simultaneous and independent, and the facial feedback hypothesis concerns expressions. Because the two-factor theory explains misattribution through relabeling of arousal, option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"The James-Lange theory holds that arousal directly determines a specific emotion, which cannot easily explain misattribution across situations.","c":"The Cannon-Bard theory says arousal and emotion arise simultaneously and independently, leaving no room for cognitive relabeling of the same arousal.","d":"The facial feedback hypothesis concerns how facial expressions influence felt emotion, not the cognitive labeling of arousal."}},{"q":"At what age can the Malin's Intelligence Scale be administered to Indian children?","concept":"The correct answer is 6 years. The Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is an Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (David Wechsler), adapted by Arthur J. Malin. Like the Wechsler children's scale on which it is based, it is designed for school-age children and is standardized for administration from about 6 years up to roughly 15 or 16 years of age. It is one of the most widely used individually administered intelligence tests in Indian clinical and educational settings, yielding a verbal IQ, a performance IQ, and a full-scale IQ derived from a set of verbal subtests (such as information, comprehension, arithmetic, similarities, vocabulary, and digit span) and performance subtests (such as picture completion, block design, object assembly, coding, and mazes). Because it is adapted and standardized on an Indian population, it provides more culturally appropriate norms than directly importing a Western test. This lower age limit of 6 years distinguishes it from preschool-level instruments used for younger children, such as the Indian adaptations of the Binet scales (Alfred Binet) or the Gesell Developmental Schedules (Arnold Gesell). Because the Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children can be administered from age 6, option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"4 years is below the standardized lower age limit of the Malin's scale.","b":"2 years is far too young for this Wechsler-based children's intelligence scale.","c":"5 years still falls short of the six-year lower boundary of the instrument."}},{"q":"Raven and his colleagues formulated a model identifying the six bases of power for influencing others. These do not include:","concept":"The correct answer is reciprocity. The classic taxonomy of social power was developed by John French and Bertram Raven, who originally identified five bases of power a person can use to influence others: reward power (the ability to give positive outcomes), coercive power (the ability to punish or impose negative outcomes), legitimate power (authority derived from one's role or position), referent power (influence arising from others' identification with or admiration of the person), and expert power (influence based on possessing superior knowledge or skill). Raven later added a sixth base, informational power, which rests on the persuasive content of a message or the information one provides, rather than on the influencer's position or relationship, and unlike the others it can produce lasting, independent change because the target internalizes the reasoning. Reciprocity, by contrast, is not one of French and Raven's bases of power; it is a norm of social exchange and one of the principles of compliance identified by Robert Cialdini, the felt obligation to return a favour. So while reciprocity is a genuine influence mechanism, it belongs to a different framework and is not among the six bases of power. Because reciprocity is not one of Raven's six bases, option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Coercive power, based on the ability to punish, is one of the classic bases of social power.","c":"Expert power, based on superior knowledge or skill, is one of the recognized bases of power.","d":"Informational power, based on the content of a persuasive message, was added as one of the six bases."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old man reports a persistent, pervasive pattern of grandiosity, a need for constant admiration, and a distinct lack of empathy. According to Kohut's self-psychology, this pathology arises from a failure in parental:","concept":"The answer is mirroring, because in Heinz Kohut's self-psychology, the grandiose, admiration-seeking, empathy-deficient presentation of narcissistic pathology arises specifically from a failure in parental mirroring. Learn Kohut's framework from scratch. Breaking from Sigmund Freud's drive theory, Kohut argued that the self develops through relationships with what he called selfobjects, meaning other people who are experienced not as fully separate individuals but as parts of the self that perform vital psychological functions for the child. He described three selfobject needs that must be adequately met for a cohesive, stable self to form. The first is the mirroring need: the child's healthy grandiosity and exhibitionism ('look at me, admire me') must be reflected back by an attuned parent's approving gaze, the gleam in the mother's eye, so the child gradually builds realistic self-esteem. The second is the idealizing need: the child needs to look up to and merge with a calm, powerful, admired parent, which internalizes into self-soothing capacities and guiding ideals. The third is the twinship or alter-ego need: the sense of being essentially like others, which grounds belonging. When mirroring fails, the child's grandiosity is never metabolized into mature self-esteem and instead persists in raw, archaic form, producing exactly the adult picture of grandiosity, insatiable need for admiration, and shallow empathy. So the correct link is grandiosity plus admiration-hunger equals failed mirroring in Kohut's self-psychology.","traps":{"b":"Idealizing needs concern the child's wish to merge with a calm, strong, admired parent; their failure yields problems with self-soothing and ideals, not the grandiosity and admiration-hunger described here.","c":"Twinship or alter-ego needs concern the sense of being alike and belonging among peers; their failure produces feelings of alienation rather than the grandiose, empathy-poor presentation of narcissism.","d":"Confrontation is a therapeutic technique, not one of Kohut's developmental selfobject needs, so it does not fit the framework the question is testing."}},{"q":"Cutting through a person’s corpus callosum causes the person to :","concept":"The correct answer is that the person will have difficulty coordinating certain kinds of activities. The corpus callosum is the large bundle of nerve fibres (the major commissure) that connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres, allowing them to communicate and share information. When it is surgically severed, a procedure called a corpus callosotomy historically used to treat severe, intractable epilepsy by preventing seizures from spreading between hemispheres, the result is a split-brain patient. Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga's famous studies of these patients revealed that the two hemispheres can no longer exchange information, producing striking but subtle effects. For example, an object shown only to the left visual field (processed by the right hemisphere) cannot be named aloud, because language resides in the left hemisphere, yet the patient can pick out the object with the left hand. In everyday life, split-brain patients function surprisingly normally, but they show difficulties in tasks requiring the two hemispheres to coordinate, such as certain bimanual movements where each hand must cooperate, and they can show conflicts between the hands. Crucially, cutting the corpus callosum does not kill the person, blind them, or destroy language, so the only accurate consequence among the options is difficulty coordinating certain kinds of activities.","traps":{"a":"Cutting the corpus callosum does not cause death; split-brain patients live and function largely normally.","b":"It does not cause blindness; visual processing is intact, though information cannot be shared between hemispheres.","c":"Language is not lost; speech and comprehension remain because language centres in the left hemisphere are untouched."}},{"q":"Children, who are distressed by their parent's absence, seek interaction but also reject it when offered. How can the reaction be categorised?","concept":"The correct answer is insecure-resistant (also called anxious-ambivalent or insecure-ambivalent) attachment. Mary Ainsworth developed the Strange Situation procedure to classify infant attachment based on the child's reactions to separation from and reunion with the caregiver. She identified secure attachment and two insecure patterns, later joined by a fourth. Insecure-resistant infants are highly distressed by the caregiver's absence and are difficult to soothe on reunion: they simultaneously seek contact and comfort yet resist or reject it when it is offered, showing an angry, ambivalent, push-pull pattern of clinging and rejecting. This stems from inconsistent, unpredictable caregiving, so the infant cannot rely on the caregiver as a secure base. By contrast, insecure-avoidant infants (from rejecting or unresponsive caregiving) minimise distress and avoid the caregiver, and securely attached infants (from sensitive, responsive caregiving) are readily comforted. The disorganised category, added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon, describes contradictory, fearful behaviour often linked to frightening caregiving. The described approach-then-resist ambivalence maps precisely onto the resistant type. This item tests knowledge of Ainsworth's attachment classifications and their distinguishing reunion behaviours.","traps":{"a":"Insecure-avoidant infants show little distress at separation and ignore or avoid the caregiver on reunion; they do not seek then reject contact.","c":"Insecure-disorganized infants show confused, contradictory, or fearful behaviours with no coherent strategy, rather than the clear approach-then-resist pattern described.","d":"Secure-resistant is not a recognised attachment category; securely attached infants are comforted and settle on reunion, unlike the ambivalent pattern here."}},{"q":"Which of the following disabilities is explicitly covered under the scope of The National Trust Act (1999) in India for the welfare of persons with the disability?","concept":"The answer is both autism and intellectual disability, because the National Trust Act of 1999 explicitly names four conditions, and the two offered in this item both belong to that list. Learn the statute precisely, because Indian disability law is a favorite exam target and the trap is confusing this Act with the broader disability laws. The full title is the National Trust for the Welfare of Persons with Autism, Cerebral Palsy, Mental Retardation and Multiple Disabilities Act, 1999. That title itself is the answer key: the four covered conditions are autism, cerebral palsy, intellectual disability (the Act uses the older term mental retardation), and multiple disabilities. The Act created a statutory body, the National Trust, to promote independent living, arrange legal guardianship, and support families of persons with these severe developmental conditions. Contrast this with two other laws examiners deliberately blur together. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 recognizes a much wider list of twenty-one disabilities, including specific learning disability, mental illness, and blindness, which is why specific learning disability is a distractor here. The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 governs treatment and rights of persons with mental illness, a different domain again. When a question cites the National Trust Act, restrict yourself to its four named conditions, and remember that two of them, autism and intellectual disability, appear together in this item, so the combined option is correct.","traps":{"a":"Autism Spectrum Disorder is indeed covered, but choosing it alone is incomplete because intellectual disability is also explicitly named in the Act.","b":"Specific Learning Disability is covered under the later Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016, not under the National Trust Act, 1999, which lists a narrower set of conditions.","c":"Intellectual disability is covered, but selecting only this option omits autism, which the Act names in the same breath, making the combined answer correct instead."}},{"q":"Following are negative features of schizophrenia except:","concept":"The correct answer is ambivalence, which is the exception. The symptoms of schizophrenia are traditionally divided into positive and negative symptoms. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal functions, things added to experience, such as hallucinations, delusions, and disorganised thought and speech. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal functions, things taken away, and they are summarised by several A-words: avolition (lack of motivation and inability to initiate or sustain goal-directed activity), alogia (poverty of speech, reduced verbal output and content), affective blunting or flattening (diminished emotional expression in face, voice, and gesture), anhedonia (inability to experience pleasure), and asociality (social withdrawal). Avolition, alogia, and affective blunting are all classic negative symptoms, so they are not the exception. Ambivalence, the simultaneous holding of contradictory feelings or wishes, is one of Eugen Bleuler's fundamental four As of schizophrenia (along with loosening of associations, affective disturbance, and autism), but in the modern positive-versus-negative symptom classification it is not categorised as a negative symptom in the same way the deficit symptoms are. Because the question asks which is NOT a negative feature, ambivalence is the correct answer to select.","traps":{"a":"Avolition (loss of drive and motivation) is a core negative symptom, so it is not the exception.","b":"Alogia (poverty of speech) is a recognised negative symptom reflecting reduced verbal output.","d":"Affective blunting (flattened emotional expression) is a classic negative symptom, so it belongs in the negative category."}},{"q":"A researcher uses a Scree plot to determine the number of factors to retain in an EFA. The plot shows a sharp drop from eigenvalue 3.5 to 1.8, followed by a gradual plateau where eigenvalues are 0.9, 0.7, and 0.5. According to the Kaiser-Guttman rule and the Scree plot, how many factors should ideally be retained?","concept":"The answer is 2 factors, and you get there by applying the two classic retention rules in exploratory factor analysis and seeing that they agree. First, the Kaiser-Guttman rule, proposed by Henry Kaiser building on Louis Guttman's work, says retain only factors whose eigenvalue exceeds 1.0. The logic is that in a standardized correlation matrix each original variable contributes exactly one unit of variance, so a factor with an eigenvalue below 1 explains less variance than a single raw variable and is not worth keeping. Here the eigenvalues are 3.5, 1.8, 0.9, 0.7 and 0.5; only 3.5 and 1.8 clear the threshold, giving two factors. Second, the scree test, introduced by Raymond Cattell, plots eigenvalues in descending order and looks for the elbow, the point where the steep cliff turns into flat rubble (scree is the loose rock at a cliff's base). You keep the factors on the cliff before the plateau begins. The question describes a sharp drop from 3.5 to 1.8 followed by a gradual plateau starting at 0.9, so the elbow falls after the second factor, again giving two. When both criteria converge, as here, the retention decision is unambiguous. Remember the pairing of names: Kaiser-Guttman for the eigenvalue-greater-than-one rule, Cattell for the scree plot.","traps":{"a":"Retaining 5 factors would mean keeping every listed eigenvalue including 0.9, 0.7 and 0.5, all of which fall below the Kaiser criterion of 1.0 and sit on the flat plateau of the scree plot.","b":"Retaining 3 factors would include the eigenvalue of 0.9, which is below 1.0 and therefore fails the Kaiser-Guttman rule, and it lies past the elbow where the plot has already flattened.","d":"Retaining only 1 factor ignores the second eigenvalue of 1.8, which is clearly above 1.0 and appears before the curve levels off, so it satisfies both retention criteria."}},{"q":"Which type of intelligence according to Sternberg's Triarchic theory of intelligence includes the ability to easily adopt to the present environment or select a more favourable environment than the existing one or modify the environment to fit one's need?","concept":"The correct answer is: Contextual. Robert Sternberg's CONTEXTUAL (practical) intelligence is the ability to adapt to, shape, or select environments, exactly the adapt/select/modify language in the stem. Componential is analytical intelligence; experiential is the third (creative) component.","traps":{"a":"Componential (analytical) intelligence is about internal information processing, not adapting to the environment.","c":"'Incremental' is not one of Sternberg's three intelligences (it echoes Carol Dweck's mindset theory).","d":"Sternberg's third component is experiential/creative, not 'experimental'; and it is not the adapt/select/shape ability."}},{"q":"Under which grade of insight does \"intellectual insight\" come under?","concept":"The correct answer is Grade 5. In psychiatric assessment, insight, the patient's awareness and understanding of their own illness, is often graded on a six-point scale. The commonly taught grading runs as follows: Grade 1 is complete denial of illness; Grade 2 is slight awareness of being sick and needing help but denying it at the same time; Grade 3 is awareness of being sick but blaming it on others, on external factors, or on physical or organic causes; Grade 4 is awareness that the illness is due to something unknown within the patient; Grade 5 is intellectual insight, in which the patient admits they are ill and acknowledges that their symptoms or social maladjustment are due to their own particular irrational feelings or disturbance, yet does not apply this understanding to actually change future behavior; and Grade 6 is true emotional insight, a deeper emotional awareness of one's motives and feelings that does lead to meaningful change in behavior and personality. The crucial distinction is that intellectual insight (Grade 5) is a head-level acknowledgment without behavioral change, whereas emotional insight (Grade 6) is accompanied by genuine change. Because intellectual insight corresponds specifically to Grade 5, option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Grade 3 refers to awareness of illness but blaming it on external or physical factors, not intellectual insight.","b":"Grade 4 is awareness that one is ill due to something unknown in oneself, still short of intellectual insight.","d":"Grade 6 is true emotional insight, which goes beyond the intellectual acknowledgement of Grade 5."}},{"q":"A group of scientists is tasked with making a decision about the use of live animals in their university research. There is great deal of community pressure to create a definitive policy and the leading scientist is concerned about the group's ability to make the correct decision since the majority of the group has used live animals in their research. How might the leader reduce groupthink among the scientists as they make their decision?","concept":"The correct answer is that the leader could ask a subset of individuals to critique animal research. Groupthink is a phenomenon identified by Irving Janis, in which the desire for harmony, unanimity, and conformity within a cohesive group overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives, leading to poor, irrational decisions. Symptoms include an illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, suppression of dissent, self-censorship, an illusion of unanimity, and the emergence of self-appointed mindguards. Janis analysed fiascos such as the Bay of Pigs invasion. Crucially, Janis also proposed remedies to prevent groupthink, and the best ones involve deliberately encouraging dissent and critical evaluation. Assigning one or more members the role of devil's advocate or critical evaluator, exactly what option C describes by asking a subset to critique the group's leaning toward animal research, forces the group to examine opposing views and counters premature consensus. Other recommended safeguards include the leader remaining impartial and withholding their own opinion at first, inviting outside experts, and breaking into subgroups. Options A, B, and D each do the opposite of Janis's advice: building cohesion, having the leader state a view first, and banning argument all suppress dissent and intensify groupthink. This item tests knowledge of Janis's groupthink concept and its prevention strategies.","traps":{"a":"Increasing group cohesion through team building would raise, not reduce, groupthink, since high cohesiveness is a key precondition for it.","b":"A leader stating his opinion first biases the discussion toward that view, promoting conformity and worsening groupthink.","d":"Discouraging argument suppresses dissent and critical evaluation, which increases groupthink rather than reducing it."}},{"q":"In paranoia, the ego’s defense mechanism as given by Melaine Klein is seen as:","concept":"The correct answer is splitting. Within Melanie Klein's object-relations theory, splitting is the most primitive defence and the one she linked to the earliest, paranoid-schizoid position of infancy. The infant cannot yet hold the idea that the same caregiver who gratifies (the good breast) is also the one who frustrates (the bad breast). To manage the resulting anxiety, the infant splits the object and the self into wholly good and wholly bad parts, keeping them separate so that the loving feelings are not contaminated or destroyed by the hostile ones. In Kleinian theory the persecutory anxiety of the paranoid-schizoid position, where the bad object is felt to threaten and persecute the self, underlies paranoid functioning, and splitting (together with projective identification) is the characteristic defence. This is why, in paranoia, the mechanism Klein emphasised is splitting rather than the more classically Freudian projection (Sigmund Freud). Splitting also reappears in adult borderline personality organisation, where people oscillate between idealising and devaluing the same person. Understanding splitting is central to object-relations and to grasping how very early mental life is organised around managing aggression and preserving the good object.","traps":{"a":"Reaction formation converts an unacceptable impulse into its opposite (hating into excessive caring); it is a higher-level neurotic defence, not Klein's account of paranoia.","c":"Suppression is the conscious, deliberate pushing aside of a thought, a mature defence, not the primitive one Klein tied to paranoia.","d":"Projection is the classic Freudian mechanism in paranoia, but the question specifically asks for Klein's formulation, which centres on splitting."}},{"q":"Brain regions in procedural learning is related to","concept":"The correct answer is: cerebellum, basal ganglia, motor cortex. Procedural (implicit, skill) learning depends on the cerebellum, basal ganglia and motor cortex. The hippocampus, in contrast, supports declarative/explicit memory, which is why option (d) is the trap.","traps":{"a":"The hindbrain and spinal cord handle basic reflexes and life support, not skill learning.","c":"The frontal/association cortex supports executive and higher associative functions, not the core motor-skill circuit.","d":"The hippocampus and amygdala serve declarative and emotional memory, not procedural skill learning."}},{"q":"Which among the following is not included within the classification of structure of temperament given by Rothbart and Bates","concept":"The correct answer is inhibition to the unfamiliar. Mary Rothbart and John Bates built their structural model of temperament on exactly three broad dimensions: (1) Surgency / extraversion, meaning positive affect, activity level, approach and sociability; (2) Negative affectivity, meaning fear, frustration, sadness and discomfort; (3) Effortful control, the capacity to voluntarily focus and shift attention and to inhibit a dominant response, which is the self-regulatory core tied to executive attention. Inhibition to the unfamiliar, better known as behavioral inhibition, is a real temperament construct but it belongs to Jerome Kagan, who studied inhibited versus uninhibited children and their wariness in novel situations. It overlaps with fear inside negative affectivity, yet it is not one of the three named Rothbart and Bates dimensions. Exam hook: surgency, negative affectivity and effortful control map to Rothbart and Bates; behavioral inhibition maps to Kagan.","traps":{"a":"Extraversion/surgency is one of the three core dimensions in Rothbart's model, so it is included.","c":"Negative affectivity is a defining dimension of the Rothbart and Bates structure.","d":"Effortful control is the self-regulatory dimension central to their model, so it is included."}},{"q":"Which kind of empathy is considered inappropriate for an initial counselling interview or session?","concept":"The correct answer is advanced empathy. In the influential skilled-helper model of counselling developed by Gerard Egan, a distinction is drawn between primary (basic) empathy and advanced (accurate) empathy. Primary empathy is the counsellor's accurate understanding and reflection of the feelings and meanings the client has directly expressed; it communicates that the client has been heard and understood and is essential for building rapport and the therapeutic alliance from the very first session. Advanced empathy goes further: it involves the counsellor drawing out and putting into words the feelings, meanings, themes, and implications that the client has only hinted at, implied, or left half-stated, in effect helping the client see themselves more fully, including material they have not yet consciously articulated. Because advanced empathy probes beneath the surface and confronts the client with deeper or unspoken content, it can feel intrusive, premature, or threatening if used before a solid, trusting relationship has been established. It is therefore considered inappropriate in an initial interview and is reserved for later stages once trust and rapport are firm. Primary empathy and culturally sensitive empathy, by contrast, are appropriate and necessary from the outset. Because advanced empathy is the type to avoid early on, option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Primary empathy, reflecting the client's expressed feelings, is exactly what is appropriate and needed early on.","c":"Since primary empathy is appropriate initially, pairing it with advanced empathy is incorrect.","d":"Culturally sensitive empathy is always appropriate and indeed essential, not something to withhold early."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a culture-bound syndrome where the patient responds to minimal stimuli with exaggerated startles, often exclaiming normally inhibited sexually denotative words?","concept":"The correct answer is Latah, a culture-bound syndrome documented primarily in Malaysia and Indonesia. It is characterized by an exaggerated startle response to a sudden stimulus, followed by involuntary behaviors such as echolalia (repeating others' words), echopraxia (imitating others' movements), automatic obedience, and coprolalia (uttering normally inhibited, sexually explicit words). Culture-bound syndromes were catalogued in the DSM-IV glossary and later reframed as cultural concepts of distress in DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association. Latah usually affects middle-aged women and is often treated within the culture as a source of amusement rather than illness. The condition illustrates how psychiatric presentations are shaped by cultural context: the hyperstartle exists cross-culturally, but the specific patterned aftermath is locally scripted. Distinguishing Latah from Koro (genital retraction panic, reported in Southeast Asia and southern China), Dhat (semen-loss anxiety in South Asia), and Susto (fright-induced soul loss in Latin America) is a common entrance-exam task, so anchoring each to its defining feature and region is the reliable strategy.","traps":{"b":"Koro is the fear that the genitals are retracting into the body, a distinct culture-bound syndrome with no exaggerated startle.","c":"Dhat is a South Asian syndrome of anxiety over semen loss, unrelated to startle responses.","d":"Susto is a Latin American 'soul loss' following fright, marked by listlessness rather than an exaggerated startle with coprolalia."}},{"q":"A researcher wants to calculate the degrees of freedom ($df$) for a $3 \\times 4$ contingency table in a Chi-square test. The df is:","concept":"The answer is 6 because the degrees of freedom for a chi-square test of independence on a contingency table are calculated as (number of rows minus 1) multiplied by (number of columns minus 1). For a 3 by 4 table, that is (3 minus 1) times (4 minus 1), which equals 2 times 3, which equals 6. Degrees of freedom represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints (here, the fixed row and column totals, called marginal totals) are imposed. Intuitively, once you know the marginal totals of a contingency table, only a limited number of cell values can be filled in freely before the rest are forced by the requirement that the rows and columns must add up to those margins; in a 3 by 4 table exactly 6 cells can vary independently, and the remaining ones are then determined. This is why the formula subtracts 1 from each dimension before multiplying. It is important to distinguish this from the chi-square goodness-of-fit test, which compares observed frequencies in a single row of categories against expected frequencies and uses df equal to the number of categories minus 1, which is where the tempting value of 11 comes from. For a two-way table always use (r minus 1)(c minus 1). Here that gives 2 times 3 equals 6.","traps":{"a":"12 is the total number of cells (3 times 4), which is not the degrees of freedom; df is based on rows and columns minus one, not the raw cell count.","b":"7 comes from wrongly adding (rows minus 1) plus (columns minus 1) as 2 plus 3 plus something, or otherwise misapplying the formula; the correct approach multiplies these terms.","d":"11 is the total cells minus one (12 minus 1), which is the formula for a goodness-of-fit test, not for a two-way contingency table."}},{"q":"The first-grade teacher gives students stickers when they perform well. If they earn five stickers in one day they are exempt from homework. The stickers in this example could also be called:","concept":"The correct answer is token. In operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner), a token is a conditioned reinforcer (a secondary reinforcer) that has no intrinsic value but can be accumulated and exchanged for something the learner actually wants, called a backup reinforcer. Here the stickers themselves do nothing biologically rewarding; their power comes entirely from the rule that five of them earn an exemption from homework. This is the defining structure of a token economy, a behaviour-modification system developed by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin and widely used in classrooms, psychiatric wards, and rehabilitation settings. The procedure works because tokens bridge the delay between desirable behaviour and the eventual reward, can be delivered immediately and consistently, and let the learner choose when to cash in. Tokens become reinforcing through association with the backup reward, exactly as money becomes reinforcing because it buys goods. The question specifically names the stickers, the exchangeable items, which is why 'token' rather than the more general 'generalized reinforcer' is the most precise label, since the stickers here buy one specific privilege rather than a wide range of rewards.","traps":{"a":"A primary reinforcer satisfies a biological need directly, like food or water; stickers have no innate value and only work through their exchange rule.","c":"A generalized reinforcer is a token that can be exchanged for many different rewards (like money); here the stickers buy only one specific privilege, so 'token' is the tighter answer.","d":"Prepotent responses refer to the more probable, preferred behaviour in the Premack principle (David Premack), not to an exchangeable reward."}},{"q":"To determine whether a newly developed intelligence test truly accurately measures intelligence or not, it is necessary to administer the test with one that has been in the field for some time. Which of the following needs to be looked for?","concept":"The correct answer is: Concurrent validity. Correlating a new test with an already established test given at the same time is CONCURRENT validity (a form of criterion validity where the criterion is measured concurrently). The presence of an existing well-validated test administered alongside is the giveaway.","traps":{"a":"Construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955) is the broader question of whether the test measures the theoretical construct; here the method is specifically comparison with an existing test.","c":"Content validity concerns whether items cover the domain, judged by experts, not comparison with another test.","d":"Predictive validity checks correlation with a FUTURE criterion, not a test given concurrently."}},{"q":"Miss Pooja is a school teacher who gives her students group projects as part of their Maths curriculum. What might she do to minimise the effects of social loafing?","concept":"The correct answer is to grade both the group as a whole and each individual group member. Social loafing is the tendency for individuals to exert less effort when working collectively on a task than when working alone, because their individual contribution is pooled and cannot be easily identified or evaluated. It was demonstrated in early work by Max Ringelmann (the Ringelmann effect, where individual pulling effort on a rope decreased as group size increased) and studied further by Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams, and Stephen Harkins. A central cause is the loss of individual identifiability and accountability: when only the group outcome is evaluated, individuals feel their slacking will go unnoticed and their extra effort unrewarded (dispensability of effort). The most effective remedy, therefore, is to restore individual accountability by making each person's contribution identifiable and evaluable. Grading both the group product and each member's individual contribution ensures that students cannot hide in the collective and are held responsible for their own share, which directly reduces loafing. Option A does the opposite by removing individual evaluation entirely. Random assignment and optional presentations do not address identifiability. This item tests understanding of social loafing and the accountability-based strategy for minimising it.","traps":{"a":"Grading only the group as a whole removes individual accountability, which actually increases social loafing rather than reducing it.","c":"Random group assignment addresses group composition but does nothing to make individual contributions identifiable, so it does not target loafing.","d":"An optional extra-credit presentation is voluntary and does not make each member accountable for their share of the core project, so it fails to reduce loafing."}},{"q":"Gordon Allport preferred which of the following research approaches in his personality research?","concept":"The correct answer is the idiographic approach. Gordon Allport, a founding figure of personality psychology, distinguished two broad approaches to studying personality: the nomothetic approach, which seeks general laws and compares people on the same set of common traits (favoured by trait and factor theorists such as Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck), and the idiographic approach, which focuses on the depth, uniqueness, and individuality of a single person, using methods like case studies, diaries, and letters. Although Allport acknowledged common traits shared across people, he strongly emphasised the idiographic study of the individual, arguing that each person possesses unique personal dispositions that cannot be fully captured by comparing them on universal dimensions. He famously classified traits into cardinal traits (dominant, pervasive), central traits (general characteristics forming the core personality), and secondary traits (situation-specific). His idiographic leaning is illustrated by his intensive analysis of personal documents, such as the Letters from Jenny study. The distractors point to statistical or group-comparison methods (factorial design, chi-square, nomothetic) that emphasise commonalities rather than individuality. This item tests whether the student links Allport specifically with the idiographic, person-centred tradition.","traps":{"a":"Factorial design and factor analysis are associated with trait theorists like Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck, who reduced personality to common factors, not with Allport's focus on the individual.","b":"The nomothetic approach seeks universal laws and common traits across people; Allport valued this less than the study of the unique individual.","d":"The chi-square method is a statistical test of association for categorical data, not a personality-research philosophy that Allport advocated."}},{"q":"Despite his severe amnesia, patient H.M. was able to learn and improve performance on a mirror-tracing task over several days, even though he had no conscious recollection of having practiced it before. This demonstrates the preservation of:","concept":"The answer is procedural memory, because H.M.'s steady improvement on the mirror-tracing task across days, despite having no conscious memory of ever practicing it, demonstrates that his procedural memory, the memory for motor skills and habits, remained intact even though his declarative memory was devastated. Understand the fundamental dissociation this reveals. Memory is not a single system; a major division separates declarative (explicit) memory, the conscious recollection of facts (semantic) and events (episodic), from nondeclarative (implicit) memory, which includes procedural memory for skills, priming, and conditioning, and operates without conscious awareness. Procedural memory is the know-how of riding a bicycle, typing, or tracing a figure while watching your hand in a mirror, a difficult perceptual-motor skill. When Brenda Milner tested H.M. on the mirror-drawing task over successive days, his performance got measurably better and smoother, the normal learning curve, yet each day he insisted he had never done the task before, because his damaged hippocampus could not form the declarative memory of the practice sessions. This double dissociation, impaired declarative learning alongside preserved procedural learning, was crucial evidence that these memory systems depend on different brain structures: the medial temporal lobe and hippocampus for declarative memory, and other structures such as the basal ganglia and cerebellum for procedural memory. So the ability H.M. retained, learning a skill without any conscious memory of learning it, is procedural (implicit) memory.","traps":{"a":"Episodic memory (Endel Tulving, 1972), the conscious recollection of specific personally experienced events, was severely impaired in H.M.; he had no memory of the practice sessions, so this declarative form was not what was preserved.","b":"Declarative memory is the umbrella term for consciously accessible memory of facts and events, precisely the system H.M. lost, so it is the opposite of what the mirror-tracing improvement demonstrates.","d":"Semantic memory, the conscious store of general facts and knowledge, is another subtype of declarative memory that was compromised for new learning, not the nonconscious skill memory shown by improved tracing."}},{"q":"According to Jean Piaget, a child in the concrete operational stage is capable of logical thought, but struggles with:","concept":"The answer is hypothetico-deductive reasoning, because the concrete operational child can reason logically about tangible, present realities but cannot yet think abstractly and systematically about hypothetical possibilities, which is the hallmark of the later formal operational stage. Understand Jean Piaget's four stages so the boundary is clear. Piaget proposed that children construct knowledge through stages of qualitatively different thinking. The sensorimotor stage (birth to about two) is about acting on the world and achieving object permanence. The preoperational stage (about two to seven) brings symbolic thought and language but is limited by egocentrism, centration, and the inability to conserve. The concrete operational stage (about seven to eleven) is when genuine logical operations appear: the child can conserve quantity, classify, seriate, and reason reversibly, but crucially only about concrete, actual, observable things in front of them. What still eludes the concrete operational child is abstraction, and specifically hypothetico-deductive reasoning, the ability to generate hypotheses about what could be true and test them systematically, as in reasoning through a scientific problem or contemplating purely verbal, contrary-to-fact propositions. That capacity emerges in the formal operational stage (about eleven onward), which also brings abstract, idealistic, and propositional thought. So the concrete operational child is defined by mastery of concrete logic paired with an inability to handle the abstract and hypothetical, making hypothetico-deductive reasoning the correct answer, and note that conservation is an achievement of this stage, not a struggle.","traps":{"a":"Object permanence, understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight, is achieved back in the sensorimotor stage in infancy, so it is not the limitation of the concrete operational child.","b":"Egocentrism, the inability to take another's perspective, characterizes the earlier preoperational stage and is largely overcome by the concrete operational period, so it is not the defining struggle here.","d":"Conservation of mass is actually an achievement of the concrete operational stage, not a struggle; the concrete operational child can conserve, so this option states the opposite of the truth."}},{"q":"What is Socratic questioning?","concept":"The correct answer is option c. Socratic questioning, a cornerstone of the cognitive therapy of Aaron Beck (named after the Greek philosopher Socrates and his method of eliciting knowledge through disciplined questioning), is a form of guided dialogue in which the therapist asks a systematic series of questions to help clients examine their own thoughts and beliefs. Two features define it precisely, and option c captures both. First, its purpose is to help the person discover and attend to previously overlooked information, that is, evidence, alternative interpretations, and perspectives the client had not previously considered or had ignored because of their cognitive distortions. Second, the direction of discovery belongs to the individual (the client), not the therapist: the therapist does not lecture, interpret, or impose conclusions but instead uses questions to guide the client to reach new understandings themselves. This client-led discovery is exactly what makes it part of guided discovery and collaborative empiricism in cognitive therapy, and it contrasts sharply with the interpretive stance of psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud). The distractor options each corrupt one or both of these elements, either by substituting previously attended for previously overlooked information, or by locating the discovery in the therapist rather than the individual. Only option c states both correctly.","traps":{"a":"This misplaces the discovery in the therapist rather than the individual, reversing the client-centered aim of Socratic questioning.","b":"This says previously attended information, but the point is to surface previously overlooked information the client had not noticed.","d":"This is doubly wrong: it refers to previously attended information and locates the discovery in the therapist."}},{"q":"The genetic mechanisms that ultimately contribute to the symptoms and difficulties experienced by people with psychological disorders are known as:","concept":"The answer is endophenotypes because the question asks for the genetically influenced intermediate mechanisms that lie between the underlying genes and the observable symptoms of a psychological disorder. An endophenotype is a measurable, heritable trait, often a neurophysiological, biochemical, cognitive, or neuroanatomical characteristic, that is not visible to the naked eye but sits along the causal pathway from genotype to the full clinical phenotype. The concept was introduced to psychiatric genetics because complex disorders like schizophrenia or bipolar disorder are not caused by single genes producing obvious effects; instead, many genes contribute to subtle intermediate processes (for example, deficits in working memory, abnormal eye-tracking, or altered prepulse inhibition) that are more directly tied to specific genes than the broad diagnostic syndrome is. By studying these simpler, more genetically tractable endophenotypes, researchers hope to bridge the gap between DNA and the messy, multiply-determined symptom picture. Contrast this with the broader terms: the genotype is the organism's complete genetic constitution, the phenotype is any observable characteristic resulting from genes plus environment, and molecular genetics is the field that studies genes at the level of DNA. The defining feature of an endophenotype is that it is the heritable intermediate that ultimately contributes to symptoms, which is exactly what the question describes, making endophenotypes the precise answer.","traps":{"b":"Phenotype is the broad term for any observable characteristic produced by the interaction of genes and environment; it is the visible outcome, not the hidden genetic intermediate mechanism the question specifies.","c":"Genotype is the underlying genetic makeup itself, the set of genes, not the intermediate mechanism that links those genes to symptoms.","d":"Molecular genetics is a field of study that examines gene structure and function at the DNA level, not a term for the heritable intermediate traits that contribute to disorder symptoms."}},{"q":"In a negatively skewed distribution, the position of the mean with reference to the median is :","concept":"The correct answer is left of the median. Skewness describes the asymmetry of a distribution. In a negatively (left) skewed distribution, the long tail points toward the low (left) end of the scale. Extreme low scores in that tail pull the arithmetic mean downward more than they move the median, because the mean is sensitive to every value while the median depends only on rank position. The result is the ordering mean less than median less than mode. So the mean sits to the left of the median. The mirror image holds for a positively (right) skewed distribution, where the ordering is mode less than median less than mean, placing the mean to the right. This relationship among the three measures of central tendency was popularized through the empirical work of Karl Pearson, who also defined coefficients of skewness using the gap between mean and mode or mean and median. Understanding it lets you infer the shape of a distribution from summary statistics alone, which is why it is a staple of psychology statistics papers.","traps":{"b":"Right of the median describes a positively skewed distribution, where the tail extends toward high values.","c":"Mean equals median only in a symmetric distribution, not a skewed one.","d":"In negative skew the mode is the highest of the three, so equating mean with mode is incorrect."}},{"q":"The correct sequence of the four stages of creative thinking, as proposed by Graham Wallas, is:","concept":"The answer is preparation, incubation, illumination, verification, because that is the exact four-stage sequence of the creative process as described by Graham Wallas in his 1926 book The Art of Thought. Understand each stage so the order becomes logical rather than memorized. Preparation is the first stage, the deliberate, conscious work of defining the problem, gathering relevant information, and attempting solutions through effortful analysis; the mind loads itself with the raw materials. Incubation follows, a period in which the person stops actively working on the problem and turns attention elsewhere, allowing unconscious processing to continue in the background; this is why solutions often surface during a walk, a shower, or sleep rather than at the desk. Illumination, sometimes called the insight or aha moment, is the third stage, the sudden emergence of the solution or key idea into consciousness, often experienced as instantaneous and effortless precisely because the unconscious work of incubation prepared it. Verification is the final stage, in which the insight is consciously tested, evaluated, refined, and elaborated to confirm it actually works and to render it usable. The logic of the ordering is a rhythm of conscious effort, then unconscious rest, then sudden insight, then conscious checking, so preparation must come first and verification last, with incubation necessarily preceding illumination. Some later writers insert a fifth stage, but Wallas's canonical four in the order preparation, incubation, illumination, verification is the exam answer, and the name to attach is Graham Wallas.","traps":{"a":"This order places illumination before incubation, which reverses the true sequence; insight (illumination) cannot precede the unconscious mulling (incubation) that generates it.","b":"This sequence starts with incubation before preparation, which is impossible because there must first be conscious effort and information-gathering before the mind can incubate on a problem.","d":"This order begins with illumination and ends with incubation, scrambling the stages entirely, since illumination is a late insight moment and cannot be the first step."}},{"q":"In the Rorschach Inkblot Test, a response that is determined primarily by the chromatic color of the blot (e.g., \"this is a blood stain because it's red\") is scored as:","concept":"The answer is C, pure Color, because a Rorschach (Hermann Rorschach, 1921) response determined primarily by the chromatic color of the blot with essentially no contribution from form, such as saying it is a blood stain because it is red, is scored as a pure Color response, coded C. Understand the color determinants in Rorschach scoring, which form a graded series reflecting how much form versus color drives the response, and which are interpreted as indexing emotional control. When a person responds to the colored (chromatic) areas of the inkblots, the scorer distinguishes among several codes based on the relative weight of form and color. FC (Form-Color) means form is the dominant determinant and color is secondary; the response has a definite shape that fits the blot, with color playing a supporting role, for example a green caterpillar where the shape is caterpillar-like and green adds to it. CF (Color-Form) means color is dominant and form is secondary and vaguer, for example some ice cream that is mostly about the color with only loose form. C (pure Color) means the response is based on color alone with no form at all, for example blood because it is red, where nothing about the shape matters, only the redness. Cn (Color Naming) is the extreme case where the person merely names colors without using them to build any percept. Interpretively, this series is read as reflecting the balance between emotional reactivity and rational control: FC suggests well-modulated, controlled affect, while CF and especially pure C suggest more impulsive, poorly regulated emotional responsiveness, since form (associated with cognitive control) drops out. The stem's blood-because-red response, driven purely by chromatic color with no form, is scored pure Color, C.","traps":{"a":"FC (Form-Color) is scored when form is the primary determinant with color contributing secondarily, meaning the shape drives the response and color merely adds to it, unlike a response driven mainly by color.","b":"CF (Color-Form) is scored when color is the primary determinant but form still plays a secondary role, whereas the stem describes a response based almost entirely on color with essentially no form, which is pure color.","d":"Cn (Color Naming) is scored when the person simply names the colors of the blot (saying that's red, that's blue) as their response, rather than using the color to form a percept like a blood stain."}},{"q":"Cyber bullies are individuals who use electronic devices to harass and act aggressively toward other individuals online. Because the internet is an anonymous forum these individuals may say and do things that they would never do in person. They are cruel, hurtful and their behaviour is often reprehensible. What group dynamic does the behaviour of a cyber bully represent?","concept":"The correct answer is deindividuation. Deindividuation is a social psychological state in which individuals lose their sense of individual identity and self-awareness within a group or under conditions of anonymity, leading to a reduction in personal responsibility and self-restraint, which in turn can produce impulsive, antisocial, or aggressive behaviour that the person would normally inhibit. The concept was elaborated by Leon Festinger (who introduced the term with colleagues), Philip Zimbardo, and Edward Diener. Anonymity, diffused responsibility, and high arousal are key contributors: when people feel unidentifiable and less accountable, internal moral controls weaken. Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment and studies showing that anonymity (for example, wearing hoods) increased aggression illustrate the effect. The internet is a powerful engine of deindividuation because it grants anonymity and physical distance, so cyberbullies feel unidentifiable and unaccountable and behave in cruel ways they would never enact face to face, exactly the scenario described. This is sometimes called the online disinhibition effect. The distractors name other group phenomena that do not hinge on loss of identity through anonymity: social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898), group polarisation, and the bystander effect (John Darley and Bibb Latané, 1968). This item tests recognition of deindividuation as the mechanism behind anonymous online aggression.","traps":{"a":"Social facilitation is about arousal from an audience affecting task performance, not about anonymity loosening moral restraint online.","b":"Group polarisation (Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni, 1969) is the shift toward more extreme group attitudes after discussion, not the disinhibited aggression of an anonymous individual.","c":"The bystander effect concerns failure to help in emergencies due to the presence of others, not aggressive behaviour enabled by anonymity."}},{"q":"The concept of \"object relations\" in psychoanalytic theory refers to:","concept":"The answer is the internalized mental representations of self and others and the relationships between them, because that is precisely what object relations refers to in psychoanalytic theory. Understand object relations theory and how it departs from classical Freudian thought (Sigmund Freud). In psychoanalytic terminology, the word object has a specific technical meaning: it refers to the target of one's emotional energy or drives, almost always another person (or a mental image of a person), beginning with the primary caregiver. Object relations theory, developed by a group of largely British theorists including Melanie Klein, W. R. D. Fairbairn, Donald Winnicott, and later Otto Kernberg, shifted the emphasis of psychoanalysis away from Freud's focus on instinctual drives seeking gratification and toward the fundamental human need for relationships. The central claim is that from infancy we internalize our experiences with important others, forming enduring unconscious mental representations, or internal objects, of both other people and of ourselves in relation to them, along with the emotional tone connecting them. These internalized self-and-object representations, laid down in early relationships, form templates that shape how we perceive, feel about, and relate to people throughout life, largely outside awareness. Disturbances in early relationships produce distorted or split internal object relations, which underlie many personality difficulties; for example, Kernberg explained borderline pathology in terms of splitting, the failure to integrate good and bad representations of self and others. So object relations means the internalized mental representations of self and others and the dynamic relationships between them, which is the correct answer.","traps":{"a":"The relationship among the id, ego, and superego is Freud's structural model of the psyche, a different concept from object relations, which concerns internalized representations of self and others.","c":"The use of defense mechanisms to protect the ego from anxiety is Freud's (and Anna Freud's) theory of defenses, not the object relations concept of internalized relational representations.","d":"The stage of psychosexual development focused on the genitals is Freud's phallic (or genital) stage, part of his drive-based developmental theory, unrelated to the object relations idea of mental representations of relationships."}},{"q":"Ria has difficulty enunciating words correctly and speaks in a slow and laboured way; her speech often makes sense, but it includes only keywords. Which part of her brain is damaged?","concept":"The correct answer is Broca's area. Broca's area, located in the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus of the (usually left) frontal lobe, was identified by the French physician Paul Broca in the 1860s through his patient known as Tan, who could understand language but could barely produce speech. Damage to this region causes Broca's aphasia, also called expressive or non-fluent aphasia. Its hallmarks match Ria's presentation exactly: speech is slow, effortful, and poorly articulated, output is reduced to essential content or keywords (agrammatic, telegraphic speech that drops function words like articles and prepositions), yet comprehension is relatively preserved and what the patient does say tends to be meaningful. Patients are often painfully aware of their deficit, which distinguishes it from Wernicke's aphasia. Wernicke's area, in the superior temporal gyrus of the temporal lobe and described by Carl Wernicke, produces the opposite pattern when damaged: fluent, effortless, but empty or nonsensical speech with impaired comprehension. Because Ria's difficulty is in production, effortful and telegraphic but meaningful, the damage localizes to Broca's area, making it the precise answer over the broader frontal lobe option.","traps":{"a":"Wernicke's area damage produces fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension, the opposite of this labored, meaningful pattern.","c":"The temporal lobe houses Wernicke's area, so damage there tends toward comprehension deficits, not effortful telegraphic output.","d":"Frontal lobe is too broad; the specific deficit points to Broca's area within the left frontal lobe."}},{"q":"Thomas and Chess identified nine dimensions of temperament by:","concept":"The correct answer is both interviewing parents using questionnaires and observing babies. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess conducted the landmark New York Longitudinal Study (NYLS), beginning in 1956, which followed infants over many years to study temperament, the biologically based, early-appearing individual differences in behavioural style. From detailed parent interviews and questionnaires about their infants' typical reactions, combined with direct observation of the babies, they identified nine dimensions of temperament: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity), approach or withdrawal to new stimuli, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span or persistence. By clustering these dimensions, Thomas and Chess described three broad temperamental types: the easy child (regular, adaptable, positive mood), the difficult child (irregular, intense, slow to adapt, negative mood), and the slow-to-warm-up child (mild, initially withdrawing but gradually adapting). They also introduced the influential concept of goodness of fit, the match between a child's temperament and environmental demands. Because their methodology combined both parental report and observation of the infants, the inclusive option is correct. This item tests knowledge of the NYLS methodology and Thomas and Chess's temperament framework.","traps":{"a":"Interviewing parents was part of their method, but selecting only this option omits the direct observation of infants they also used.","b":"While clinical experience informed their broader work, the nine dimensions were derived empirically from parent interviews and observation, not primarily from prior studies.","c":"Observing babies was part of their approach, but choosing it alone ignores the parent interviews that were equally central to identifying the dimensions."}},{"q":"A client with OCD washes their hands excessively to neutralize the anxiety caused by intrusive thoughts of contamination. This hand-washing behavior is BEST conceptualized as a(n):","concept":"The answer is an avoidance response or safety behavior, because the compulsive hand-washing functions to reduce and prevent the anxiety triggered by contamination obsessions, and behavior that removes or forestalls an aversive state is an avoidance response maintained by negative reinforcement. Understand the behavioral model of obsessive-compulsive disorder, most influentially the two-process theory applied to OCD by Stanley Rachman and others, building on O. Hobart Mowrer's account of how fears are acquired and maintained. In OCD, an intrusive thought, such as an image of contamination, becomes associated with intense anxiety (this classical-conditioning-like process establishes the obsession as a trigger for distress). The person then discovers that performing a compulsion, here hand-washing, temporarily reduces that anxiety. This is the operant half of the model: because the compulsion is followed by relief from an aversive state (the drop in anxiety), it is strengthened through negative reinforcement, the removal of an unpleasant stimulus increasing the behavior. The compulsion thus becomes an avoidance or safety behavior, an action performed to escape or prevent feared consequences and the accompanying distress. The tragic paradox is that this relief is short-lived and the behavior is self-perpetuating: by neutralizing the anxiety, the compulsion prevents the person from ever learning that the feared catastrophe would not have happened anyway, so the obsession is never disconfirmed and the cycle strengthens. This is exactly why the treatment of choice, exposure and response prevention, deliberately blocks the safety behavior so the anxiety can extinguish naturally. The hand-washing is best conceptualized as an avoidance or safety behavior sustained by negative reinforcement.","traps":{"a":"A conditioned stimulus is a previously neutral cue that, through pairing, comes to elicit a conditioned response; the hand-washing is an active operant behavior the person performs, not a stimulus that triggers a reflexive response.","b":"An unconditioned response is an automatic, unlearned reflex elicited by an unconditioned stimulus, whereas compulsive hand-washing is a deliberate, learned, goal-directed action, not an innate reflex.","d":"Positive reinforcement would mean the hand-washing is maintained by adding something pleasant; in fact the compulsion is maintained by removing or preventing an aversive state (anxiety), which is negative reinforcement, not positive."}},{"q":"Choose the correct arrangement of these stages of elevated mood based upon severity: i) Elation, ii) Exaltation, iii) Euphoria, iv) Ecstasy.","concept":"The correct ascending order of elevated mood by severity is euphoria, elation, exaltation, and ecstasy, which corresponds to sequence iii, i, ii, iv, making b the right answer. This question tests the descriptive psychopathology of mood elevation, a graded continuum used especially in describing mania and hypomania. At the mildest end is euphoria, an exaggerated sense of well-being and contentment that may still seem within normal bounds. Next is elation, a more pronounced and infectious feeling of joy and heightened confidence accompanied by increased activity and optimism. More severe is exaltation, an intense elevation of mood often coloured by grandiosity, in which the person may feel exceptionally powerful, important, or expansive. The most extreme degree is ecstasy, a state of overwhelming, transcendent rapture or bliss, frequently associated with a sense of mystical union or being in contact with the divine, and seen in severe mania and certain psychotic or seizure-related states. Recognising this hierarchy matters clinically because the degree of mood elevation helps gauge the severity of a manic episode and the likelihood of accompanying psychotic features and impaired insight. The trick in the question is that the four terms are listed out of order, so the candidate must reorder them from least to most intense rather than accept the given sequence.","traps":{"a":"i, ii, iii and iv (elation, exaltation, euphoria, ecstasy) is wrong because it places euphoria third when euphoria is actually the mildest and should come first.","c":"iv, iii, ii and i (ecstasy, euphoria, exaltation, elation) is a descending and internally jumbled order, not the correct ascending severity sequence.","d":"None of the above is incorrect because option b correctly captures the ascending severity order, so a valid answer exists."}},{"q":"‘Reinforcement will have positive effect on performance,’ which type of hypothesis is this?","concept":"The correct answer is alternate and directional. In hypothesis testing, researchers distinguish between the null hypothesis and the alternate (alternative) hypothesis, and between directional and non-directional forms. The null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or no relationship, for example that reinforcement has no effect on performance. The alternate hypothesis (H1) states that there is an effect or relationship. The statement 'Reinforcement will have a positive effect on performance' asserts that an effect exists, so it is an alternate hypothesis, not a null one. Furthermore, it specifies the direction of that effect: not merely that reinforcement affects performance, but that it improves it (a positive effect). When a hypothesis specifies the direction of the expected effect (such as 'greater than,' 'less than,' 'improves,' or 'reduces'), it is a directional hypothesis, tested with a one-tailed test. When it merely predicts a difference or relationship without specifying which way it will go (such as 'reinforcement will affect performance'), it is non-directional, tested with a two-tailed test. Because this statement both claims an effect (alternate) and specifies that the effect is positive (directional), it is an alternate and directional hypothesis. This is a standard research-methodology classification.","traps":{"a":"Null and directional is wrong because the statement predicts an effect (so it is alternate, not null), even though it is directional.","c":"Null and non-directional is wrong on both counts: it predicts an effect (alternate) and specifies a positive direction (directional).","d":"Alternate and non-directional is half right (alternate) but wrong on direction, since the statement does specify a positive direction."}},{"q":"\"Escape learning\" differs from \"avoidance learning\" in that, in escape learning, the behavior:","concept":"The answer is that in escape learning the behavior terminates an aversive stimulus that is already present, because this is precisely what distinguishes escape learning from avoidance learning. Understand both within the framework of negative reinforcement and O. Hobart Mowrer's two-process theory. Both escape and avoidance are forms of negatively reinforced behavior, meaning the behavior is strengthened by the removal or non-occurrence of something aversive, and the key difference lies in timing relative to the aversive event. In escape learning, the aversive stimulus is already occurring, and the behavior ends it. For example, a rat in a chamber where the floor is being electrified learns to jump to the other side to stop the shock it is currently feeling; the shock is present and the response escapes it. In avoidance learning, the behavior occurs before the aversive stimulus begins and prevents its onset altogether, usually cued by a warning signal. For instance, if a light comes on a few seconds before the shock, the rat learns to jump when the light appears, thereby avoiding the shock entirely so it never occurs. Mowrer's two-process theory explains avoidance as involving both classical conditioning (the warning signal becomes a conditioned stimulus that elicits fear) and operant conditioning (the avoidance response is negatively reinforced by the reduction of that conditioned fear). Developmentally, avoidance often grows out of escape: the organism first learns to escape the aversive event, then learns to respond to the warning signal to avoid it. Because the stem asks what escape learning specifically involves, the correct feature is terminating an aversive stimulus that is already present.","traps":{"a":"Preventing the onset of an aversive stimulus before it occurs defines avoidance learning, not escape learning; the distinction hinges on whether the aversive event has already begun.","c":"Escape learning is maintained by negative reinforcement (removal of an ongoing aversive stimulus), not by positive reinforcement (adding a pleasant stimulus), so this mischaracterizes the reinforcement mechanism.","d":"Being under the control of a discriminative stimulus describes stimulus control in operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner) generally and is characteristic of the warning-signal in avoidance learning, not the defining feature that separates escape from avoidance."}},{"q":"Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM) measure which kind of intelligence?","concept":"The correct answer is fluid intelligence. Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices (SPM), developed by John C. Raven in 1938, is a nonverbal test in which the examinee identifies the missing element that completes a visual pattern or matrix, with items increasing in difficulty. It is designed to measure fluid intelligence, the ability to reason, perceive relationships, and solve novel problems independently of previously acquired knowledge or education. This maps onto the Gf-Gc distinction introduced by Raymond Cattell and elaborated by John Horn: fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity for abstract reasoning and problem-solving with novel material, whereas crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the accumulated knowledge, vocabulary, and skills gained through experience and education. Because the matrices rely on abstract visual reasoning rather than language or learned facts, they are considered relatively culture-fair (or culture-reduced) and are used cross-culturally. The test was also historically important as a strong marker of Spearman's g, the general intelligence factor identified by Charles Spearman, and the SPM does correlate highly with general intelligence. However, its specific and intended construct is fluid reasoning, not crystallised knowledge. Because Raven's Progressive Matrices most precisely measure fluid intelligence, option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"Crystallised intelligence reflects acquired knowledge and vocabulary, which the culture-reduced matrices deliberately avoid tapping.","c":"Because the matrices target reasoning rather than learned knowledge, they do not measure crystallised intelligence, so both is wrong.","d":"While the SPM correlates highly with general intelligence, its specific target is the fluid reasoning component."}},{"q":"The bystander effect, where the presence of others inhibits helping, is primarily explained by two factors:","concept":"The answer is diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance, because these are the two classic mechanisms that Bibb Latane and John Darley identified as producing the bystander effect. Understand the phenomenon and the research behind it. The bystander effect is the counterintuitive finding that the more people who are present at an emergency, the less likely any single individual is to help. Latane and Darley investigated this after the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese, and through a series of ingenious experiments they isolated the psychological processes responsible. The first is diffusion of responsibility. When many bystanders are present, the felt personal responsibility to act is divided among all of them, so each individual feels less obligated to intervene, reasoning that someone else will or should do it; when a person is the sole witness, all the responsibility falls on them and they are far more likely to help. The second is pluralistic ignorance, a process operating during the interpretation of the situation. In an ambiguous emergency, each bystander looks to the reactions of others to decide whether the situation is truly an emergency; because everyone is trying to appear calm and is simultaneously looking to others, each person concludes from the others' apparent calm that nothing is wrong, so the group collectively misreads the emergency as a non-emergency. Latane and Darley formalized these ideas in a decision model of helping with sequential steps, noticing the event, interpreting it as an emergency, assuming responsibility, knowing how to help, and deciding to act. The two processes that inhibit helping when others are present are diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance.","traps":{"a":"Social loafing (reduced individual effort in groups) and deindividuation (loss of self-awareness in crowds) are group-influence phenomena but are not the two mechanisms Latane and Darley identified as driving the bystander effect.","c":"Social comparison (evaluating oneself against others) and normative influence (conforming to be accepted) are conformity-related processes, not the specific pair explaining bystander inaction in emergencies.","d":"Group polarization (groups adopting more extreme positions) and groupthink (faulty consensus-seeking in cohesive groups) concern group decision-making, not the failure of individuals to help when others are present."}},{"q":"The basic assumption of Adler's individual psychology is","concept":"The correct answer is: human behaviour is purposeful and goal directed.. Behaviour is purposeful and goal directed. This teleology is the core of Alfred Adler: people are pulled by future goals (striving for superiority, fictional finalism) rather than only pushed by past drives. Optimism (b) and social embeddedness/social interest (c) are Adlerian flavours but not the defining basic assumption, and (d) is Erik Erikson's lifespan/ego-identity view.","traps":{"b":"An optimistic view is a feature of Adler's tone, not the foundational assumption of the theory.","c":"Social interest matters to Adler, but the BASIC assumption is the goal-directed, purposive nature of behaviour.","d":"Lifelong development with ego identity central is Erik Erikson's model, not Adler's."}},{"q":"The brain region most critically involved in processing the prosody or emotional tone of speech (speech intonation) is typically:","concept":"The answer is right-hemisphere regions homotopic to the language areas, because the emotional prosody of speech, its melodic intonation and emotional tone, is processed chiefly by regions of the right hemisphere that mirror, or are homotopic to, the classical language areas of the left hemisphere. Understand the concept of hemispheric specialization and homotopic regions. In most people the left hemisphere is dominant for the propositional or literal aspects of language, the phonology, grammar, and lexical meaning handled by Broca's area (production; Paul Broca) and Wernicke's area (comprehension; Carl Wernicke). But language carries more than literal content; the same sentence can convey very different meanings and emotions through its prosody, the pitch, rhythm, stress, and melody of speech that signal whether a statement is a question, sarcastic, angry, joyful, or sad. Research, notably by Elliott Ross who described the aprosodias, showed that this emotional and affective prosody is largely a right-hemisphere function, and specifically it is processed by areas in the right hemisphere that are the mirror-image counterparts of the left-hemisphere language areas; these mirror-image regions are called homotopic areas (homotopic meaning occupying corresponding positions in the two hemispheres). Thus damage to the right-hemisphere region homotopic to Broca's area produces motor aprosodia, an inability to produce emotional intonation, so speech sounds flat and monotone, while damage to the right-hemisphere region homotopic to Wernicke's area produces sensory aprosodia, difficulty comprehending the emotional tone in others' speech. This elegant left-right division means the left hemisphere handles what is said while the right handles how it is said emotionally. The correct answer is therefore the right-hemisphere regions homotopic to the language areas.","traps":{"a":"Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus handles speech production and grammar, not the emotional melody or intonation of speech, which is a right-hemisphere function.","c":"The primary motor cortex controls voluntary muscle movements, including those of articulation in a purely motor sense, but it does not process the emotional prosody or tonal meaning of speech.","d":"The occipital lobe is dedicated to visual processing and has no role in analyzing the emotional tone of spoken language."}},{"q":"At which stage, according to Kohlberg's theory of moral development, does the individual view laws as instruments for expressing the will of the majority and furthering welfare?","concept":"The keyed answer is the social contract orientation. Lawrence Kohlberg proposed that moral reasoning develops through three broad levels, each with two stages, defined by the kind of reasoning behind a judgment rather than the content of the judgment. The preconventional level (stages 1 and 2) judges right and wrong by consequences to the self, avoiding punishment and seeking reward. The conventional level (stages 3 and 4) judges by social approval and then by maintaining law and order for its own sake. The postconventional or principled level (stages 5 and 6) reasons from abstract ethical principles. Stage 5, the social contract orientation, is exactly the view the question describes: laws are understood not as fixed and sacred but as social contracts, instruments agreed upon to express the will of the majority and to maximise welfare, and therefore as changeable through democratic process when they fail to serve the common good. Stage 6, the morality of individual conscience, goes further to universal ethical principles that may override unjust laws. So the phrase laws as instruments for expressing the will of the majority and furthering welfare is the textbook signature of stage 5. The broader exam point is that Kohlberg's progression moves from self interest, through conformity and rule following, to a flexible, principle based understanding of justice.","traps":{"a":"Punishment and obedience orientation is stage 1, the most basic preconventional reasoning that obeys to avoid punishment, far below reasoning about majority welfare.","b":"Social order maintaining morality is stage 4, which obeys fixed laws to preserve order for its own sake, not because laws are revisable instruments of welfare.","c":"Morality of individual principles of conscience is stage 6, governed by self chosen universal principles that can override laws, a step beyond the social contract idea."}},{"q":"The behavioral technique that involves removing a desirable stimulus following an undesirable behavior in order to reduce the frequency of that behavior is called:","concept":"The answer is response cost because it is precisely defined as the removal of a desirable or reinforcing stimulus following an unwanted behaviour, in order to decrease that behaviour. Response cost is a form of negative punishment within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning framework, and understanding the two-by-two logic of operant consequences makes it clear. Operant consequences are classified by two questions: does the consequence increase or decrease the behaviour, and does it involve adding (positive) or removing (negative) a stimulus. Reinforcement always increases behaviour, punishment always decreases it; positive means a stimulus is presented, negative means a stimulus is taken away. So negative punishment means decreasing a behaviour by taking away something the person values. Response cost is the everyday name for this: fining someone money for speeding, deducting points or privileges, or taking tokens away in a token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) after misbehaviour. A closely related negative-punishment procedure is time-out, which removes access to a reinforcing environment. The distractors each occupy a different cell of the framework: negative reinforcement removes an aversive to increase behaviour, positive punishment adds an aversive to decrease behaviour, and extinction is a separate process of withholding the reinforcement that maintained a behaviour so it gradually fades. Because the question describes taking away a pleasant stimulus to reduce a behaviour, that is response cost.","traps":{"a":"Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus in order to increase a behaviour, which is the opposite of removing something pleasant to decrease a behaviour.","c":"Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus after a behaviour to reduce it; it involves adding something unpleasant, not removing something desirable.","d":"Extinction reduces a behaviour by no longer providing the reinforcement that previously maintained it; it withholds an expected reward rather than removing a separate desirable stimulus as a penalty."}},{"q":"We know that a distinction is made between positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia. What is the difference between these symptoms?","concept":"The correct answer is that positive symptoms indicate the presence of something unusual, and negative symptoms indicate the absence of something normal. In schizophrenia, symptoms are grouped into two broad categories based on whether they add something abnormal or take away something normal, a distinction refined by researchers such as Timothy Crow and Nancy Andreasen. Positive symptoms are pathological excesses or additions: they reflect the presence of experiences that should not be there, such as hallucinations (perceiving things that are not present), delusions (fixed false beliefs), and disorganised speech and thought. The word positive here means added, not good. Negative symptoms are pathological deficits: they reflect the absence or reduction of normal functions, such as flat or blunted affect (reduced emotional expression), avolition (loss of motivation), alogia (poverty of speech), and social withdrawal. The word negative means taken away, not bad. Option c captures this precisely, while the other options either reverse the definitions or muddle presence and absence. This distinction matters clinically because positive and negative symptoms respond differently to treatment: antipsychotic medications, especially older ones, are more effective against positive symptoms, whereas negative symptoms are often more persistent and harder to treat, and they strongly predict long-term disability and functional outcome.","traps":{"a":"This option is internally inconsistent and does not correctly define positive symptoms at all.","b":"This wrongly frames positive symptoms as the presence of something normal, when they are the presence of something abnormal.","d":"This reverses the definitions entirely, wrongly assigning absence to positive and presence to negative symptoms."}},{"q":"In experimental psychology, what does a p-value represent when using biostatistical tests?","concept":"The p-value, central to null hypothesis significance testing developed by Ronald Fisher and formalized alongside the framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, is defined as the probability of obtaining results at least as extreme as those observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true. It is a conditional probability: it is computed under the assumption that the null (usually of no effect or no difference) holds, and it quantifies how surprising the data would be under that assumption. A small p-value (conventionally below 0.05) means the observed data would be unlikely if the null were true, providing evidence to reject the null. The most common error, captured by distractor a, is to read the p-value as the probability that the null hypothesis is true; it is not, because it presupposes the null rather than evaluating it directly. Distractor c confuses it with effect size, and d with sample size. Because the p-value is the probability of the observed (or more extreme) data given a true null hypothesis, option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"This is the single most common misinterpretation: the p-value is NOT the probability that the null hypothesis is true; it is conditioned on the null being true.","c":"Magnitude of correlation is an effect size (such as r), a different quantity entirely from a p-value.","d":"Sample size is a design parameter, not what a p-value represents."}},{"q":"The representativeness heuristic can lead to errors when :","concept":"The correct answer is individuals ignore base rates and focus solely on similarity. The representativeness heuristic, identified by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, is the shortcut of judging the probability that an object or event belongs to a category by how closely it resembles the typical or prototypical member of that category. Its most famous failure mode is base-rate neglect: people focus on how representative or similar a case seems and ignore the prior probability, or base rate, of the category in the population. For instance, in the classic Linda problem and the lawyer-engineer studies, participants judged category membership by stereotype fit while disregarding how common each category actually was, leading to systematic errors including the conjunction fallacy. Because the defining error of representativeness is overweighting similarity and underweighting base rates, option A is correct. Relying on readily available information (option B) describes the availability heuristic, a different shortcut based on ease of recall. Failing to consider alternative explanations (option C) is a broad reasoning shortcoming, not the specific mechanism by which representativeness misleads. Adjusting judgements based on initial estimates (option D) describes the anchoring and adjustment heuristic, in which an initial anchor unduly influences the final judgement. The signature pitfall of representativeness is precisely the neglect of base rates in favour of resemblance. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","traps":{"b":"Relying on readily available information describes the availability heuristic, not the representativeness heuristic.","c":"Failing to consider alternative explanations is a general reasoning flaw, not the specific base-rate neglect of representativeness.","d":"Adjusting judgements from initial estimates describes the anchoring and adjustment heuristic, a different shortcut."}},{"q":"Which of the following refers to concurrent validity?","concept":"The correct answer is the notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute (assessed at the same time). Concurrent validity is a subtype of criterion-related validity, the family of validity concerned with how well a test's scores correspond to an external criterion. Criterion validity has two forms distinguished by timing. Predictive validity assesses how well a test predicts a criterion measured in the future (for example, whether an aptitude test predicts later job performance). Concurrent validity assesses how well a test correlates with an established criterion or an existing validated measure of the same construct assessed at roughly the same point in time. For instance, a new, shorter depression scale shows concurrent validity if its scores correlate highly with scores on an already-established depression measure administered concurrently. The essence is agreement with a criterion in the present. Option A is a trap because it takes 'concurrent' too literally as merely testing simultaneously, without the crucial requirement of correlation with a valid criterion measuring the same attribute. Option B describes inter-rater reliability, and option C describes internal consistency reliability, both of which are reliability rather than validity. This item tests the correct definition of concurrent validity as present-time correlation with a criterion measure of the same construct.","traps":{"a":"Merely administering two tests at the same time is a superficial reading of the word 'concurrent'; concurrent validity requires that the test correlate with an established criterion measured at the same time, not just simultaneous testing.","b":"Agreement among clinicians is inter-rater reliability, not concurrent validity.","c":"Items consistently relating to each other is internal consistency reliability, not validity."}},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of \"self-esteem\" in contrast to \"self-concept\"?","concept":"The answer is that self-esteem is the evaluative and affective component of the self, how one feels about oneself, while self-concept is the cognitive description of who one is, because this correctly distinguishes the two closely related constructs. Understand the structure of the self in psychology, where theorists separate the descriptive from the evaluative. The self can be analyzed into components that answer different questions. Self-concept answers the question who am I; it is the cognitive component, the organized set of beliefs, descriptions, and knowledge a person holds about their own attributes, roles, values, physical characteristics, and abilities. It is essentially a mental self-portrait or self-schema (Hazel Markus, 1977), for example I am a student, I am tall, I am a sister, I play the guitar, statements of fact and description without necessarily any judgment of good or bad attached. Self-esteem answers a different question, how do I feel about who I am; it is the evaluative and affective component, the overall judgment of one's own worth and the emotions, pride or shame, satisfaction or self-dislike, that accompany that judgment. Self-esteem is whether a person likes and values themselves, high or low, positive or negative. The two are linked but distinct: the self-concept provides the content that self-esteem evaluates, so a person can have a detailed self-concept (I am short and shy) and then feel positively or negatively about those descriptions, and that feeling is self-esteem. William James early on connected self-esteem to the ratio of successes to pretensions or aspirations. The correct distinction, then, is that self-concept is the cognitive description of who one is and self-esteem is the affective evaluation of one's worth.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the two definitions, calling self-esteem the cognitive appraisal and self-concept the evaluative component, which is backwards; self-concept is the cognitive description and self-esteem is the evaluative feeling.","c":"Treating self-esteem and self-concept as identical and interchangeable is incorrect because they are distinct constructs: one is the cognitive content of the self and the other is the affective evaluation of it.","d":"Defining self-esteem as the collective self in relation to others and self-concept as the individual self confuses these constructs with the personal-versus-collective identity distinction, which is a different framework altogether."}},{"q":"Kelly is to the Rep test as Cattell is to the","concept":"The correct answer is the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF). This analogy item asks you to match each theorist with the assessment instrument they created. George Kelly, the founder of personal construct theory, developed the Role Construct Repertory Test (the Rep test or Rep grid), a technique for eliciting the bipolar personal constructs an individual uses to interpret and anticipate their world; by comparing and contrasting significant people in their life, the person reveals the dimensions of meaning through which they construe experience. Analogously, Raymond Cattell developed the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16 PF), a self-report personality inventory built through factor analysis to measure sixteen primary source traits (such as warmth, reasoning, emotional stability, dominance, and vigilance) that Cattell derived as the fundamental dimensions of personality. So just as the Rep test is Kelly's characteristic instrument, the 16 PF is Cattell's. The distractors are all real inventories but attributable to other authors: the California Psychological Inventory to Harrison Gough, the Q-sort method to William Stephenson (and used clinically by Carl Rogers to study the self-concept and the real-ideal self discrepancy), and the MMPI to Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley. Because Cattell's instrument is the 16 PF, option c correctly completes the analogy.","traps":{"a":"The California Psychological Inventory was developed by Harrison Gough, not Cattell.","b":"The Q-sort method is associated with William Stephenson and used by Carl Rogers, not with Cattell.","d":"The MMPI was developed by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, not by Cattell."}},{"q":"According to Karl Lashley's research on memory storage, where did he conclude that memories are primarily stored in the brain?","concept":"The answer is distributed throughout the entire cortex because Karl Lashley, after decades of experiments searching for the physical location of the memory trace (which he called the engram), concluded that memories are not stored in one specific spot but are spread across the cortex. Lashley trained rats to run mazes and then surgically removed varying amounts of cortical tissue from different locations, expecting to find the one region that, when destroyed, would erase the maze memory. He never found it. Instead he discovered two principles. The first is mass action: the impairment of learning and memory was proportional to the amount of cortex removed, not to which particular area was removed, meaning the cortex acts as a whole and more damage means more deficit. The second is equipotentiality: within a functional area, any part can, up to a point, take over the job of a damaged part, so no single region holds a given memory exclusively. Together these implied the engram is distributed rather than localised. This work was foundational even though later research refined it, showing that specific structures like the hippocampus (from patient H.M.) and specific circuits do have specialised memory roles. For the exam, tie Lashley's name to mass action, equipotentiality, and distributed cortical storage of memory.","traps":{"a":"Strict localization of specific memories in specific spots is exactly what Lashley's experiments argued against; he could not find any single storage site.","c":"Lashley worked with cortical lesions in rats and pointed to distributed cortical storage; his conclusion was not about the hippocampus, whose memory role was established later.","d":"His principle of mass action implicated the cortex as a whole, not just the frontal and temporal lobes."}},{"q":"Jean Piaget's theory of moral development suggests that children in the stage of \"heteronomous morality\" (typically ages 5-10) base their moral judgments on:","concept":"The answer is that heteronomous children judge morality by treating rules as unchangeable and by focusing on the consequences of actions, because that is exactly how Jean Piaget characterized the first stage of moral development. Understand Piaget's two-stage theory of moral reasoning, which he derived from studying children playing marbles and responding to moral stories. The word heteronomous means being governed by others, and heteronomous morality, also called moral realism, dominates roughly ages five to ten. In this stage the child sees rules as fixed, sacred, absolute, and handed down by authority figures such as parents or God, and therefore unchangeable; rules simply exist and must be obeyed. Two features define the child's judgments here. First, morality is judged by objective consequences rather than intentions, so a child who accidentally breaks fifteen cups is judged naughtier than one who breaks a single cup while deliberately stealing jam, because the physical damage is greater. Second, the child believes in immanent justice, the idea that wrongdoing is automatically and inevitably punished by the world itself. Piaget contrasted this with autonomous morality, or moral relativism, emerging around age ten, when the child understands that rules are social agreements that can be changed by consent, and begins judging acts by the actor's intentions rather than raw outcomes. The stem's two clues, rules as unchangeable and focus on consequences, are the twin signatures of heteronomous morality, and note that the distractors about social contracts and abstract justice belong to Lawrence Kohlberg, not Piaget.","traps":{"a":"Judging by intentions and valuing equality belongs to the later stage of autonomous morality (moral relativism), when children realize rules are human agreements and weigh the actor's motives, not to heteronomous morality.","c":"Social contracts and individual rights describe a high level of Lawrence Kohlberg's postconventional morality, a different theorist and a far more advanced stage than Piagetian heteronomous morality.","d":"Abstract principles of justice describe Kohlberg's highest postconventional stage of universal ethical principles, again from a different framework and well beyond the concrete rule-bound thinking of the young child."}},{"q":"Which behavioral technique involves imagining a scene where an undesirable behavior is paired with an unpleasant consequence to reduce the behavior?","concept":"The answer is covert sensitization because it is precisely the technique in which an undesirable behaviour is imagined and then paired, in imagination, with an unpleasant or aversive consequence in order to reduce that behaviour. Developed by Joseph Cautela in the 1960s, covert sensitization is a form of aversion therapy, but the word 'covert' is the key clue: everything happens internally, in the client's imagination, rather than using real aversive stimuli like actual nausea-inducing drugs or electric shocks. A classic application is with problem drinking or unwanted sexual or eating behaviours: the therapist has the client vividly imagine engaging in the target behaviour and then imagine a strongly aversive outcome such as becoming violently nauseous and vomiting. Through repeated pairing, the behaviour itself begins to evoke the aversive feeling, so its appeal diminishes, an application of classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) to internal imagery. The distractors are all exposure-based anxiety treatments with a different aim, and distinguishing them is the exam skill: flooding is intense real exposure, implosion is intense imagined exposure, and systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe) is gradual exposure paired with relaxation. All three reduce fear through exposure, whereas covert sensitization reduces an unwanted behaviour by pairing it with an imagined aversive consequence. The cue words 'imagine' plus 'unpleasant consequence to reduce the behaviour' point to covert sensitization.","traps":{"a":"Flooding is real-life or intense direct exposure to a feared stimulus for a prolonged time until anxiety subsides; it uses actual exposure, not imagined aversive pairing.","b":"Implosion (implosive therapy) uses vivid imagined exposure to feared cues to extinguish anxiety, but its goal is anxiety reduction through exposure, not pairing a behaviour with an aversive consequence.","d":"Systematic desensitization pairs deep relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared stimuli to reduce anxiety; it uses relaxation and gradual exposure, not an unpleasant consequence."}},{"q":"The chance of developing schizophrenia if you have an identical twin with the disorder is approximately:","concept":"The correct answer is approximately 50 percent. Schizophrenia has a strong genetic component, and twin studies provide the clearest evidence for it. The monozygotic (identical) twin concordance rate for schizophrenia is around 40 to 50 percent, meaning that if one identical twin has schizophrenia, the other has roughly a 50 percent chance of also developing it. This figure, drawn from classic twin research including work reviewed by Irving Gottesman, is far higher than the roughly 1 percent lifetime risk in the general population and higher than the roughly 17 percent concordance in dizygotic (fraternal) twins or about 9 to 13 percent risk in a sibling or child of an affected person. The crucial interpretive point is that because identical twins share 100 percent of their genes yet the concordance is only about 50 percent rather than 100 percent, genetics alone cannot fully explain the disorder. This supports the diathesis-stress model, in which a genetic vulnerability (diathesis) interacts with environmental stressors (such as prenatal factors, cannabis use, or psychosocial stress) to trigger onset. The distractors correspond to the general-population risk, first-degree relative risk, and an implausibly high near-deterministic figure. This item tests knowledge of twin concordance and the gene-environment interaction in schizophrenia.","traps":{"a":"1 percent is the lifetime risk in the general population, not the concordance for an identical twin of an affected person.","b":"About 10 percent is the risk for a first-degree relative such as a sibling or child, not for a monozygotic co-twin.","c":"80 percent overstates the concordance; if it were that high, genetics would be nearly deterministic, contradicting the well-established role of environment."}},{"q":"Which of the following statements about the F-distribution is false?","concept":"The correct answer is that 'negative values of F are associated with a higher probability value' is FALSE, because F can never be negative. The F-distribution, named after Ronald Aylmer Fisher, is the sampling distribution of the ratio of two variance estimates (mean squares) in analysis of variance. Because it is a ratio of two variances, and variances are computed from squared deviations, both the numerator and denominator are always non-negative, so the F statistic itself can only range from 0 to positive infinity. Negative F values are mathematically impossible, which makes the statement about negative F values with higher probability simply false. The other three statements are all true properties of the F-distribution. It is asymmetrical, specifically positively skewed (skewed to the right), with the exact shape determined by two separate degrees-of-freedom parameters for the numerator and denominator. It is one-tailed in application: because larger between-groups variance relative to within-groups variance drives F upward, the region of rejection lies only in the upper tail. And when the between-groups (between-cells) variance equals the within-groups (within-subjects) variance, the ratio equals exactly 1.00, the value expected under the null hypothesis of no group differences. This item tests knowledge of the mathematical properties of the F-distribution.","traps":{"a":"This is true: the F-distribution is positively skewed and asymmetrical, so it is not the false statement.","b":"This is true: F tests are inherently one-tailed because F reflects a ratio of variances that grows only in one direction, so it is not false.","d":"This is true: when between-groups and within-groups variances are equal, their ratio F equals 1.00, which is a correct statement."}},{"q":"Which of the following is most effective in conditioning?","concept":"The correct answer is delayed conditioning, which is generally the most effective arrangement for forming a strong conditioned response in classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov). The effectiveness of conditioning depends heavily on the temporal relationship between the conditioned stimulus (CS) and the unconditioned stimulus (UCS). In delayed conditioning, the CS is presented first and remains on (or its onset precedes the UCS) and overlaps with the UCS, so that the CS is still present when the UCS arrives. This arrangement is considered the most effective because the CS reliably signals and predicts the coming UCS while the two are paired in time, allowing the strongest associative link to form. Compare the alternatives along this timing dimension. In trace conditioning the CS begins and ends before the UCS appears, leaving a gap or trace in between, which weakens learning because the organism must hold the CS in memory. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS occur at exactly the same instant, so the CS has no predictive value (it does not warn of anything) and conditioning is weak. In backward conditioning the UCS comes before the CS, which is the least effective of all because the CS cannot predict an event that has already happened. The unifying principle is predictiveness: the CS must reliably precede and signal the UCS, and delayed conditioning maximises this signalling while preserving close temporal pairing, which is why it produces the most robust learning.","traps":{"a":"In backward conditioning the unconditioned stimulus precedes the conditioned stimulus, so the CS cannot predict the UCS at all; this is generally the least effective arrangement, not the best.","c":"In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS occur at the same time, giving the CS no warning or predictive value, which makes learning weak rather than strong.","d":"In trace conditioning the CS ends before the UCS begins, leaving a temporal gap that must be bridged by memory, producing weaker conditioning than the overlapping delayed arrangement."}},{"q":"Construct validity is a notion that refers to:","concept":"The correct answer is the level to which the theoretical construct is related to other similar measures (that is, how well the test represents and behaves like the underlying theoretical construct). Construct validity is the most fundamental and overarching form of validity, formalised in the influential work of Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl. It concerns the degree to which a test truly measures the abstract theoretical construct (such as intelligence, anxiety, or self-esteem) that it is intended to measure, given that such constructs are hypothetical and cannot be observed directly. Construct validity is established by accumulating evidence that the test relates to other variables in ways predicted by theory. It has two key components: convergent validity, where the test correlates strongly with other measures of the same or related constructs (option A actually describes convergent validity specifically), and discriminant (divergent) validity, where the test does not correlate with measures of unrelated constructs. Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske developed the multitrait-multimethod matrix to assess both together. Because option B captures the broad idea of how the construct relates to other similar measures, it is the best fit for the overall notion of construct validity, whereas option A is a subtype, and options C and D describe reliability (internal consistency and consistency of results). This item tests the definition of construct validity.","traps":{"a":"High correlation with tests of the same attribute specifically describes convergent validity (a component of construct validity), which is narrower than construct validity as a whole.","c":"Items consistently relating to each other describes internal consistency reliability, not validity.","d":"Producing consistent results describes reliability in general, not construct validity."}},{"q":"Some students experience an intense period of adaptation when they leave their old school environment and start college, especially during those initial days before they find companions, start to fit in and feel comfortable. This process is best described as:","concept":"The correct answer is re-socialisation. Socialisation is the lifelong sociological process by which individuals learn the norms, values, and behaviours of their society. Re-socialisation refers to the process of shedding former behaviour patterns and adopting new ones as part of a transition into a new social environment, role, or stage of life. When students leave a familiar school setting and enter college, they must unlearn old routines and learn the new expectations, culture, and social norms of the college, which is precisely re-socialisation. The concept is associated with sociologist Erving Goffman, who studied intense re-socialisation in total institutions, though it also applies to milder everyday transitions like starting college, a new job, or moving countries. The distractors reference other self and identity concepts: the looking-glass self from Charles Horton Cooley describes how we build self-image from others' perceived judgments, self-awareness is a state of self-focused attention, and institutionalisation is the loss of independence within a controlling institution. The item tests whether the student can distinguish the socialisation processes and correctly label adaptation to a new environment as re-socialisation.","traps":{"a":"The looking-glass self (Charles Horton Cooley) is about forming self-concept from how we imagine others see us, not about adapting to a new social setting.","b":"Self-awareness is simply attending to oneself as an object of attention; it does not capture the process of learning new norms in a new environment.","c":"Institutionalisation refers to becoming dependent on the routines of a total institution (like long-term hospitalisation or prison), not to a normal transition into college life."}},{"q":"The cognitive strategy where an individual sets low expectations for their performance and experiences anxiety about a task, which paradoxically motivates them to prepare thoroughly to avoid failure, is BEST described as:","concept":"The answer is defensive pessimism, because setting low expectations and using the resulting anxiety as a spur to prepare thoroughly and avoid failure is exactly the cognitive strategy known as defensive pessimism. Understand the concept and how it contrasts with related strategies. Defensive pessimism was identified and studied by the psychologist Nancy Cantor and her colleague Julie Norem in the 1980s. It describes a strategy that some anxious but high-performing people use to manage their anxiety in achievement situations. Rather than trying to suppress their worry, defensive pessimists deliberately set unrealistically low expectations about how they will perform, expecting the worst, and then they engage in extensive mental rehearsal of everything that could go wrong. Crucially, this negative thinking is not paralyzing for them; it is motivating. By vividly imagining possible failures, they channel their anxiety into concrete preparation, planning for each contingency and working hard so that the feared bad outcome does not occur. Paradoxically, this pessimistic strategy leads to good performance, and importantly, if you force defensive pessimists to think positively or reassure them, their performance actually suffers, because you have removed the anxiety-management mechanism they depend on. Contrast this with strategic optimism, the opposite style where people set high expectations and stay calm, and with self-handicapping, where people sabotage their own preparation to have a ready excuse for failure. Defensive pessimism is adaptive and preparation-focused, whereas self-handicapping (Steven Berglas and Edward Jones, 1978) is self-defeating. The stem's combination of low expectations plus anxiety channeled into thorough preparation is the signature of defensive pessimism, a term from Norem and Cantor.","traps":{"a":"Strategic optimism is the opposite cognitive style, in which the person sets high expectations and feels calm and confident going into a task, rather than harnessing anxiety and low expectations to prepare.","b":"Depressive realism is the finding that mildly depressed people sometimes judge their control over outcomes more accurately than nondepressed people, a perceptual accuracy phenomenon, not a motivational preparation strategy.","d":"Self-handicapping is the reverse maneuver, in which a person creates obstacles or withholds effort so that failure can be blamed on the handicap rather than on ability, thereby protecting self-esteem, which is the opposite of thorough preparation."}},{"q":"The part of the brain responsible for producing enhanced memory for emotional or arousing events is:","concept":"The correct answer is the amygdala, the almond-shaped limbic structure that is critical for attaching emotional significance to events and for enhancing the consolidation of memories that occur during emotional arousal. The mechanism is well established: during emotionally charged or stressful experiences, the body releases stress hormones (adrenaline and cortisol) and the amygdala becomes highly active, and this amygdala activation modulates the hippocampus, which is the structure that actually forms new declarative memories. In effect the amygdala tells the hippocampus that an event matters and should be remembered strongly, producing the vivid, durable, often emotionally laden memories we associate with significant life events (sometimes called flashbulb memories). Joseph LeDoux's research mapped the amygdala's central role in fear processing and the fast subcortical pathway from thalamus to amygdala that allows rapid emotional appraisal. James McGaugh's work specifically demonstrated that the amygdala enhances memory consolidation for arousing material and that blocking adrenergic activity in the amygdala reduces this emotional memory advantage. The key distinction to learn is the division of labor in the limbic system: the hippocampus encodes and consolidates the factual content of memories, while the amygdala assigns emotional value and boosts the strength of memories formed under arousal. So when a question specifically links emotion or arousal to enhanced memory, the answer points to the amygdala rather than the hippocampus alone.","traps":{"a":"The hippocampus is essential for forming and consolidating new declarative (explicit) memories generally, but it is not the structure responsible for the specifically emotional enhancement of memory; that modulatory boost comes from the amygdala acting on the hippocampus.","b":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus is the master biological clock governing circadian rhythms, with no role in emotional memory enhancement.","d":"The hypothalamus regulates homeostatic drives (hunger, thirst, temperature, the stress hormone axis) and basic emotional expression, but it does not produce the enhanced encoding of emotional memories that the amygdala does."}},{"q":"According to Erik Erikson's psychosocial development theory, the virtue of \"purpose\" emerges from the successful resolution of which developmental crisis?","concept":"The answer is Initiative vs. Guilt because in Erik Erikson's eight-stage theory each psychosocial crisis, when resolved successfully, produces a specific 'virtue' or ego strength, and the virtue of purpose is the one that emerges from the Initiative vs. Guilt stage. This stage occurs during the preschool or play age, roughly ages three to six years. In it, the child becomes increasingly capable of initiating activities, making plans, asserting power over their environment through play, questioning, and taking on tasks and goals of their own devising. If caregivers encourage this budding initiative and allow the child to make choices and lead activities, the child develops a sense of initiative and gains the virtue of purpose, the confidence to pursue goals without excessive fear. If, instead, the child's efforts are constantly criticised, controlled, or made to feel like an intrusion, the child develops guilt about their needs and desires and becomes inhibited. The wider exam skill is memorising Erikson's stage-to-virtue pairings, since these are frequently tested: Trust vs. Mistrust yields hope, Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt yields will, Initiative vs. Guilt yields purpose, Industry vs. Inferiority yields competence, Identity vs. Role Confusion yields fidelity, Intimacy vs. Isolation yields love, Generativity vs. Stagnation yields care, and Integrity vs. Despair yields wisdom. Purpose maps to Initiative vs. Guilt.","traps":{"a":"Autonomy vs. Shame and Doubt yields the virtue of 'will', not 'purpose', so it does not match this question.","c":"Industry vs. Inferiority yields the virtue of 'competence', not 'purpose'.","d":"Identity vs. Role Confusion yields the virtue of 'fidelity', not 'purpose'."}},{"q":"In the context of psychopathology, a \"formulation\" differs from a \"diagnosis\" in that a formulation primarily aims to:","concept":"The answer is that a formulation provides a hypothesis about the cause, precipitants, and maintaining factors of an individual's difficulties, because that individualized explanatory function is precisely what separates formulation from diagnosis. Understand the distinction from first principles, since it sits at the heart of clinical psychology practice. A diagnosis is a category. It takes a person's presenting problems and matches them to a labeled disorder in a nosological system such as the DSM-5 or ICD-11; its strengths are shared language, communication, and access to treatment guidelines, but it is descriptive and generic, telling you what to call the problem, not why this person has it. A formulation is a personalized explanatory story. Drawing on a theoretical model, often the cognitive-behavioral framework associated with Aaron Beck or, in the UK tradition, the biopsychosocial and the five-Ps approach, the clinician hypothesizes how the difficulties arose and persist. Clinicians frequently organize it around predisposing factors (vulnerabilities such as temperament or early adversity), precipitating factors (the triggers or recent stressors), perpetuating or maintaining factors (the cycles like avoidance or safety behaviors that keep the problem alive), and protective factors (strengths and resources). Because a formulation is testable and revisable, it directly guides which interventions to choose and can be shared collaboratively with the client. Two people with the identical diagnosis will have quite different formulations. The exam signal is any wording about causes, triggers, or maintaining factors, which always points to formulation, not diagnosis.","traps":{"a":"Classifying symptoms according to a standardized system like DSM-5 is exactly what a diagnosis does, which is the very thing a formulation goes beyond.","c":"Determining prognosis and likely course is a downstream clinical judgment often informed by both diagnosis and formulation, but it is not the primary aim that distinguishes a formulation.","d":"Ruling out organic causes is a component of differential diagnosis and medical work-up, not the individualized explanatory purpose that defines a case formulation."}},{"q":"According to the DSM-5-TR, which of the following is NOT a specifier for a manic episode?","concept":"The answer is 'with limited prosocial emotions,' because that specifier belongs to Conduct Disorder, not to a manic episode. This is a cross-category recognition question, and the way to master it is to know where each specifier actually lives in the DSM-5. A specifier is an extension attached to a diagnosis to add clinically important detail. For mood episodes, including manic episodes in bipolar disorder, the DSM-5 offers a specific menu of specifiers: with anxious distress, with mixed features, with rapid cycling, with melancholic features, with atypical features, with mood-congruent or mood-incongruent psychotic features, with catatonia, with peripartum onset, and with seasonal pattern. Three of the four options here, mixed features, psychotic features, and anxious distress, sit squarely on that mood-episode menu. The odd one out, with limited prosocial emotions, comes from an entirely different part of the manual. It is the specifier for Conduct Disorder, used to identify the callous-unemotional presentation, marked by lack of remorse or guilt, shallow affect, callous lack of empathy, and unconcern about performance. This subgroup is theoretically important because it flags youths at higher risk for later psychopathy and is linked to research by Paul Frick and others on callous-unemotional traits. The exam trap is that all four options sound clinical and plausible, but only one is misfiled: limited prosocial emotions is a conduct-disorder specifier, so it is the correct answer to a question asking which is NOT a manic-episode specifier.","traps":{"a":"'With mixed features' is a legitimate specifier for a manic episode, applied when depressive symptoms coexist with mania, so it is not the exception.","b":"'With psychotic features' is a valid manic-episode specifier used when delusions or hallucinations accompany the mania, so it belongs to the set.","c":"'With anxious distress' is a recognized specifier across mood episodes including manic episodes, indicating co-occurring anxiety, so it too is a real specifier."}},{"q":"In the analysis of variance the significance level indicates","concept":"The correct answer is the probability of making a Type I error. In hypothesis testing, including the analysis of variance, the significance level, denoted alpha, is the threshold probability that the researcher sets in advance (commonly 0.05) for rejecting the null hypothesis. It represents the probability of making a Type I error, which is rejecting a true null hypothesis, in other words concluding that there is a real effect or group difference when in fact none exists (a false positive). This framework of controlling error rates was developed by Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, who formalised the distinction between two kinds of error. A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: wrongly rejecting a true null hypothesis. A Type II error (beta) is a false negative: failing to reject a false null hypothesis, that is, missing a real effect. The power of a test equals 1 minus beta. Setting alpha at 0.05 means the researcher accepts a 5 percent risk of a Type I error. So in ANOVA, when we test the F ratio against a critical value at a chosen significance level, that level tells us the probability of a Type I error we are willing to tolerate. Option C confuses alpha with beta, and option B is not a coherent statistical statement. This item tests understanding of the significance level as the Type I error rate.","traps":{"b":"'That F is correct' is not a meaningful statistical statement; the significance level is a probability threshold, not a verification that the F statistic is correct.","c":"The probability of a Type II error is beta, not the significance level; the significance level (alpha) governs Type I errors, so this confuses the two error types.","d":"Since option B is meaningless and only A is correct, combining A and B is wrong."}}]});
